===Etymology===
From {{affix|la|ab-|gloss1=from, away from|*nuō|gloss2=nod}}, literally “to reject by a nod”.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|abnuō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|abnuō|abnu|abnuit}}

# {{lb|la|literally|rare|often in conjuction with {{m|la|adnuō}}}} to say no, to [[nod]] in [[negation]]
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Truculentus|prol|4|thru=6|quote=Quid nunc? Datūrīn estis an nōn? Annuont.<br>{{...}}<br>Quid sī dē vostrō quippiam ōrem? '''Abnuont'''.|t=Well then? Are you going to give it or not? They nod in approval.<br>{{...}}<br>What if I asked for something of yours? They '''nod in disapproval'''.}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Tristia|5|10|41|through=42|quote=Utque fit, in sē aliquid fingī, dīcentibus illīs<br>     '''abnuerim''' quotiēns adnuerimque, putant.|t=Either way, they think I'm inventing something about them, whenever they speak<br>     and I '''nod in negation''' and affirmation.}}
# to [[refuse]], [[reject]]
#: {{syn|la|recūsō|negō|renuō}}
#: {{ant|la|adnuō|aiō}}
#* {{Q|la|Sallust|Bellum Iugurthinum|47|quote={{...}}rēgī pācem quam postulābat neque '''abnuere''' neque pollicērī{{...}}|t={{...}}he neither refused nor promised the king the peace he was asking for{{...}}}}
#* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab Urbe Condita|10|10|8|quote=Dē societāte haud '''abnuunt''' barbarī: dē mercēde agitur.|t=The barbarians '''refuse''' not an alliance: it's all about remuneration.}}
#* {{Q|la|Statius|Achilleid|1|917|quote=Nec tamen '''abnuerit''' generō sē iungere tālī.|t=Nor will he '''refuse''' to join with such a son in law.}}
<!--#* {{Q|la|Tacitus|Histories|4|44|quote=Octāvius Pontiam Postumīnam, stuprō cognitam et nūptiās suās '''abnuentem''', impotēns amōris interfēcerat, Sosiānus prāvitāte mōrum multīs exitiōsus.|t=Octavius, in impotent jealousy, murdered Pontia Postumina, whom he'd had an affair with and who '''refused''' to marry him, while Sosianus in his depravity proved ruinous to many.}}-->
#* {{Q|la|Tacitus|Annals|13|14|quote=Nōn '''abnuere''' se, quīn cūncta īnfēlīcīs domūs mala patefīerent, suae in prīmīs nūptiae, suum venēficium.|t=She didn't '''reject''' that all the evils of the unhappy house be made known, her marriage first of all, and her poisoning.}}
#* '''248 {{CE}} – 258 {{CE}}''', [[w:Cyprian|Cyprian]], ''Letters'' [https://archive.org/details/corpusscriptoru16wissgoog/page/724/mode/2up 65.3] in ''[[w:Corpus Scriptorum Ecclesiasticorum|Corpus Vindobonense]]'' (volume III, part 2), Franz Pauly, [[w:Vienna|Vienna]] (1871), page 724, lines 3–4:
#*: Nec mīrum sī cōnsilia nostra aut Dominī praecepta nunc '''abnuunt''' quī Dominum negāvērunt.
#*:: No wonder that those who denied the Lord now '''reject''' our decisions or the Lord's teachings.
## {{lb|la|poetic|or|post-classical|mostly|of|inanimate subjects}} to not [[admit]] of
## {{lb|la|military|very rare}} to [[decline]] service
##* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab Urbe Condita|27|49|3|quote=Ille fessōs '''abnuentēs'''que taediō et labōre nunc precandō, nunc castigandō accendit.|t=It was he who, now by entreaties, now by chastising, stimulated the soldiers, tired and '''declining service''' due to weariness and difficulty.}}
# to [[deny]]
#* {{Q|la|Lucretius|De rerum natura|3|640|thru=641|quote=At quod scinditur et partīs discēdit in ūllās,<br>scīlicet aeternam sibi nātūram '''abnuit''' esse.|t=But if something breaks and separates into any parts,<br>it surely '''denies''' having an eternal nature.}}
#* {{Q|la|Pliny the Elder|Naturalis Historia|10|70|137|quote=Quī crēdat ista, et Melampodī profectō augurī aurēs lambendō dedisse intellēctum avium sermōnis dracōnes nōn '''abnuat'''.|t=Anyone who would believe these would also surely not '''deny''' that serpents gave Melampus the augur the understanding of bird language by means of licking his ears.}}
#* {{Q|la|Apuleius|Metamorphoses|2|27|quote=Ēmeditātīs ad haec illa flētibus quamque sānctissimē poterat adiūrāns cūncta nūmina, tantum scelus '''abnuēbat'''.|t=With cries devised just for this, swearing by all the gods as solemnly as she could, she '''denied''' having committed such a crime.}}
#* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 310 {{CE}}''', [[w:Lactantius|Lactantius]], ''Divinarum Institutionum Epitome'' [https://archive.org/details/CorpusScriptorumEcclesiasticorumLatinorum19/page/n944/mode/2up 31 (36)] in ''[[w:Corpus Scriptorum Ecclesiasticorum Latinorum|Corpus Vindobonense]]'' (volume IXX), Samuel Brandt, [[w:Vienna|Vienna]] (1890), page 706, lines 10–12:
#*: Epicūrī doctrīna haec est inprīmīs, nullam esse prōvidentiam, et īdem deōs esse nōn '''abnuit''': utrumque contrā ratiōnem.
#*:: Chiefly, the doctrine of Epicurus is that there is no Providence, and he also '''denies''' not the existence of gods: both go against reason.

====Usage notes====
The figurative meaning has almost completely overtaken the literal one.

The word is a favourite of [[w:Livy|Livy]] and [[w:Tacitus|Tacitus]]. Older authors prefer the negative formula “''haud abnuō''”, while authors after the time of Tacitus use “''nōn abnuō''”.

