Page 262 adipiscor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{affix|la|ad-|gloss1=to, towards, at|apīscor|gloss2=reach after something, pursue; reach}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|adipīscor}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|adipīscor|adept}}

# to [[arrive]] at, [[reach]]; [[overtake]], [[catch]] up with
#: {{syn|la|possideō|potior|obsideō|apprehendō|cōnsequor|exōrō|impetrō|mereō}}
# to [[get]], [[obtain]], [[acquire]], get [[possession]] of, [[achieve]]
#: {{syn|la|acquīrō|cōnsequor|parō|pariō|impetrō|mereō|sūmō|emō|potior|inveniō|comparō|apīscor|obtineō|occupō|conciliō|nancīscōr|colligō|alliciō}}
#: {{ant|la|āmittō}}

====Usage notes====
* Since this verb is deponent, its past participle {{m|la|adeptus}} usually has an '''active''' sense ("having obtained"). But along with a few others (e.g. {{m|la|comitātus}} from {{m|la|comitor}}, {{m|la|complexus}} from {{m|la|complector}}) it can sometimes have a '''passive''' sense too ("obtained"), as in ''adeptā libertāte'' found in Sallustius.
* {{m|la||adipiscing}} (not a real Latin word) is a {{m|en|lorem ipsum}}.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|adipīscor|adept}}

