===Etymology 1===
From {{affix|la|aeternus|-ō|id2=denominative}}.

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1+|aeternō}}

# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[exist]] over a period of time or [[indefinitely]]: to [[abide]], to [[continue]], to [[last]], to [[perdure]], to [[persist]]
#: {{syn|la|maneō|permaneō|persevērō|persistō|remaneō}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to cause to [[last]]: to [[continue]], to [[perpetuate]]
#: {{syn|la|continuō|perpetuō}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to make [[everlasting]]: to [[eternalize]], to [[immortalize]]
# {{lb|la|transitive|figuratively}} to confer [[fame]] upon: to render [[famous]] or [[illustrious]], to make [[renowned]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|1+|aeternō}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{affix|la|aeternus|-ō|id2=adverb}}.

====Adverb====
{{la-adv|aeternō|-}}

# [[constantly]], [[continuously]], [[perpetually]]
#: {{syn|la|iūge|iūgiter|perpetuō|continenter|prōtinus}}
# [[lastingly]], [[perduringly]], [[persistently]]
#: {{syn|la|iūge|iūgiter}}
# [[always]], [[ever]]
#: {{syn|la|semper}}
# [[forever]], [[endlessly]], [[eternally]], [[everlastingly]], [[evermore]]; {{sense|of [[animate]] things}} [[immortally]]
#: {{syn|la|aeternāliter}}

=====Usage notes=====
As an adverb, ''aeternō'' [[comprise]]s the meanings of {{m|la|aeternus}}, {{m|la|aeternālis}}, and {{m|la|aeternātus}} + {{m|la|-ō}}; compare the narrower semantic scope of {{l|la|aeternāliter}}.

