Page 527 ago: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*agō}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*h₂éǵeti}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sga|aigid}}, {{cog|grc|ἄγω|t=I lead}}, {{cog|non|aka|t=move, drive}}, {{cog|ae|𐬀𐬰𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌}}, {{cog|sa|अजति|tr=ájati|t=to drive, propel, cast}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|agō}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-ago.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|agō|ēg|āct}}

# to [[act]], to [[behave]]
#* {{RQ:Vulgate|Paralipomenon II|32|7|passage=viriliter '''agite''' et confortamini nolite timere nec paveatis regem Assyriorum {{...}}|translation="'''Act''' strongly and be courageous. Do not fear nor tremble before the king of Assyria"}}
#* {{Q|la|{{w|Seneca the Younger|Lucius Annaeus Seneca}}|{{w|Epistulae Morales ad Lucilium}}|Epistula 94, 6.1|year=64 CE|quote=...doces illum quid sano faciendum sit, non efficis sanum. ''Pauperi ut '''agat''' divitem monstras'': hoc quomodo manente paupertate fieri potest?|trans=...you teach a man what a healthy man should do, but you don't make him healthy. ''You show the poor how '''to behave''' as a rich man'': but if they remain poor, how can they do so?}}
# to [[do]]
#* "'''Agere'''...does not express, as facere does, the principle, author, ''nor a single act of producing; but a series of cares and a continued activity.''" - ''Latin Synonyms, with Their Different Significations, etc.'' by M. J. B. Gardin Dumesnil, Trans. Gosset, London, 1819.
#* "'''Agere''', Facere et Gerere hoc differunt, quod '''agere''' et corporis, et vocis, et mentis agitatum comprehendit. Facere tantum refertur ad opera, quae corpore efficimus; aliquando et pro consentire ponitur. His enim loquendi modis utebantur recte antiqui: mecum seu tecum ''faciam'', hoc est, mecum seu tecum consentiam. Gerere est muneris et oneris..." - ''Ausonii Popmae frisii de differentiis verborum cum additamentis'' ab Hekelii, Richteri, Messerschmidii et Vallaurii, 1865.
#* '''''c.'' 200 [[Appendix:Glossary#BCE|BCE]],''' [[:w:en:Plautus|Plautus]] ''[[:w:en:Amphitryon (Plautus play)|Amphitryon]]'' 2.1.1
#*:  ''(AMPHITRYON to SOSIA):'' '''age''' ī tū secundum,
#*:: ''Direct/literal translation:'' "'''Do''' thou walk after/following (me)!" ''Functional/colloquial translation:'' "Come, '''do''' follow after me!"
#* {{RQ:Cicero Catiline}}
#*: {{quote|la|Nihil '''agis''', nihil moliris, nihil cogitas quod non ego non modo audiam sed etiam videam planeque sentiam.|"'''You do''' nothing, you plan nothing, you think of nothing which I not only do not hear, but which I do not see and know every particular of."}}
# to [[make]] {{q|something that does not continue to exist after the maker stops}}
# to [[negotiate]], [[discuss]], [[confer]], [[talk]] with one about a person or thing
# to [[effect]], [[accomplish]], [[achieve]]
# to [[treat]], to [[deal]]
# to [[act]], [[play]], [[perform]] {{gloss|e.g., a role in a play}}
# to [[perform]], [[transact]], [[conduct]], [[manage]] {{gloss|e.g. business, affairs}}
# to [[administer]], [[direct]], [[guide]], [[govern]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|574|quote=“Trōs Tyriusque mihī nūllō discrīmine '''agētur'''.”|trans=''“Trojan and Tyrian shall be governed [alike], with no difference to me.” – Queen Dido''}}
# to [[drive]] (sense of providing an impetus for motion), [[impel]], [[move]], [[push]], [[put]] in [[motion]]
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Titus Livius|Titus Livius]]|[[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]]|I, 12|quote=Mettius Curtius [...] {{l|la|ab}} {{l|la|arce}} {{l|la|decurrerat|decucurrerat}} {{l|la|et}} {{l|la|effusos}} '''egerat''' {{l|la|Romanos}}|t= Mettius Curtius [..] had run down from the citadel, and '''driven''' the Romans in disorder}}
# to [[conduct]], [[drive]] (sense of providing governance to motion)
#* '''1877''', Sophocles (in translation), ''Electra'', in ''Aeschyli et Sophoclis: Tragoediae et Fragmenta'' (Paris: Institutiae Franciae Typographo)
#*: {{quote|la|Intereā Orestēs postrēmus omnium ultimō locō equōs '''agēbat''', in fīne certam spem victōriae ponēns.
|translation="Meanwhile, Orestes '''had been driving''' in last place and holding his horses back, putting his trust in the finish."}}
# to [[discuss]], [[debate]], [[deliberate]] {{q|used in civil, political and legal contexts}}
# {{lb|la|law}} to [[plead]]
# to [[think]] upon; to be [[occupied]] with
#* {{Q|la|{{w|Ovid|Publius Ovidius Naso}}|{{w|Heroides}}|Carmen IIX, Lines 208-212 (ultimate 5 lines)|year=8 CE|quote=ingentis parturit ira minas. / Quo feret ira, sequar! facti fortasse pigebit — / Et piget infido consuluisse viro. / Viderit ista deus, qui nunc mea pectora versat! / Nescio quid certe ''mens mea maius '''agit'''!''|trans=Tremndous anger abounds with threat. / I’ll follow where anger carries me. Perhaps I’ll regret my deeds: / I regret having given thought to the interests of an unfaithful husband. / Let the god see to that, which churns within my heart. / Assuredly, I don't know what '''''occupies''' my mind more greatly!''}}
# to [[aim]] at, to [[get]] at {{gloss|generally in the subjunctive mood and preceded by {{m|la|ut}}, and so meaning: "that to might achieve..."}}
# to [[stir up]], [[excite]], [[cause]], [[induce]]
# to [[disturb]], [[agitate]], [[vex]]
# to [[lead]], [[drive]] {{gloss|e.g., livestock}}
# to [[chase]], [[pursue]]
# to [[drive at]], [[pursue]] (a course of action)
# to [[rob]], [[steal]], [[plunder]], [[carry off]]
# {{lb|la|of time}} to [[pass]], [[spend]], [[lead]]
# {{lb|la|of offerings}} to [[slay]], [[kill]] (as a sacrifice)
# {{lb|la|of plants}} to [[put forth]], [[sprout]], [[extend]]
# {{lb|la|law}} to [[hold]] {{gloss|a court}}
# {{lb|la|passive}} to [[go on]], to [[take place]], to be at [[issue]]

====Usage notes====
''Ago'' renders a sense of doing or making which is [[continuative]] or [[behavioral]]. For a sense of a specific [[instance]] or [[occasion]] of doing or making, see {{l|la|facio}}. For a sense of doing or making which is yet more [[continuative]], see {{l|la|agito}} and {{l|la|gero}}.

According to Döderlein, another difference between ''ago'' and ''facio'' when they mean "make" is that ''ago'' typically has to do with making something that does not continue after the "actor" stops doing the action; whereas with ''facio'', the object continues to exist after the maker has made the thing. In other words, ''ago'' is temporal, whereas ''facio'' is spatial.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|agō|ēg|āct}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|accomplish}} {{l|la|perficio}}, {{l|la|conficio}}, {{l|la|fungor}}, {{l|la|defungor}}, {{l|la|efficiō}}, {{l|la|perfero}}, {{l|la|absolvo}}, {{l|la|expleo}}, {{l|la|exsequor}}, {{l|la|gero}}, {{l|la|nāvō}}, {{l|la|perpetrō}}, {{l|la|conclūdō}}, {{l|la|condō}}, {{l|la|peragō}}, {{l|la|inclūdō}}, {{l|la|claudō}}, {{l|la|cumulō}}, {{l|la|prōflīgō}}, {{l|la|trānsigō}}, {{l|la|impleō}}, {{l|la|exhauriō}}
* {{sense|conduct, drive}} {{l|la|duco}}, {{l|la|deduco}}, {{l|la|traduco}}, {{l|la|veho}}, {{l|la|portō}}, {{l|la|produco}}
* {{sense|stir}} {{l|la|percieō}}, {{l|la|concieō}}, {{l|la|cieō}}, {{l|la|molior}}, {{l|la|perpello}}
