Page 551 allegorizo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Late Latin corruption of {{cog|grc|ἀλληγορέω||I speak allegorically}}, probably by analogy with other borrowings in {{m|grc|-ίζω}} largely in Early Christian texts. Thence from {{m|grc|ἄλλος||other}} + {{m|grc|ἀγορεύω||I speak in the assembly, harangue}}, from {{m|grc|ἀγορά||assembly}}, from {{m|grc|ἀγείρω||I gather, collect}} from {{cog|ine-pro|*h₂ger-||to assemble; collect; gather}}, cognate with {{cog|la|grex||flock}} and {{cog|en|cram}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|allēgorizō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1.nopass|allēgorizō|allēgorizāv}}

# {{lb|la|Late Latin}} {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[speak]] in [[allegories]], [[allegorise]] or allegorize
#* '''208''', Tertullianus, ''Adversus Marcionem'', IV.xvii.12:
#*: Multo enim haec congruentius in ipsos interpretabimur, quae Christus in homines '''allegorizavit''', non in duos deos, secundum scandalum Marcionis.
#*:: For in applying to these heretics the figurative words which Christ used of men in general, we shall make a much more suitable interpretation of them than if we were to deduce out of them two gods, according to Marcion's grievous exposition. ([https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/03124.htm Source of translation])

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1.nopass|allēgorizō|allēgorizāv}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|it|allegorizzare}}
* {{desc|es|alegorizar}}
* {{desc|pl|alegoryzować|lbor=1}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
-------- end text --------
