Page 594 amo: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|amō}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-amo.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*amāō}}, of disputed etymology. Possibly from {{der|la|ine-pro|*am-a-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*am-||mother, aunt}}, a lost {{onomatopoeic|la|title=nursery-word}} of the {{m|la|papa}}-type. Compare {{m|la|amita||aunt}}, {{cog|goh|amma||nurse}}.

Alternatively, Olav Hackstein and Michiel De Vaan suggest a derivation from {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₃emh₃-||to seize, to take hold}} via {{inh|la|itc-pro|*amāō||to take hold}}, applying a semantic shift “to take by the hand” > “to regard as a friend” > “to love, to be fond of”.

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1+.poet-sync-perf|amō}}

# to [[love]]
#: {{syn|la|dīligō}}
#: {{ant|la|exsecror|abhorreō|abōminor|dēspuō|odiō}}
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Mostellaria|quote=Tū mē '''amās''', egō tē '''amō'''; meritō id fierī uterque exīstumat.|trans=You '''love''' me, I '''love''' you; and each of us believes it to be well-deserved.}}
#* {{Q|la|Seneca the Younger|{{w|Letters to Lucilius}}|IX|6|quote=Sī vīs '''amārī''', '''amā'''.|trans='''Love''' if you wish '''to be loved'''.}}
# to be [[fond]] of, [[like]], [[admire]]
#: {{syn|la|dīligō|probō|approbō|comprobō}}
#: {{ant|la|improbō|reprobō}}
# to be [[please|pleased]] by or with (someone or something) for (a particular reason); to [[derive]] [[pleasure]] from...(for...), [[delight]] in...(for...)
#: {{syn|la|dēlector}}
#* {{Q|la|Horace|{{w|Carmen Saeculare}}|year=17 BCE|quote=...hīc magnōs potius triumphōs, hīc '''amēs''' dīcī pater atque prī̆nceps, neu sinās Mēdōs equitāre inultōs // tē duce, Caesar.|trans=...rather, '''may you delight''' in these great triumphs, to be called father and the first man (of state), and may you not allow the Medes to ride unpunished while you lead, Caesar.}}
# {{lb|la|with|[[sē]]}} to be pleased (with oneself), to be [[content]]
# {{lb|la|with infinitive}} to be [[accustom|accustomed]] (to), [[enjoy]] an [[activity]]
#: {{syn|la|assuefio|fruor}}
# to be [[thankful]], [[grateful]] to, feel [[oblige|obliged]] for a [[service]]
#* {{Q|la|Terence|{{w|Eunuchus|The Eunuch}}|quote=Ō Thāis mea, meum sāvium, quid agitur? Ecquid nōs '''amās''' dē fīdīcinā istāc?|trans=O Thais, my sweetie, what's happening? Are you '''grateful''' to us for that harpist?}}
#* {{Q|la|Terence|{{w|Adelphoe|The Brothers}}|year=~160 BCE|quote=Bene facis, meritō tē amō.|trans=You're very kind, '''I'm''' rightly '''obliged''' to you.}}
#* {{Q|la|Cicero|{{w|Epistulae ad Atticum}}|CXXIII|year=68-44 BCE|quote=Dē raudusculō Numeriānō multum tē '''amō'''.|trans=Regarding Numerius' bit of coin '''I am''' very much '''obliged''' to you.|notes=The phrase ''raudusculo Numeriano'', "Numerius' bit of coin", here refers to a small monetary debt assumedly having been owed by Cicero to Numerius, and paid for Cicero by Atticus}}

=====Usage notes=====
The ancient Romans were accustomed to saying "I shall/will love you!" ("[[te]] [[amabo]]"/"[[amabo te]]") in supplication, and "I love you!" ("[[te]] [[amo]]") when they were expressing gratitude. Latin "''amare''" has a broader semantic range than English "to love", and so can be a semantically "weaker" or, perhaps, less intense verb. ''Amare'' was therefore appropriate for speech etiquette in situations of supplication or the expression of gratitude. Because of the semantic differences between the Latin and English verbs, and especially of the narrower semantic range of English "to love", a literal translation into English will in such cases (involving supplication or gratitude) inevitably appear strange. Accordingly, translators have ever resorted to expressions like "appreciate", "be thankful" and "be obliged" as a workaround, but in such cases the Romans actually meant "love" as they construed that emotion.

