Page 663 apo: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|apiō}}

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*h₂ep-|id=join}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*apō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₂ep-||to get, grab; to join|id=join}}. Cognate with {{m|la|apex}}, {{cog|hit|𒄩𒀊||tr=ḫapp-|to join, attach}}, {{cog|grc|ἅπτω||I fasten}}. <!--{{nc|ae|𐬁𐬟𐬆𐬥𐬙𐬈}}, {{nc|ae|𐬀𐬞𐬀𐬫𐬈𐬌𐬙𐬌}}-->

The term is only attested in another form than the participle in the work of the grammarian {{w|Sextus Pompeius Festus}} and in the [[w:Etymologiae|Etymologiae]] of [[w:Saint Isidore of Seville|Saint Isidore of Seville]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|eccl=yes|apō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|apō|ap|apt}}

# to [[fasten]]; [[attach]], [[connect]]; [[join]], [[bind]]
#* {{Q|la|Aulus Gellius|Noctes Atticae|1|15|1|quote={{...}}linguam autem dēbēre aiunt non esse līberam nec vagam, sed vinclīs de pectore īmō ac dē corde '''aptīs''' movērī et quasi gubernārī.|trans=They say that the tongue should not be free and wandering, but that it should be moved and, so to say, steered by cords '''attached''' to the deep chest and heart.}}
#* {{RQ:Paul.Fest.|17|9
|passage=Apex, quod est sacerdotum īnsigne, dictus est ab eō, quod comprehendere antīquī vinculō '''apere''' dīcēbant. Unde '''aptus''' est, quī conventienter alicui iūnctus est.
|translation=The ''apex'', which is the ensign of the [[w:Flamen|Flamen]], is called so because of the fact that in, the old language, tying with a rope was called '''''apere'''''. Whence '''''aptus''''' is something which is conventiently joined to something.}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|apō|ap|apt}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|apīscor}}
* {{l|la|aptō}}
* {{l|la|aptus}}
* {{l|la|coepiō}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|la|apex}}
* {{l|la|cōpula}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
* {{R:ine:Kloekhorst2008}}
* {{R:grc:Beekes|volume=1|page=120}}
* {{R:itc:EDL|head=apīscor|page=47}}
* {{R:Sihler 1995}}

