Page 725 ardeo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*h₂eHs-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*āzidēō}}, equivalent to {{affix|la|āridus|alt1=ār(i)dus|-eō}}. Cf. {{m|la|audeō}} < {{m|la|avidus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|ārdeō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2|ārdeō|ārs|ārs}}

# to [[burn]] (to be consumed by fire)
#: {{syn|la|[[uro|ūror]]|cōnflagrō|flagrō|īnflammō|flammō|incendō|accendō|cremō|adoleō}}
#* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab Urbe Condita|22|1|8|thru=9|quote=Augēbant metum prōdigia ex plūribus simul locīs nūntiāta:{{...}} et Praeneste '''ārdentēs''' lapidēs caelō cecidisse,{{...}}|trans=Prodigies from various places announced at the same time increased fear:{{...}} and at Praeneste '''burning''' rocks fell from the sky,{{...}}}}
#* {{Q|la|Jerome|Vulgate|Exodus|3|2|quote=Apparuitque eī Dominus in flammā ignis dē mediō rubī et vidēbat quod rubus '''ārdēret''' et nōn conbūrerētur.|trans=The Lord appeared to him in a flame of fire from the middle of a bramble bush, and he saw that the bush '''was burning''' and not being consumed.}}
# {{lb|la|of eyes}} to [[glow]]
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Captivi|3|4|62|quote='''Ārdent''' oculī.|trans=His eyes are '''glowing'''.}}
# {{lb|la|poetic}} to [[glisten]] with a feature, usually with a [[colour]]
#* {{Q|la|Vergil|Aeneid|4|259|thru=264|quote=Ut prīmum ālātīs tetigit māgālia plantīs,<br>Aenēān fundantem arcēs ac tēcta novantem<br>cōnspicit. Atque illī stēllātus iaspide fulvā<br>ēnsis erat, Tyriōque '''ārdēbat''' mūrice laena<br>dēmissa ex umerīs, dīvēs quae mūnera Dīdō<br>fēcerat, et tenuī tēlās discrēverat aurō.|trans=As soon as he reached the huts with winged heels,<br>Aeneas founding towers and making new houses<br>he watched. And his sword was<br>starred with yellow jasper, and a cloak '''glistened''' with Tyrian purple,<br>hung from his shoulder, all gifts which the rich Dido<br>had made, and had interwoven the web with thread of gold.}}
#* {{Q|la|Vergil|Aeneid|11|601|thru=602|quote={{...}}Tum lātē ferreus hastīs<br>horret ager campīque armīs sublīmibus '''ārdent'''.|trans=Then, the iron plain<br>shivers far and wide with iron, and the fields '''glisten''' with raised weapons.}}
# {{lb|la|figurative}} to [[burn]], be strongly affected with an [[emotion]] (not always specified; when yes, most often in the [[ablative]], but also with ''ad'' or ''in'')
#* {{Q|la|Terence|Adelphoe|3|2|12|quote=Mē miserum! vix sum compos animī, ita '''ārdeō''' īrācundiā.|trans=Woe is me! I hardly am in control of my soul, so much I '''burn''' with anger.}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|12|1|thru=4|quote=Turnus ut īnfrāctōs adversō Mārte Latīnōs<br>dēfēcisse videt, sua nunc prōmissa reposcī,<br>sē sīgnārī oculīs, ultrō implācābilis '''ārdet'''<br>attollitque animōs.|trans=As Turnus sees the Latins crushed with Mars turned against them<br>having gotten disheartened, his pledge called back,<br>and himself the mark of every eye, '''burns''' moreover implacable<br>and raises his courage.}}
# {{lb|la|figurative}} to be [[eager]]
# {{lb|la|figurative}} to be [[ardently]], [[fervently]], or [[perfervidly]] [[in love]], to [[burn]] with (especially [[romantic]]) [[desire]]
#: {{syn|la|adamō}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|3|545-546|quote='''ārserat''' Aenēae Dīdō miserābilis igne,<br/>ārserat exstructīs in sua fāta rogīs|trans=''Wretched Dido, '''she had been burned''' with her fire for Aeneas;<br/>she had been burned on a funeral pyre built for her own death.''<br/>(In a mere two lines, Ovid vividly summarizes the tragedy of {{w|Dido}} by contrasting two different meanings for the word ''ārserat.'')}}

