Page 908 baubor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{onomatopoeic|la}}. It could be inherited from a {{der|la|ine-pro|*bau-||to bark, to bay}}, if {{cog|grc|βαὺ}} and {{cog|lt|baũbti||to shout, to roar}} are viewed as cognates<ref>{{R:ine:IEW|pages=94-95|volume=I|head=bau-}}</ref>. However, Olander (2020) considers it more likely that these were independent onomatopoeic formations, not genuine cognates.<ref>{{cite-journal|title=To *''b'' or not to *''b'' Proto-Indo-European *''b'' in a phylogenetic perspective|author=Thomas Olander|year=2020|journal=Historische Sprachforschung / Historical Linguistics|volume=133|page=189|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/27188462}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|baubor}}

# {{lb|la|of dogs}} to [[bark]], [[bay]], [[howl]] (moderately)

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+|baubor}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|bark}} {{l|la|lātrō}}

===References===
<references/>

===Further reading===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}

[[Category:la:Animal sounds]]
