===Etymology===
From {{m|la|bēta||beet}}, from Augustus, quoted by Suetonius.

<small>''Ponit assidue et pro stulto "baceolum" et pro pullo "pulleiaceum" et pro cerrito "vacerrosum" et "vapide" se habere pro male et "'''betizare'''" pro languere, quod vulgo "lachanizare" dicitur''  <br> -- [[Suetonius]] ''De Vita Caesarum Liber VIII Divus Augustus'' 87.2</small>

<small>He also habitually used "baceolus"(dolt) for "stultus"(fool), "pulleiaceus"(darkish) for "pullus"(dark), "vacerrosus"(blockhead) for "cerritus"(mad); also "vapide se habere"(to feel flat) for "male se habere"(to feel bad) and '''"betizare"'''(to be like a beet) for "languere"(to be weak) which is "lanchanizare" in vulgar terms</small>

===Noun===
{{la-verb|1+|bētīzō}}

# To be as a beet; to be tired

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+|bētīzō}}
