Page 954 cado: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*ḱh₂d-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*kadō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*ḱh₂d-||to fall}}.

Compare {{cog|xcl|ցածնում}}, {{cog|sga|casar||hail, lightning}}, {{cog|cy|cesair||hail}}, {{cog|br|kazerc'h}}, {{cog|kw|keser}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|cadō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3.pass-impers|cadō|cecid|cās}}

# to [[fall]], to [[fall out]]
#: {{syn|la|corruō|accidō|incidō|incurrō|occidō|ruō}}
#: {{ant|la|orior}}
# to [[fall]] in [[battle]], [[die]], be [[slain]], fall so as to be unable to rise
#: {{syn|la|concidō}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|2|212-213|quote=sīc iterum, sīc saepe '''cadunt'''|trans=''Again in this way, and thus so often, '''they fall''' [in the battle].''}}
# to be [[frustrated]], [[fail]], to be or [[remain]] [[fruitless]]
#: {{syn|la|discēdō|dēficiō|dēsum|dēlinquō}}
# to [[lose]] all one's [[strength]], [[worth]], [[value]], [[perish]], [[vanish]], [[cease]], [[decay]]
#: {{syn|la|concidō|senēscō|ēlanguēscō|languēscō|dēgenerō|dēsīdō|prōlābor}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Titus Livius|Titus Livius]]|[[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]]|I, 11|quote={{l|la|quod}} {{l|la|alienis}} {{l|la|cladibus}} '''ceciderant''' {{l|la|animi}}|t=''Because their spirit had been '''decayed''' by the defeats of the others''}}
# to [[abate]], to [[subside]], to [[die away]]
#: {{syn|la|morior|pereō|occumbō|dēfungor|exspīrō|dēcēdō|occidō|discēdō|intereō|dēficiō}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|154|quote=sīc cūnctus pelagī '''cecidit''' fragor |trans=''In this way all the clamor of the sea '''subsided'''''}}
# to [[suit]], to be [[suitable]] for {{gloss|+ {{m|la|in}} + ''accusative''}}
# to [[fall under]], to [[come under]] {{gloss|+ {{m|la|in}} or {{m|la|sub}} + ''accusative''}}
# {{lb|la|impersonal}} to [[happen]], [[occur]], [[befall]], [[fall]] upon, [[fall]] to, [[turn out]] [[come to pass]]
#: {{syn|la|incidō|ēveniō|interveniō|expetō|incurrō|ēvadō|accēdō|accidō}}

