===Etymology 1===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*keh₂n-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*kanō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*keh₂n-|t=to sing}}. Compare {{m|la|carmen|t=song}} (< {{m|itc-pro|*kanmen}}).

Cognates include {{cog|sga|canaid}}, {{cog|cy|canu}}, {{cog|got|𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌰|t=cock}}, {{cog|grc|καναχέω|t=ring, clash, clang}}, {{cog|ru|канюк|t=buzzard}}, {{cog|en|hen}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA|canō}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|3|canō|cecin|cant}}

# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[sing]], [[recite]], [[play]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|1|quote=Arma virumque '''cano''' {{...}}|trans=I '''sing''' of weapons and a man {{...}}}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[sound]], [[play]], [[blow]] (a trumpet), especially a military call
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[foretell]], [[predict]], [[prophesy]]
#: {{syn|la|praesāgiō|praemoneō|portendō|moneō|praedīcō|vāticinor}}
# {{lb|la|transitive|Medieval Latin}} to [[celebrate]] [[Mass]]
# {{lb|la|transitive|or|intransitive|Medieval Latin}} to [[pretend]] (that)
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[sing]], make [[music]]
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[chant]]
# {{lb|la|intransitive|of owls}} to [[hoot]]
# {{lb|la|intransitive|of a musical instrument}} to [[sound]], [[resound]], [[play]]
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[sound]], [[play]]

