Page 1000 careo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*kazēō}}, possibly from {{der|la|ine-pro|*ḱes-||to cut}}.

De Vaan rejects the etymology from {{m|ine-pro||*ḱes-}}, finding it too semantically difficult to reconcile the [[stative]] ending -ēre, 'to lack, be lacking', and a stative Indo-European 'to be cutting something'. He mentions a 2000 paper by George Dunkel linking the derived {{m|la|castīgō}} to Sanskrit {{m|sa|शास्ति||to teach, correct}}, but rejects this too as other words in the word family seem to stem from 'be lacking, pure'.

Noteworthy is the same development in the Portuguese use of {{m|pt|precisar}} (in passive meaning equivalent to {{m|pt|carecer}}, from this ''careō''), derived from {{m|pt|preciso}}, from Latin {{m|la|praecīsus||cut, shortened}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|careō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2+|careō}}

# to [[lack]], be [[without]]. (usually with [[Appendix:Glossary#ablative case|ablative]]), to be [[deprive]]d of
#: {{syn|la|dēsum|egeō|indigeō|dēlinquō|deficiō|cessō|perdō}}
#: {{ant|la|flōreō|niteō|abundō|affluō}}
#: {{usex|la|Sī versūs hōrum duōrum poētārum neglegētis, magnā parte litterārum '''carēbitis'''.|If you neglect the verses of these two poets, '''you will miss''' a great part of literature.}}
#: {{usex|la|Imperātor mīlitibus proeliō '''caruit'''.|The general '''was without''' soldiers for the battle.}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Caesar|Caesar]]|[[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]]|VII, 17|quote=Usque eo ut complures dies frumento milites '''caruerint'''|t=to the degree that the soldiers '''were without''' corn}}
# to be [[separate]]d from

====Usage notes====
* Always used with the [[ablative]] of separation.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|2+|careō}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|carians}}
* {{l|la|cariceus}}
* {{l|la|cariēs}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|la|cassus}}
* {{l|la|castrō}}
* {{l|la|castus}}

====Descendants====
