Page 1009 castigo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-}}
From an unattested noun *''castis'' ("purity, virtue") – compare {{m|la|castus||chaste}} – , from {{m|la|careō||I lack}} + {{m|ine-pro|*-tis|-tis}}, {{suffix|la||-igō|t2=act, make|id2=denominative}}, the latter a [[suffixal]] form of {{m|la|agō}}. Confer {{m|la|fūstīgō}} and {{m|la|fatīgō}}, the formations of which also involve ti-derivations.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|castīgō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|castīgō}}

# to [[castigate]], [[chastise]], [[reprove]]
#: {{syn|la|increpō|accūsō|corripiō|exprobrō|incūsō|obloquor|arripiō|pulsō|reprehendō|animadvertō}}
# to [[punish]]
#: {{syn|la|multō|pūniō|expiō|obiūrgō|animadvertō|mulctō|moneō|plēctō|ulcīscor|exsequor}}
#: {{ux|la|Qui bene amat, bene '''castigat'''.|He, who loves well, '''castigates''' well (lit. Spare the rod and spoil the child.)}}
#* {{Q|la|Cicero|Tusculan Disputations|3|64|quote=Pueros vero matres et magistri '''castigare''' etiam solent, nec verbis solum, sed etiam verberibus. Si quid in domestico luctu hilarius ab is factum est aut dictum, plorare cogunt. Quid?|t=Mothers and teachers are used to '''chastise''' children not only with words, but also with hits of the rod. If the children do or say something rather "cheerful" in a small argument, they make them cry. Why?}}
# to [[rebuke]], [[reprimand]]
#: {{syn|la|obiūrgō|perstringō|arguō|īnsector|corripiō|accūsō|incūsō|damnō|obloquor|increpō|acclāmō|inclāmō|compellō|reprehendō|animadvertō}}
#* {{Q|la|Quintilian|Institutiones|2|2|5|quote=Plurimus ei de honesto ac bono sermo sit: nam quo saepius monuerit, hoc rarius '''castigabit'''.|t=May his [the ideal rhetoric teacher's] discussion be very often about what is honourable, good: the more he reminds the student, the less '''he will reprimand''' the student.}}
# to [[correct]], [[amend]]

