Page 1152 citharizo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{bor|la|grc|κιθαρίζω||I play the lyre}}, from {{m|grc|κιθάρα||lyre}}, of pre-Greek origin.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|citharizō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|citharizō}}

# to [[play]] the [[cithara]]
#* {{Q|la|Cornelius Nepos|Liber de excellentibus ducibus exterarum gentium|Epaminodas 2.1|quote=Nam et citharizare et cantare ad chordarum sonum doctus est a Dionysio, qui non minore fuit in musicis gloria quam Damon aut Lamprus, quorum pervulgata sunt nomina, cantare tibiis ab Olympiodoro, saltare a Calliphrone.|trans=For at the lyre and at singing in harmony with its strings he was taught by Dionysius, who is held in no less glory amongst musicians than Damon or Lamprus, whose names are ubiquitous; to sing to the flutes, by Olympiodorus; and to dance by Calliphron.|year=100-24 B.C.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{m|la|citharīzō}} is the sole Classical Latin borrowing of a Greek verb in {{m|la|-ίζω}}; all other borrowings in this form took place in the Late Latin period, largely by Early Christian writers, originally in religious contexts.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+|citharizō}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|la|cithara}}
* {{l|la|citharista}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|it|cetereggiare|citarizzare}}
* {{desc|ro|cetereza}}

===References===
