Page 1167 clino: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*kleināō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*ḱley-}}, from {{suffix|ine-pro|*ḱel-|*-éyti|t1=to incline|id1=incline|pos2=*éy-present suffix|nocat=1}}. Although clearly a nasal present, a nasal present of Proto-Indo-European date would be {{m|ine-pro||*ḱl̥-n-i-}}, which would not give the attested Latin form. According to De Vaan<ref>{{R:itc:EDL}}</ref>, the nasal present was re-formed as {{m|ine-pro||*ḱli-n-}} in pre-Italic, a change shared also by other Indo-European languages. The long vowel could be by analogy with the perfect, and may be of Italic date.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|clīnō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|clīnō}}

# {{lb|la|rare|nonstandard|_|except as past participle}} to [[bend]], [[incline]]
#* '''1st century BC''', Titus Lucretius Carus; in: ''De rerum natura libri sex: quibus interpretationem et notas addidit Thomas Creech, collegii omnium animarum olim socius. Accedunt variae lectiones IV. edd. antiquissimarum necnon annotationes R. Bentleii'', Oxonii, e typographeo Clarendoniano, 1818, page 85f.:
#*: {{quote|la|Quare etiam atque etiam paullum '''clinare''' necesse 'st<br/> Corpora, nec plus quam minimum, ne fingere motus<br /> Obliquos videamur, et id res vera refutet.<br><small>(In note 243 to this quote the editor clarifies: “Alii, ''inclinare''; sed quis ''clinare'' rejiceret, qui ''clinamen'', v. 292. admittit?”)</small>}}
#* {{quote-text|la|year=1687|author=Jean-Jacques Magnet|title=Pharmacopoea Schroedero-Hoffmanniana illustrataet aucta|page=306
|passage=Optime est Botritis, densa, modice grauis, & in laevitatem magis '''clinans''' […] }}

====Usage notes====
* In Classical Latin, this is only found with certainty as a past participle {{m|la|clīnātus}}.
* Some older editions of classical texts seem to attest various inflected forms of this verb (''clīnāre'' (Lucretius), ''clīnāvit'' (Petronius), ... ) which seem to have been corrected to different forms (prefixed, or to different words altogether) in modern editions.
* In New Latin, the word is very rarely found, possibly as a back-formation from the prefixed forms.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+|clīnō}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|acclīnō}} / {{l|la|adclīnō}}
* {{l|la|clīnāmen}}
* {{l|la|clīnātus}}
* {{l|la|dēclīnō}}
* {{l|la|inclīnō}}
* {{l|la|prōclīnō}}
* {{l|la|reclīnō}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|la|clīvus}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|eo|klini|bor=1}}
* {{desc|it|chinare}}
* {{desc|fro|cliner}}
* {{desc|pms|chiné}}

