Page 1206 cognosco: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|conōscō||Late Latin}}

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*ǵneh₃-}}
From {{af|la|con-|pos1=prefix indicating completion|nosco|alt2=gnōscō|t2=to know}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|cognōscō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3.poet-sync-perf|cognōscō|cognōv|cognit}}

# to [[learn]], to [[get to know]]
#: {{syn|la|agnōscō|inveniō|sentiō|cōnsciō|sapiō|sciō|nōscō|scīscō|intellegō|percipiō|discernō|comperiō|tongeō|cernō|audiō}}
#: {{ant|la|ignōrō|nesciō}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|ab}} (or {{l|la|ex}}) {{l|la|aliqua}} '''cognoscere'''|t=to '''learn''' from someone}}
#* {{Q|la|Julius Caesar|De Bello Gallico|1|21|quote=Eodem die ab exploratoribus certior factus hostes sub monte consedisse milia passuum ab ipsius castris octo, qualis esset natura montis et qualis in circuitu ascensus qui '''cognoscerent''' misit.|t=Having been informed by explorers that the enemy had sat down at the feet of a mount about eight thousand paces away from his camp, he [Julius Caesar] sent men to '''know''' what the mount was like and what was its ascent.}}
#* {{Q|la|Jerome|Vulgate|Ecclesiastes|3|12-13|quote=Et '''cognovi''' quod non esset melius nisi laetari et facere bene in vita sua. <br> Omnis enim homo qui comedit et bibit et videt bonum de labore suo: hoc donum Dei est.|t=And I '''learned''' that there's nothing better than to be happy and do good in one's life. Every person who eats and drinks and sees the fruit of work: this is God's gift.}}
# to be [[acquaint]]ed (with someone), [[recognize]], [[apprehend]]
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Poenulus|1130|quote=''Giddene(ne)s.'' "'''Cognoscin''' Giddenenem ancillam tuam?". ''Hanno.'' Novi.|t="'''Do you know''' Giddenes, your servant maid?" "I know her".}}
# {{lb|la|in perfect tense}} to [[know]], [[perceive]], [[understand]]
#: {{syn|la|comprehendō|dēprehendō|apprehendō|accipiō|concipiō|teneō|apīscor|capiō|complector|excipiō|cōnsequor|exaudiō}}
#* {{Q|la|Sallust|Jugurtha|79|quote=Cyrenenses tardius iere. Id socordiane an casu adciderit, parum '''cognovi'''.|t=The Cyrenians went late. '''I know''' little about whether this event of laziness truly happened.}}
#* {{Q|la|Cicero|Ad Atticum|15|17|quote=De consulum ficto timore '''cognoveram'''; Sicca enim {{lang|grc|φιλοστόργως}} ille quidem sed tumultuosius ad me etiam illam suspicionem pertulit.|t=I '''knew''' about the consuls' imagined fear; our beloved man Sicca told me of course, even if rather disturbed, about that speculation too.}}
# to [[have sex]] with, {{lb|la|Biblical}} to [[know]]
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Heroides|6|133-4|quote=Turpiter illa virum '''cognovit''' adultera virgo; <br> me tibi, teque mihi, taeda pudica dedit.|t=Shamelessly, she '''lay with''' a man as an adulterous virgin, (but) a chaste wedding torch gave me to you, and you to me.}}
#* {{Q|la|Jerome|Vulgate|Genesis|4|1|quote=Adam vero '''cognovit''' Havam uxorem suam, quae concepit et peperit Cain|t=Adam '''knew''' his wife Eve, who conceived and gave birth to Cain}}

====Conjugation====
