Page 1239 colo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*kʷel-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*kʷelō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*kʷel-||to move, to turn (around), to revolve around, and therefore to sojourn, to dwell}}. The same root also gave {{m|la|inquilinus|in-quil-īnus|inhabitant}} and {{m|la|anculus||servant}}.

Cognates include {{cog|grc|πέλω}}, {{m|grc|πόλος}}, {{m|grc|τέλλω}}, {{m|grc|τέλος}}, {{m|grc|τῆλε}}, {{m|grc|πάλαι}}, {{m|grc|κύκλος}}, {{cog|sa|चरति|tr=cárati}}, {{cog|en|wheel}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA|colō|eccl=yes}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|3|colō|colu|cult}}

# to [[cultivate]] the land, [[till]], [[tend]], [[take]] [[care]] of a field or garden (literal)
#: {{syn|la|incolō|subigō}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|3|779|quote=cum '''colerent''' prīscī studiōsius agrōs|trans=''when [the people] of ancient [times] '''were tilling''' their fields more diligently''}}
# to [[inhabit]]
#: {{syn|la|habitō|obsideō|resideō|possideō|cōnsīdō|iaceō|subsīdō|stabulō|incolō|vīvō|versor}}
# to [[frequent]], be the [[guardian]] of, [[cherish]], [[care for]], [[protect]], [[nurture]]
#: {{syn|la|cūrō|accūrō|prōcūrō|videō|respiciō|cōnsultō|cōnsulō|caveō|serviō}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|15-16|quote=quam Iūnō fertur terrīs magis omnibus ūnam<br/>posthabitā '''coluisse''' Samō [...].|trans=''[Carthage], which Juno is said '''to have cherished''' more than any other place, even Samos having been less esteemed [...].''<br/>(The queen of the gods – Juno or Hera – cherished and protected Carthage even more than the island of Samos, where a large temple was dedicated to her worship. See: {{w|Juno (mythology)}}; {{w|Carthage}}; {{w|Samos}}; {{w|Heraion of Samos}}.)}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[worship]], [[honor]], [[revere]], [[reverence]]
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|6|728|quote=coepit Aventīna Pallas in arce '''colī'''|trans=''Pallas began '''to be worshiped''' on the Aventine summit.''<br/>(A temple on the {{w|Aventine Hill}} had been dedicated to Pallas, an epithet of {{w|Athena}}, whom the Romans equated with {{w|Minerva}}.)}}
#* {{Q|la|Jerome|Vulgate|Exodus|20|5|quote=Nōn adōrābis ea, neque '''colēs''': ego sum Dominus Deus tuus fortis, zēlōtēs, vīsitāns inīquitātem patrum in fīliōs, in tertiam et quārtam generātiōnem eōrum quī ōdērunt mē.|trans=''Thou shalt not bow down thyself to them, nor '''serve''' them: for I the LORD thy God am a jealous God, visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children unto the third and fourth generation of them that hate me.''}}

=====Usage notes=====
The words {{m|la|colō}} and {{m|la|excolō}} can be confused in usage. Their root being the {{der|la|ine-pro|*kʷel-}}, originally {{m|la|colō}} probably meant turning (plowing for cultivation) the soil, and by extension of inhabiting a place; by further extension, it adopted the senses of improving said habitation by cultivating the land and through the specific nurture of crops. While figurative senses of nurturing and improving are attributable to {{m|la|colō}}, they are more properly rendered by {{m|la|excolō}}, since nurture and improvement are the parts of the (literal) process of land cultivation "out of" ({{m|la|ex-}}) which springs {{m|la|excolō}}, rendering the figurative and universal sense of cultivating. This means {{m|la|colō}}/{{m|la|cultus}}/{{m|la|cultiō}} can properly render cultivation strictly in the agricultural sense, while {{m|la|excolō}}/{{m|la|excultus}}/{{m|la|excultiō}} are for the senses of cultivation—improvement by means of effort or labor—in the general, non-agricultural sense.

