Page 1293 como: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From {{prefix|la|con|emō}}. Compare with its uncontracted form {{m|la|coemō}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA|cōmō}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|3|cōmō|cōmps|cōmpt}}

# to [[bring]] together, [[form]], [[frame]], [[construct]], [[arrange]]
# {{lb|la|usually of hair}} to [[take care of]], [[care for]]; [[comb]], [[dress]]; [[braid]]
# {{lb|la|in general}} to [[adorn]], [[deck]], [[dress]], [[ornament]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|3|cōmō|cōmps|cōmpt}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{m|la|quōmodo}} through consonant elision or some kind of metanalysis of the /d/ when the following /o/ elided in antevocalic position. Cf. the Romance reflexes compounded with {{m|la|et}}.

Securely attested from mid-1st c. CE onwards.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|la|quomo||Late Latin}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA|cōmō̆|eccl=0}}
* {{la-IPA|como|eccl=0}}
* Note: the length of the final vowel is unattested. It most likely displayed the same vacillation as the other adverbs such as {{m|la|ergō}} and {{m|la|vērō}} do during the post-Augustan period. Romance descendants show both open and closed first vowel.

====Adverb====
{{la-adv|cōmō̆|-}}

# {{lb|la|non-literary}} {{alternative form of|la|quōmodō̆|t=in what manner or way, how}}

=====Descendants=====
{{see|la|quōmodo}}

===References===
* {{cite-journal|it|author=Daniela Urbanová|title=Alcune particolarità della comparazione (quomodo – sic, quemadmodum – sic, ita uti – sic) in latino volgare, con particolare attenzione alle defixiones|work=Graeco-Latina Brunensia|page=329–343|year=2016|url=http://digilib.phil.muni.cz/handle/11222.digilib/136244|issue=2|issn=1803-7402|doi=10.5817/GLB2016-2-23}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
* {{R:Stillwell}}

