Page 1629 continor: -------- begin text --------
===Alternative forms===
*{{alt|la|continuor}}
===Etymology===
Uncertain. In Lewis & Short explained as a mediopassive use of {{m|la|continuō}} in the sense of "making oneself continuous to". In the {{w|Thesaurus Linguae Latinae|Thesauro Linguae Latinae}} a relation to {{m|la|contiō}} and {{m|la|conventiō}} is suggested; Rönsch connected it with {{m|la|contus}}<ref>Hermann Rönsch (1891): Collectanea philologica. Bremen: Heinsius, p. 237.
 </ref>, inferring the basic sense "I hit (with a pole)".

===Pronunciation===
*{{la-IPA|continor}}
===Verb===
{{la-verb|1|continor}}
# to [[encounter]]
#* {{Q|la|Apuleius|The Golden Ass|1|24|quote=inde mē commodum ēgredientem '''continātur''' Pȳthias.}}
#* {{Q|la|Apuleius|The Golden Ass|6|18|quote=jamque confectā bonā parte mortiferae viae '''contināberis''' claudum asinum lignōrum gerulum cum agāsōne similī.}}
#* {{Q|la|Symmachus|Epistulae|year=4th century CE|1|53|2|quote=nisi forte in silvīs Apollinem '''contināris''', ut ille pāstor Hēsiodus, quem poētica laurū Camēnālis familia corōnāvit.}}
====Declension====
{{la-conj|1|continor}}

===References===
*{{R:Gaffiot}}
*{{R:L&S|continuor}}
*{{R:Georges}}
*{{R:TLL|continor|4|720|64}}
<references/>
-------- end text --------
Page 1630 continuo: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|continuō}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{affix|la|continuus|-ō|id2=denominative}}.

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1+|continuō}}

# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[join]], [[connect]], [[unite]], [[make]] [[continuous]]
#: {{syn|la|colligo|illigo|ligo|cōnserō|coniungo|consocio|iungo|contraho|concilio}}
# {{lb|la|transitive|time}} to [[follow]] [[successively]] or [[uninterruptedly]] or [[immediately]], [[pursuit]]
#: {{syn|la|persequor|sequor|cōnsequor|excipiō|exsequor}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[extend]], [[prolong]], [[lengthen]]
#: {{syn|la|extendō|porrigō|prōlongō|prōferō|prōtrahō|trahō|explicō}}
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[continue]], [[keep on]], [[do]] without [[pause]], [[persist]]

