Page 1708 corusco: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|la|choruscō|corruscō|coriscō||pcl|med}}
* {{alter|la|scoruscō||vet}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|coruscō}}

===Etymology 1===
Related to {{m|la|coruscus|t=vibrating, flashing}}. Of {{unc|la|nocap=1}} origin according to the [[w:Thesaurus Linguae Latinae|TLL]]; perhaps related to [[Ancient Greek]] {{m|grc|κορύσσω|t=equip with helmet}} and {{m|grc|κορύπτω|t=headbutt}}. Compare Ancient Greek {{m|grc|σκαίρω|t=hop, dance}}, {{m|grc|ἀσκαρίζω|t=throb}}, {{m|grc|σκιρτάω|t=leap, bound}}.

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1+.nopass|coruscō}} {{tlb|la|poetic|_|in|_|Classical|much more popular in|_|Late Latin}}

# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[shake]], [[brandish]], [[wave]], [[move about]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|5|641|thru=643|quote=Haec memorāns prima infēnsum vī corripit ignem<br>sublatāque procul dextrā cōnīxa '''coruscat'''<br>et iacit.|trans=Mentioning these, she first grasped the deadly fire with violence<br>and with the right hand raised high, '''brandishes''' it with effort<br>and throws.}}
#* {{Q|la|Statius|Thebaid|12|431|thru=432|quote=Exundant dīvīsō vertice flammae<br>alternōsque apicēs abruptā lūce '''coruscant'''.|trans=The flames rush up with a bifurcated top<br>and '''wave''' their tips with intermittent light.}}
#* '''4th C. {{CE}}''', [[w:Avienus|Avienus]], ''Aratea'' [[s:la:Aratea (Avienus)|636–638]]:
#*: {{quote|la|Namque et sīdereīs cycnus secat aethera pinnīs,<br>dōnātus caelō, nōn clārō lūcidus astrō,<br>sed tamen ōs flagrāns et guttura longa '''coruscāns'''.
|translation=For the swan splits the aether with starry wings too,<br>gifted to the sky, not luminous with a bright star,<br>but still '''brandishing''' its shining mouth and long neck.}}
#* '''354 {{CE}} – 450 {{CE}}''', [[w:Saint Augustine|Saint Augustine]], ''Enarratio in Psalmos'' [https://archive.org/stream/patrologiaecurs50migngoog#page/n309/mode/2up 121.9]:
#*: Portāvērunt Deum, et dē ipsīs Deus '''coruscābat''' mīrācula, tonābat terrōrēs, pluēbat cōnsōlātiōnēs.
#*:: They carried the Lord with them, and the Lord '''brandished''' miracles, thundered terrors, rained consolations regarding them.
# {{lb|la|intransitive|somewhat|uncommon}} to [[shake]], [[vibrate]], [[flit]]
#* {{Q|la|Lucretius|De rerum natura|2|320|quote={{...}}satiātī agnī lūdunt blandēque '''coruscant'''.|trans=The sated lambs play and gently '''thrust with the horns'''.}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Georgicon|4|73|quote=Tum trepidae inter sē coeunt pinnīsque '''coruscant'''{{...}}|trans=Then [the bees] come together in a hurry and '''vibrate''' with their wings.}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Metamorphoses|4|492|thru=494|quote=Mōtae sonuēre colubrae,<br>parsque iacent umerīs, pars circum pectora lapsae<br>sībila dant saniemque vomunt linguīsque '''coruscant'''.|trans=The disturbed snakes hissed<br>and a part sit on the shoulders, a part, fallen down to the breast,<br>give hisses and vomit blood and '''flutter''' with their tongues.}}
#* {{Q|la|Juvenal|Satires|3|234|thru=236|quote=Scinduntur tunicae sartae modo, longa '''coruscat'''<br>serrācō veniente abiēs, atque altera pīnum<br>plaustra vehunt.|trans=Tunics just mended are torn, the long pine log<br>'''shakes''' as its waggon nears, and another cart<br>is carrying a pine tree.}}
#* '''3rd C. {{CE}}''', [[w:Tertullian|Tertullian]], ''De pudicitia'' [https://archive.org/stream/quintiseptimiflo20tert#page/n265/mode/2up 14], in ''Quinti Septimi Florentis Tertulliani opera'' (volume I), [[w:August Reifferscheid|August Reifferscheid]] and [[w:Georg Wissowa|Georg Wissowa]], 1890, pages 248–249:
#*: {{quote|la|Vidēmus itaque hōc in locō dīvīsam apostolī sevēritātem in quendam īnflātum et in quendam incestum, in alterum virgā, in alterum sententiā armātum. Virga, quam minābātur, sententia, quam exsequēbātur; illam adhūc '''coruscantem''', hanc statim fulminantem, quā increpābat, qua damnābat.
|translation=And so we see in this place the apostle's divided severity against someone haughty and someone unchaste, equipped with a rod against one and with words against the other. The rod, which he was threatening, the words, which he was saying; the former still '''shaking''', the latter immediately fulminating, one with which he was rattling, one with which he was condemning.}}
# {{lb|la|personal|and|rarely|impersonal}} to strike or flash [[lightning]]
#* '''220 {{BCE}} – ''[[circa|c.]]'' 130 {{BCE}}''', [[w:Marcus Pacuvius|Marcus Pacuvius]], ''Tragic fragments'' [https://archive.org/stream/bub_gb_rMaGAAAAIAAJ#page/n159/mode/2up 45.5]:
#*: Flamma inter nūbēs '''coruscat''', caelum tonitrū contremit.
#*:: Fire '''strikes''' amongst clouds, the sky trembles with thunder.
#* {{Q|la|Pliny the Elder|Naturalis Historia|2|43|112|quote={{...}}et esse tonitrua inpactōrum ignium plāgās ideōque prōtinus '''coruscāre''' igneās nūbium rīmās.|trans=[And I agree that] thunderclaps are the blows of fires collided and that for that reason the fiery cracks of clouds '''flash lightning''' at once.}}
#* {{Q|la|Apuleius|De mundo|15|quote=Quippe ubi nūbēs adflīctrīx ignem, ut ignifera saxa adtrīta inter sē, dat, obtutus velōcius inlūstriōra contingit, audītus, dum ad aurēs venit, sēriōre sēnsū concipitur; ita prius '''coruscāre''' caelum crēditur et mox tonāre.|trans=Of course when a striking cloud emits fire, like flintstones rubbed against one another, the sight comes to the eyes faster; the sound is felt with tardier sense, until it reaches the ears; thus the sky is believed to '''flash lightning''' first and thunder soon after.}}
#* '''4th C. {{CE}}''', [[w:Saint Jerome|Saint Jerome]], ''[[w:Vulgate|Vulgate]]'' [[w:Book of Ezekiel|Ezekiel]] 1:14:
#*: {{quote|la|Et animālia ībant et revertēbantur, in similitūdinem fulguris '''coruscantis'''.
|translation=And the beings went and came, like a '''striking''' lightning.}}
#* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 540 {{CE}} – ''[[circa|c.]]'' 554 {{CE}}''', [[w:Cassiodorus|Cassiodorus]], ''Expositio in Psalterium'' [https://archive.org/stream/patrologiaecurs27goog#page/n480/mode/2up 127.1] in ''[[w:Patrologia Latina|Patrologia Latina]]'' (volume 70), [[w:Jacques-Paul Migne|Jacques-Paul Migne]], 1865, page 932:
#*: Sed nē forsitan ''timendum'' sōlummodo putārēs ''Dominum'' cum tonat, cum '''coruscat''', cum terrās tremōre concutit, cum crīminōsīs minātur interitum, addidit, ''quī ambulant in viīs eius'', ut non sōlum suspendāmur ab āctibus prāvīs, vērum etiam in fidē probēmur ambulāre rēctissimā.
#*:: But so that you may not consider ''the Lord'' to be ''feared'' only when He thunders, when He '''strikes lightning''', when he threatens the sinful with death, he added “''that walketh in his ways''”, so that we may not only doubt in the wrong acts, but that we may also be shown worthy in faith to walk on the straightest way.
# {{lb|la|figurative|frequent}} to [[flash]], [[coruscate]], [[gleam]]
#* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 850 {{CE}} – 930 {{CE}}''', [[w:Hucbald|Hucbald]], ''Ecloga de calvis'' 142–143 in ''[[w:Monumenta Germaniae Historica|Monumenta Germaniae Historica]]'' (tome 4, fascicle 1), Paulus de Winterfeld, Berlin 1899, page [https://archive.org/stream/p1monumentagerma04geseuoft#page/271/mode/2up 271]:
#*: Collūcent calvī: calvōrum cassida candet,<br>conrutilāns caelī ceu cōpia clāra '''coruscat'''.
#*:: The bald are bright: their pate shines,<br>glowing reddish it '''gleams''' like the brilliant fullness of the sky.

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|1+.nopass|coruscō}}

=====Derived terms=====
