Page 2264 duco: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*dewk-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*doukō}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*déwkti}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*dewk-}}.  Cognate with {{cog|en|tow}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|dūcō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|irreg|dūcō}}

# to [[lead]], [[guide]], [[conduct]], [[lead]] [[away]]
#: {{syn|la|moderor|ago|deduco|produco|perfero|traduco|impingo}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|4|527|quote=cui dea '''‘dūc!’''' inquit ‘scīstī, quā cōgere possēs’|trans=''To which the goddess replies: ‘‘'''Lead on!''' You have understood how you are able to compel me.’’''<br/>(A humble farmer named Celeus and his young daughter have spoken kindly to an old woman, and have invited her to visit their cottage, unaware that their guest is the goddess Ceres in disguise.)}}
# {{lb|la|by extension}} to [[take]]
#* {{Q|la|Catullus|Carmina|64|quote=''In vestrās potuistī '''dūcere''' sēdēs.''|trans= You could have '''taken''' (me) to your home.}}
# to [[draw]], [[pull]]
# to [[think]], [[consider]], [[regard]]
# to [[marry]], to [[take]] {{gloss|as one's wife}}
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Miles Gloriosus|679|quote=''Propter dīvitiās meās, licuit uxōrem dōtātam genere summō '''dūcere'''.''|trans=By reason of my wealth, I could have '''married''' a dowered wife of the best family.}}
# to [[prolong]], to [[protract]]
#: {{syn|la|prōdūco}}
# {{lb|la|military|transitive}} to [[march]], [[command]], [[lead]] {{gloss|e.g., an army}}
# {{lb|la|passive|_|with|_|active|_|meaning}} to [[march]] {{gloss|said of soldiers, lit. "be led"}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|diu|Diū}} {{l|la|mīlitēs}} '''dūcēbantur'''.|For a long time, the soldiers '''were marching'''.}}
# to [[forge]] {{q|rare}}

