Page 2436 eripio: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{affix|la|ex-|gloss1=out of, from|rapiō|gloss2=grab, seize}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|ēripiō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|ēripiō|ēripu|ērept}}

# to [[rescue]], [[deliver]], [[free]], [[save]]
#: {{syn|la|salvō|tūtor|vindicō|cū̆stōdiō|sospitō|teneō|adimō|prōtegō|tegō|dēfendō|sustineō|arceō|tueor|servō|excipiō|prohibeō}}
#: {{ant|la|immineō|īnstō}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|2|289|quote=“‘Heu fuge, nāte deā, tēque hīs, ait, '''ēripe''' flammīs.’”|trans=''[Aeneas recalls Hector’s dire warning:] “‘Alas! Flee, goddess-born,’ [Hector] says, ‘and '''deliver''' yourself from these flames!’”''}}
# to [[preempt]], [[take]] by [[force]]
# to [[snatch]], [[snatch]] [[away]], [[take away]], [[tear out]], [[pull out]], [[pluck]], [[rob]]
#: {{syn|la|rapiō|adimō|auferō|tollō|abdūcō|fraudō|āmoveō|rēmoveō|exhauriō|dēmō|āvertō|corripiō|praedor|extorqueō|agō}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|88-89|quote='''Ēripiunt''' subitō nūbēs caelumque diemque<br/>Teucrōrum ex oculīs; pontō nox incubat ātra.|trans=''Stormclouds suddenly '''take away''' both sky and day[light] from the eyes of the Trojans; black night broods upon the sea.''<br/>(“They take away, suddenly, stormclouds…”: The poet exploits Latin’s flexible word-order and begins line 88 with the energetic verb “ēripiunt.” Note: The Trojans were “Teucrians” or descendants of {{w|King Teucer}}.)}}
# to [[escape]], [[flee]]
#: {{syn|la|fugiō|effugiō|ēvādō|refugiō|cōnfugiō|aufugiō|prōfugiō|perfugiō|diffugiō|āvolō|ēlābor|lābor}}
# {{lb|la|passive}} to [[die]] [[suddenly]], to be [[suddenly]] [[taken away]], to be suddenly [[snatched]] [[away]]

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|ēripiō|ēripu|ērept}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|agnum lupo eripere velle}} (to wish the impossible, literally: to wish to rescue a lamb from a wolf)

====Descendants====
* {{desc|pro|erebre}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
* {{R:M&A}}
