Page 2739 fallo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{unc|la}}. According to De Vaan, from {{inh|la|itc-pro|*falsō}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*(s)gʷʰh₂el-||to stumble}}. Formerly considered to be from {{der|la|ine-pro|*ǵʰwel-|t=to lie, deceive}}, but this does not account for the /a/.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|fallō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|fallō|fefell|fals}}

# to [[deceive]], [[trick]], [[cheat]], [[disappoint]]
#: {{syn|la|dēcipiō|mentior|frūstror|ēlūdō|dēstituō|fraudō|circumdūcō|circumveniō|ingannō|indūcō}}
# {{lb|la|reflexive}} to [[mistake]], be [[mistaken]], [[deceive]] oneself
#* '''426 CE''', [[w:Augustine of Hippo|Augustine of Hippo]], ''De civitate Dei contra paganos'', 11.26:
#*: {{quote|la|Si enim fallor, sum. Nam qui non est, utique nec falli potest; ac per hoc sum, si fallor. Quia ergo sum si fallor, quo modo esse me fallor, quando certum est me esse, si fallor? Quia igitur essem qui fallerer, etiamsi fallerer, procul dubio in eo quod me novi esse, non fallor.|Well, if I am mistaken, I exist. For a man who does not exist can surely not be mistaken either, and if I am mistaken, therefore I exist. So, since I am if I am mistaken, how can I be mistaken in believing that I am when it is certain that if I am mistaken I am. Therefore, from the fact that, if I were indeed mistaken, I should have to exist to be mistaken, it follows that I am undoubtedly not mistaken in knowing that I am. [tr. David S. Wiesen]}}
# to [[escape]] the [[notice]] of, be [[unseen]]
# to [[appease]], [[beguile]]
# to [[swear]] [[falsely]], [[perjure]]

