Page 2760 ferio: -------- begin text --------
{{attention|la|a second verb related to "feria" is missing}}

===Etymology===
Possibly from {{der|la|ine-pro|*bʰerH-||to pierce, strike}}, perhaps with root-final [[laryngeal]] dropped in a [[prevocalic]] position. [[o-grade|O-grade]] reflex is attested in {{m|la|forō}}. Cognate with {{cog|sq|bie||to fall}}, {{cog|ang|ġebered||crushed, kneaded}}, {{cog|en|berry||to beat, thrash}}, {{cog|xcl|բերան}}. More at {{l|en|berry}}.

Alternatively, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*bʰer-||to scrape, to cut}}. Compare {{cog|mga|berna}}, {{cog|goh|berjan}}, {{cog|gmh|berjen}}, {{cog|ang|bered}}, {{cog|grc|φάρω}}/*{{cog|grc|φαράω}},<ref>[[w:Robert S. P. Beekes|R. S. P. Beekes]], ''Etymological Dictionary of Greek'', Brill, 2009, p. 1555.</ref> {{cog|ae|𐬙𐬌𐬲𐬌𐬠𐬁𐬭𐬀}}.

===Verb===
{{la-verb|4.sup-futr-actv-only|feriō|feri|ferīt}}

# to [[hit]], to [[strike]], to [[smite]], to [[beat]], to [[knock]], [[injure]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|102-103|quote=Tālia iactantī, strīdēns Aquilōne procellā<br/>vēlum adversa '''ferit''', flūctūsque ad sīdera tollit.|trans=''[Even as Aeneas is] crying out such [great words], a gust howling from the Northwind '''is striking''' [his] sail full on, and raising up waves to heaven.''<br/>(The description of waves reaching the sky exemplifies [[hyperbole]]. The Northwind: See [[aquilo|Aquilo]]; [[Anemoi]], {{w|Anemoi}}.)}}
#: {{syn|la|tango|percutio|pello|discutio|pulsō|tundō|accido|affligo|percello|impingo|ico|verbero|vulnerō|secō|noceō}}
#: {{ux|la|'''Feriri''' a serpente.|t=To be '''stung''' by a snake.}}
# to [[cut]], to [[thrust]]
# (with accusative) to [[kill]] by striking, to [[slay]], to give a [[deathblow]]
#: {{ux|la|{{l|la|aliquem}} {{l|la|securi}} '''ferire'''.|t=To '''behead''' someone with an axe.}}
# {{lb|la|money}} to [[strike]], to [[stamp]], to [[coin]]
#: {{ux|la|Asses sextantario pondere '''ferire'''.|t=To '''strike''' asses only the sixth part of a pound.}}
#* [[w:en:Moneyer|moneyer]]; in the Roman Republic, the abbreviation ''III. VIR. AAAFF.'' or even ''III. VIR. A.P.F.'' (''tresviri ad pecuniam '''feriundum''''') was written on the coins, but it stood for:
#*: Tresviri aere argento auro flando '''feriundo'''.
#*:: ''Three men for '''striking''' and casting bronze, silver and copper coins.''
# to [[make]] a [[deal]] or [[contract]] or [[compact]], [[covenant]], or [[treaty]]
#: {{syn|la|ī̆cō|percutiō|pacīscor|pangō}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[strike]], [[reach]], [[affect]]
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|6|518|quote=clāmor Aventīnī saxa propinqua '''ferit'''|trans=''The cry '''strikes''' the nearby rocks of Aventine Hill.''<br/>(See: {{w|Aventine Hill}}.)}}

====Usage notes====
* Perfect and passive forms are rare. Perfect forms and perfect passive participle are usually supplied by its synonym, {{m|la|percutio}}. Sometimes {{m|la|ictus}}, the perfect passive participle of {{m|la|īcō}}, is used.
* The verb form {{m|la|feriunt||they strike}} had the archaic spelling {{m|la|ferinunt}}.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|4.sup-futr-actv-only|feriō|feri|ferīt}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|secūrī feriō}} (I [[behead]])
* {{l|la|foedus feriō}} (I make a compact, covenant or treaty)
* {{l|la|amōrum turpissimōrum foedera feriō}} (I form illicit connections)

