===Etymology===
Probably from {{der|la|ine-pro|*bʰleh₂-||to swing back and forth}}, whence also {{m|la|flagrum}}. In that case cognate to {{cog|non|blaka|blaka, blakra|to flap, flutter}}, {{cog|lt|blokšti||to throw, fling}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|flāgitō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+.p3inf|flāgitō}}

# to [[demand]], [[require]]
#: {{syn|la|requīrō|exposcō|precor|petō|repetō|rogitō|exigō|exorō}}
# to [[entreat]] or [[solicit]]
#: {{syn|la|supplicō|ōrō|obsecrō|expetō|efflāgitō|rogō|ērogō|quaesō}}
#* {{Q|la|Julius Caesar|Bello Gallico|1|16|quote=Interim cotidie Caesar Haeduos frumentum, quod essent publice polliciti, '''flagitare'''.|trans=Meanwhile, Caesar kept daily '''importuning''' the Aedui for the corn which they had promised in the name of their state.}}
# to [[accuse]]

