Page 2836 fragro: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|la|flagrō|fraglō}}

===Etymology===
Ultimately from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*bʰreh₁g-||to smell}}. De Vaan derives it as a denominative verb in {{affix|la|-ō|alt1=-ō, -āre|nocat=1}} from a hypothetical Italic adjective {{m|itc-pro||*fragros}} = {{m|ine-pro||*bʰrh₁g-ro-}} from {{af|ine-pro|*bʰreh₁g-|*-rós|alt2=*-ro-|nocat=1}}.<ref name="EDL">{{R:itc:EDL|page=238|head=fragrō, -āre}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|frag;rō}}
* The ''a'' in the first syllable is short per Schrijver (1991)<ref>{{R:la:Schrijver|pages=185-191}}</ref> and De Vaan (2008)<ref name="EDL"></ref>. Although the first syllable frequently scans heavy in verse due to the following potentially heterosyllabic ''-gr-'', Ernout and Meillet point to the scansion of the participle (found in the manuscript with the dissimilated spelling ''flagrans'') in Catullus 6, 8 as support for the short quantity of the vowel.<ref>{{R:la:EM|head=fragrō, -ās, -āuī, -āre|page=251}}</ref>

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1.nopass|fragrō|fragrāv}}

# to [[emit]] a [[smell]] of, to smell of, to be [[redolent]] of, to [[reek]] of
#: {{syn|la|adoleō}}
# to emit smell, either [[pleasant]] or [[unpleasant]], of

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1.nopass|fragrō|fragrāv}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|fragrāns}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|it|fragrare}}
{{qualifier|through Late or Vulgar Latin {{m|la|flagrō|id=smell}}}}
{{top2}}
* {{desc|ca|flairar}}
* {{desc|fro|flairier}}
** {{desc|en|flair}}
** {{desc|fr|flairer}}
*** {{desc|eo|flari}}
**** {{desc|io|flarar}}
* {{desc|oc|flairar}}
* {{desctree|roa-opt|cheirar}}
* {{desc|scn|ciagurari}}
{{bottom}}

