Page 2883 fundo: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|fundō}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*ǵʰewd-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*hundō}} (with fūsus for *fussus after fūdī), from {{der|la|ine-pro|*ǵʰewd-}}. The change ''h-'' > ''f-'' is irregular (before -u-? Weiss, Outline, p. 77f.). Cognates include {{cog|grc|χέω}} and {{cog|ang|ġēotan}}.

====Verb====
{{la-verb|3.pass-3only|fundō|fūd|fūs}}

# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[pour out]], [[shed]]
#: {{syn|la|effundō|confundō|diffundō|dēfundō|differō|indūcō|sternō|dissipō}}
# {{lb|la|military}} to [[overthrow]], [[overcome]], [[rout]], [[vanquish]] an [[enemy]], [[rout]], [[scatter]]
#: {{syn|la|conquestō|dēvincō|vincō|superō|domō|prōflīgō|caedō|obruō|prōsternō|sternō|opprimō|ēvincō|exsuperō|pellō|impellō|subiciō|premō|subigō|dissipō}}
# to [[throw]] or [[cast]] to the [[ground]], [[prostrate]]
#: {{syn|la|prōsternō|sternō|prōflīgō|ruō}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|192-193|quote=nec prius absistit quam septem ingentia victor<br/>corpora '''fundat''' humī et numerum cum nāvibus aequet.|trans=''Nor does it stop before [Aeneas], [as] victor, '''throws''' seven huge bodies to the ground, and equals the number with his ships.''<br/>(The deer hunt shows Aeneas’s potential to be a military leader; note Virgil’s use of the present anticipatory subjunctive – “fundat” and “[[aequet]]” – to express purposeful actions.)}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[found]], make by [[smelt]]ing
# {{lb|la|transitive|figuratively}} to [[moisten]], [[wet]]
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[extend]], [[spread]] out
#: {{syn|la|sternō}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[utter]]

