Page 2886 fungor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*bʰewg-|id=enjoy}}
From {{inh|la|ine-pro|*bʰewg-||to enjoy}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|fungor}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|fungor|fūnct}}

# to [[perform]], [[execute]], [[administer]], [[discharge]], [[finish]], [[complete]] or [[end]] something, [[observe]] (with [[Appendix:Glossary#ablative case|ablative]] of the task or function)
#: {{syn|la|perficiō|dēfungor|cōnficiō|agō|efficiō|cumulō|absolvō|inclūdō|claudō|expleō|nāvō|conclūdō|condō|peragō|perpetrō|patrō|exsequor|trānsigō|gerō|exhauriō}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|vicis|vice}} {{l|la|alicuius}} '''fungor'''|t=I [[deputise]] for someone}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Caesar|Caesar]]|[[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]]|VII, 25|quote={{l|la|eodem}} {{l|la|illo}} {{l|la|munere}} '''fungebatur'''|t='''Was performing''' the same function}}

====Usage notes====
* The verb {{m-self|la|fungor}} and others like it, {{m|la|ūtor}}, {{m|la|fruor}}, {{m|la|potior}}, {{m|la|vescor}}, and their compounds, regularly [[wikipedia:Case government|govern]] the ablative case.
* In early Latin, {{m-self|la|fungor}} governed the accusative case.<ref>Charles Edwin Bennett, 1914. ''Syntax of Early Latin: The cases'', [https://books.google.com/books?id=AFRAAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA211 page 211]</ref>

====Conjugation====
