Page 2927 glubo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*glewbʰ-}}
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*glewbʰ-||to split}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|glūbō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|glūbō|glūps|glū̆pt}} {{tlb|la|uncommon}}

# {{lb|la|literally}} to [[strip]] the [[bark]] from a [[tree]], to [[peel]], to [[shuck]]
#* {{Q|la|Cato the Elder|De Agricultura|33|quote=Salictum suo tempore caedito, '''glubito''', arteque alligato.|t=Cut down any willow at its due time, '''strip its bark''', and bind the bark well.}}
#* {{Q|la|G. F. H. Sykes|Grammatical Exercises in Latin Prose Composition|year=1877|quote=Filia mihi haec poma '''glupsit'''.|refn=<ref>{{cite-book|author=G. F. H. Sykes|title=Grammatical Exercises in Latin Prose Composition|year=1877|page=89|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=R5UCAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA89}}</ref>}}

# {{lb|la|vulgar}} to peel back the [[foreskin]] of, to [[masturbate]]
#* {{Q|la|Catullus|58|quote=Nunc in quadriviis et angiportis <br> '''glubit''' magnanimi Remi nepotes.|t=Now in the crossroads and alleyways <br> she '''masturbates''' the grandsons of brave Remus.||notes=or "magnanimos Remi nepotes" "the brave grandsons of Remus"}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Wilhelm Hertzberg|Wilhelm Hertzberg]]|Sex. Aurelii Propertii Elegiarum libri quattuor. [...]|34|year=1843|quote=Quid de Lesbia dicam, quae, quamvis Catulli chartis immortalis facta, in angiportis '''glupsit''' magnanimos Remi nepotes (cf. Cat. LVIII.), quid de Lycoride, Galli amica (Prop. El. II, 34, 91.), cuius verum nomen Cytheridem fuisse Servius docet (ad Virg. Bucc. X, 1.), Volumnii Eutrapeli liberta (de qua cf. Cic. Phil. II, 24. ad Attic. X, 10. 16. ad Div. IX, 26. Plut. vit. Ant. c. 9. Plin. Nat. Hist. VIII, 16.), mima simul et meretrice?|t= What shall I say of Lesbia, who, ever so much made immortal by Catullus's writings, '''peeled/masturbated''' in the alleys the brave grandsons of Remus (cf. Cat. LVIII.), what of Lycoris, mistress of Gallus (Prop. El. II, 34, 91.), whose true name, Servius teaches, was Cytheris (ad Virg. Bucc. X, 1.), a freedwoman of Volumnius Eutrapelus (on which cf. Cic. Phil. II, 24. ad Attic. X, 10. 16. ad Div. IX, 26. Plut. vit. Ant. c. 9. Plin. Nat. Hist. VIII, 16.), a mime actress and at the same time a prostitute?|refn=<ref>{{cite-book|author=Wilhelm Hertzberg|authorlink=Wilhelm Hertzberg|title=Sex. Aurelii Propertii Elegiarum libri quattuor. [...] addidit, quaestionum Propertianarum libris tribus et commentariis illustravit Guil. Ad. B. Hertzberg. Tomus I. Quaestiones continens.|year=1843|page=34|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=EctUAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA34|oclc=1164653337}}</ref>}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|glūbō|glūps|glū̆pt}}
*No perfect stem is attested in Classical Latin<ref>{{cite-book |last=Allen |first=Joseph H. |last2=Greenough |first2 = James B. |date=1874 |title=A Latin Grammar for Schools and Colleges Founded on Comparative Grammar|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/latingrammar03allegoog/page/n94/ |publisher=Ginn Brothers |location=Boston |page=73}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book |last=Adam |first=Alexander |date=1818 |title=The Rudiments of Latin and English Grammar; Designed to Facilitate the Study of Both Languages, by Connecting Them Together.|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/rudimentslatina02adamgoog/page/n98 |publisher=E. Duyckinck, and James Eastburn & Co. |location=New York |page=89}}</ref>; nevertheless, some grammars and dictionaries list a perfect stem in ''glūps-'' (compare {{m|la|scrībō}}, perfect stem ''scrīps-'' and {{m|la|nūbō}}, perfect stem ''nūps-'') and at least one form built on this stem has been used in New Latin (see Hertzberg 1843 and Sykes 1877, quoted above). The supine stem is attested once for the prefixed derivative {{m|la|dēglūbō}}, but not in a way that clarifies the hidden quantity of the vowel (which could be long by analogy with the present stem, but could conceivably be short if descended from an old zero-grade form {{m|ine-pro||*glubʰto-}}).

====Synonyms====
* {{l|la|exuō}}
* {{l|la|spoliō}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|dēglūbō}}

===References===
<references/>

===Further reading===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
* {{R:itc:EDL}}

