Page 2958 habeo: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|abeō||Vulgar|Late Latin|Pompeian inscriptions}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|la|itc-pro|*habēō}} or {{m|itc-pro|*haβēō}}, the latter may be from {{der|la|ine-pro|*gʰeh₁bʰ-||to grab, to take}}. Compare {{cog|sga|gaibid||takes, holds}}, {{cog|pl|gabać||to accost, sue}}. {{ncog|en|have}} is '''not''' a cognate, despite similarity in meaning and form; it is instead cognate with {{m|la|capiō||to take}} through Proto-Indo-European.

Oscan and Umbrian have cognate forms with ''-b-''<ref>{{R:itc:EDL}}</ref>, which must reflect an original {{m|ine-pro||-b-}}, because Proto-Italic {{m|itc-pro||-β-}} (and therefore [[Proto-Indo-European|PIE]] {{m|ine-pro||-bʰ-}}) becomes ''-f-'', not ''-b-'', in those languages. On the other hand, {{m|ine-pro||b}} is a rare phoneme in [[Proto-Indo-European|PIE]], whose status is still disputed. Thus, the exact origin of this word is not clear.

Among the oldest attestations are the works of Plautus (circa 254 to 184 BC) and the ''Senatus consultum de Bacchanalibus'' (186 BC). Umbrian cognate ''hab-'' attested in the ''Iguvine Tablets'' (oldest tablets 3rd century BC). Oscan cognate ''haf-''<ref>Perfectum: ''hip-''; Carl Darling Buck believes the ''f'' is a mistake and should be a ''p'' so the present stem would be ''hap-''.</ref> attested in the ''Tabula Bantina'' (89 BC).

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|habeō|eccl=yes}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-habeo.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2+.p3inf|habeō}}

# to [[have]], [[hold]]
#: {{syn|la|possideō|teneō}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|spero|Spero}} {{l|la|ut}} {{l|la|pacem}} '''habeant''' {{l|la|semper}}|t=I hope that they may always '''have''' peace}}
#:: ''O di immortales, ubinam gentium sumus? Quam rem publicam '''habemus'''? In qua urbe vivimus?.''
#::: O ye immortal gods, where on earth are we? What is the government '''we have?''' In what city are we living?
# to [[own]], have {{gloss|possessions}}
# to [[possess]], have {{gloss|qualities}}
#: {{syn|la|possideō|obtineō|teneō|capiō}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|annos|Annos}} {{l|la|viginti}} '''habet'''.|t=He is twenty years old.}}
#* [[w:Quintilian|Quintilian]], Institutio Oratoria 6.3.73
#*: ''triginta ... annos '''habere'''''
#*:: to be thirty years old
#* [[w:Sallust|Sallust]], Bellum Catalinae
#*: Nam divitiarum et formae gloria fluxa atque fragilis est, virtus clara aeternaque '''habetur'''.
#*:: For the glory of wealth and beauty is fleeting and perishable; that of the mind is illustrious and '''possessed''' forever.
# to [[retain]], [[maintain]]
# to [[conduct]], [[preside]] over
# to [[regard]], [[consider]] or [[account]] a person or thing as something
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Titus Livius|Titus Livius]]|[[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]]|I, 40|quote='''habuerant''' {{l|la|se}} {{l|la|pulsos}}|t=They '''considered''' themselves being expelled}}
#: {{uxi|la|in numerō '''habēre'''|to '''rank'''}}
#: {{uxi|la|Diemque cladis quotannis maestum '''habuerit''' ac lugubrem|t=And each year he '''considered''' the day of the disaster gloomy and mournful}}
# to [[accept]], [[bear]], [[endure]]
# {{lb|la|of feelings, problems}} to [[affect]], [[trouble]] (someone)
#* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab Urbe Condita|26|1|quote=Ea tum cura maxime intentos '''habebat''' Romanos, non ab ira tantum, quae in nullam unquam ciuitatem iustior fuit, quam quod urbs tam nobilis ac potens, sicut defectione sua traxerat aliquot populos, ita recepta inclinatura rursus animos uidebatur ad ueteris imperii respectum.|year=27-25 BCE|trans=This concern in particular '''troubled''' the mindful Romans at the time, not so much because of anger, which has never been more justified against any other city, rather because a city so noble and powerful, in the same way that it had attracted the support of a number of communities by its revolt, was thought would again turn attention back towards respect for the previous government once recaptured.}}
# {{lb|la|Late Latin|Medieval Latin|auxiliary verb for perfect tense}} to [[have]]
#* {{quote-text|la|year=430-489|author=w:Caius Sollius Modestus Apollinaris Sidonius|title=Epistolae Liber Octavus, Epistola I|page=278|passage={{...}}hoc quasi stagnum pernavigemus nam satis '''habeo deliberatum''', sicut adhibendam in consciptione diligentiam{{...}}}}
#: {{uxi|la|Nec in publico vestimenta lavare, nec berbices tondere '''habeant licitum'''|They haven't allowed clothes to be washed in public, neither to shave sheep}}
#: {{uxi|la|Illud sacramentum quod juratum '''habeo'''|t=The oath that I have sworn}}
# {{lb|la|Late Latin|Medieval Latin|present with infinitive}} to [[want]]; [[will]], [[shall]], [[should]]
#: {{uxi|la|Feri eum adhuc, nam si non feriveris, ego te ferire '''habeo'''|t=Hit him again, for if you don't, I shall hit you}}
#: {{uxi|la|Currens affer illum ad me, ego enim eum '''habeo''' baptizare|t=Bring him to me quickly, I will baptize him}}
#: {{uxi|la|Ipse enim, quia ægrotat, '''habeo''' eum visitare|t=He who is sick, I want to visit him}}
# {{lb|la|Late Latin|Medieval Latin|past imperfect with infinitive}} [[would]]
#* {{Q|la|Augustine|Sermones|CCLIII|253|quote= '''Sanare te habebat''' Deus per indulgentiam, si fatereris.}}
# {{lb|la|Late Latin|Medieval Latin}} to [[have to]]; to be [[compel]]led
#: {{uxi|la|A patria Cathaloniæ se absentare '''habuerunt''', et in fugam se constituerunt, ne justitia de ipsis fieret|t=They '''had to''' leave from the land of Cathalonia, and decided to escape, so that justice would not be made of them}}
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin|existential}} [[there be]]
#: {{uxi|la|'''Habet''' in Spinogilo mansum dominicatum cum casa et aliis casticiis sufficienter|t=There is a lord's villa in Spinogilo with a house and other buildings}}

====Usage notes====
* Another way of denoting ownership besides using the verb {{m|la|habeō}} is using the possessor in the [[dative case]] (e.g. {{m|la|mihi||to me}}, {{m|la|tibi||to you}}, {{m|la|nōbīs||to us}}) with the copula {{m|la|sum|esse|to be}}, literally asking whether the item in question "is at you". For example:
*: '''''Habēs'''ne epistolas?'' - Do you '''have''' the letters?
*: ''Suntne tibi epistolae?'' - Do you have the letters? (literally, Are the letters for you?)
* And to answer one could say:
*: ''Ita est, '''habeo''' epistolas.'' - Yes, I '''have''' the letters.
*: ''Etiam, sunt mihi epistolae.'' - Yes, I have the letters. (literally, Yes indeed, the letters are for me.)
* A more common way to express age, especially found in the writings of both Cicero and Cornelius Nepos, is the idiom ''aliquot annos natus sum'' (lit. "I have been born for some number of years"):
*: ''[Cato], qui annos quinque et octoginta natus excessit e vita, ...'' (Cicero, ''Brutus'' 80)
*: Cato, who died when he was 85 years old, ...
*In Late Latin, as the classical synthetic future tense began to decline in speech from phonetic changes, one of the various periphrases thereof was to use the present inflections of habeo with the infinitive; originally this construction was strictly modal:
** '''Late 2nd century CE''', Aulus Gellius, ''Noctes Atticae'', Book 20, 10,2:
**: {{ux|la|si quid igitur ex Vergilio, Plauto, Ennio '''quaerere habes''', quaeras licet.|t=If you have something then to ask of Vergil & Plautus & Ennius, you may ask it.}}
*Eventually it became the popular way to express the future tense in Romance:
** '''Early 5th century CE''', Augustine, ''In Evangelium Ioannis Tractatus'', I, 4,2:
**: {{ux|la|Tempestas illa '''tollere habet''' totam paleam de area|t=The storm will lift up all the chaff from the ground}}

