Page 2960 habito: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{suf|la|habeō|itō|pos2=frequentative suffix}}. Clifford Weber notes that the underived ''habeō'' occasionally conveyed the same sense of residing or dwelling in pre-Classical Latin where later usage normally has ''habitō''.<ref>WEBER, CLIFFORD. "Three Notes on Habeo and Ac in the "Itinerarium Egeriae"." Illinois Classical Studies 10, no. 2 (1985): 285-94. Accessed March 6, 2021. http://www.jstor.org/stable/23062550.</ref> The frequentative usually implies habit or frequent repetition, which is natural given the meaning of the word.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|habitō}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-habito.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+.poet-sync-perf|habitō}}

# to [[reside]], [[inhabit]], [[remain]], [[dwell]], [[live]]
#: {{syn|la|possideō|obsideō|resideō|cōnsīdō|subsīdō|incolō|colō|stabulō|iaceō|vīvō|versō}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[linger]]
#: {{syn|la|moror|cōnsistō}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+.poet-sync-perf|habitō}}

====Derived terms====
{{der4|la|habitābilis|habitāculum|habitantēs|habitātiō|habitātiuncula|habitātor|habitātrix|exhabitō|inhabitō}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|la|habeō}}

