An [[Itala]] and [[Vulgate]] morphological {{calque|la|grc|-|nocap=1}} to mirror the proportion ''(κατα-)''{{m|grc|κληρονομέω|κληρονομέω}} :: {{m|grc|κληρονομίᾱ}}. Like the Greek verb itself, sometimes translating {{cog|he|יָרַשׁ|t=to drive out, occupy, rob, inherit, expel, ruin}}. Formally {{af|la|hērēditās|alt1=hērēditās, hērēditātem|-tō}}, with [[haplology]] for the expected *-itātitō.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|hērēditō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|hērēditō}} {{tlb|la|Late Latin|Medieval Latin}}

# {{lb|la|translationese|solecism}} to [[drive out]], [[destroy]]
#: {{syn|la|expellō|dēleō}}
## to [[take away]], [[rob]]
#: {{syn|la|auferō}}
# {{lb|la|translationese}} to be an [[heir]]
#: {{syn|la|[[hērēs]] sum}}
# {{lb|la|translationese}} to [[inherit]], to [[heir]]
#* {{Q|la|Augustine|Enarrationes in Psalmos 110-118|quote=Nōnnūllī ūnō verbō volentēs dīcere, quod ūnō verbō in Graecō positum est, '''hērēditāvī''' interpretātī sunt. Quod etsī Latīnum esse posset, magis significāret eum, quī dedit hērēditatem, quam eum, quī accēpit; ut sīc esset 'hērēditāvi', quōmodo 'dītāvī'. Melius ergō duōbus verbīs īnsinuātur integer sēnsus{{...}}|t=Some have translated this as ''''hereditavi'''' since in the Greek version it's a single word. Although this could be a Latin word, it would rather mean the person who gave the inheritance than the person who received it, so 'hereditavi' "I made heir" like 'ditavi' "I enriched". Therefore it's best to use two words so that the correct meaning is expressed{{...}}}}
#: {{syn|la|hērēditātem [[adeō]], [[capiō]], [[possideō]]|hērēditāte [[accipiō]], [[acquīrō]]}}
# to make [[heir]]
# to give [[inheritance]]

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+|hērēditō}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|inhērēditō}}

