Page 2996 horior: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*ǵʰer-|id=yearn}}
From {{der|la|itc-pro|*herjō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*ǵʰer-}}. Cognate with {{cog|grc|χαίρω}}. The active was lost.

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|horior}}

# {{lb|la|archaic|hapax}} to [[encourage]], [[urge]]

====Conjugation====
The only attested form of this verb is {{m|la|horitur}}, appearing once in Ennius:
* {{Q|la|Ennius|Annales|432|quote={{...}}prandere iubet '''horitur'''que|trans=he orders and encourages them to break their fast}}
Assuming that this fragmentary verse appears at the end of a line of dactylic hexameter, the vowel in {{m|la||horitur}} would be short, making ''horior'' a third-conjugation verb. This conjugation is listed in TLL, De Vaan and Lewis and Short.
{{la-conj|3|horior}}

On the other hand, Gaffiot gives it as a fourth-conjugation verb, perhaps on the basis of the archaic frequentative {{m|la|horitō}} (< {{m|la||*horitus}}), also attested in Ennius.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|hortor}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
* {{R:itc:EDL}}
* {{R:TLL|lemma=46935}}
-------- end text --------
