===Etymology===
From {{affix|la|iaciō|t1=throw|-tō}} (frequentative suffix). See {{m|la|iactus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|iactō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+.p3inf|iactō}}

# to [[throw]], [[cast]], [[hurl]]
#: {{syn|la|coniciō|iniciō|adiciō|obiciō|abiciō|permittō|iaciō|trāiciō|iaculor|impingō|ēmittō|mittō|lībrō}}
# to [[scatter]], [[toss]]
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[disturb]], [[disquiet]], [[torment]], [[agitate]], [[shock]]
#: {{syn|la|cieo|perpello|molior|instigo|instinguo|agito|turbo|ango}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Tristia|1|41-42|quote=carmina sēcessum scrībentis et ōtia quaerunt;<br/>mē mare, mē ventī, mē fera '''iactat''' hiems|trans=''Verses require of [their] writing [both] solitude and leisure;<br/>the sea, winds, [and] a savage winter '''disturb''' me.''<br/>(The poet, sailing to exile during wintertime, is disturbed both physically and mentally.)}}
# to [[utter]], [[speak]], [[throw out]]
# to hurl [[insult]]s
# to be [[officious]] or [[active]] in, give oneself up to, [[devote]] oneself to a thing
#: {{syn|la|versō|exerceō|operor}}
# to [[boast]], act [[conceitedly]], be [[officious]], [[show]] [[off]], [[display]], [[parade]], [[throw]] one’s [[weight]] [[around]], [[make]] oneself [[conspicuous]], [[flaunt]] oneself
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|139-141|quote=“Tenet ille immānia saxa,<br/>vestrās, Eure, domōs; illā sē '''iactet''' in aulā<br/>Aeolus, et clausō ventōrum carcere rēgnet.”|trans=''“He controls those monstrous rocks, [which are] your abode, Eurus; let Aeolus '''flaunt''' himself in that palace, and rule in the locked prison of the winds.”''<br/>({{w|Neptune (mythology)}} dismisses {{w|Eurus}} and the other winds with a [[derogatory]] warning for their king, {{w|Aeolus (son of Hippotes)}}.)}}
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin}} to [[invest]]

