Page 3015 ico: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|la|ī̆ciō}}

===Etymology===
From the same {{der|la|ine-pro|-}} root as {{cog|grc|αἰχμή|t=point of a spear}} and {{m|grc|ἴξ|t=kind of worm}}.<ref>{{R:la:WH|head=alucita|page=33|vol=I}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|ī̆cō}}<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=B64AAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA66 ''Appendix to Bennett's Latin Grammar''], Charles Edwin Bennett, 1895, page 66</ref>

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|ī̆cō|īc|ict}}

# to [[hit]], [[strike]] or [[smite]]
#: {{syn|la|pellō|feriō|discutiō|percellō|percutiō|tangō|pulsō|afflīgō|verberō}}
# to [[stab]] or [[sting]]
# to [[make]] a [[treaty]]
#: {{syn|la|pacīscor|percutiō|pangō|feriō}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|foedus}} '''icio'''|I make a treaty}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Titus Livius|Titus Livius]]|[[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]]|I, 1|quote=Inde foedus '''ictum''' inter duces|t=Then the commanders '''made a treaty''' (literally: Then a '''treaty being made''' between the commanders)}}

====Usage notes====
Forms built on the present stem are rare in classical prose; synonymous verbs like {{m|la|feriō}} are usually found in their place. The first-person singular present active indicative is unattested and so it is unknown whether it was {{m|la||ī̆cō}} or {{m|la|ī̆ciō}}.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|ī̆cō|īc|ict}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|ictus}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
* {{R:M&A}}
* [https://www.dizionario-latino.com/dizionario-latino-italiano.php?lemma=ICIO100 Dizionario Latino-Italiano]
<references/>

