Page 3023 ignosco: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{prefix|la|in-|t1=after|id1=after|nōscō|t2=to get to know|alt2=(g)nōscō}}. The meaning developed from “subsequently realise” to “acknowledge” to “forgive”.<ref>{{R:ine:LIPP|vol=2|pages=242}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|ignōscō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|ignōscō|ignōv|ignōt}}

# to [[forgive]], [[pardon]], [[excuse]], [[overlook]] (with [[Appendix:Glossary#dative case|dative]])
#: {{syn|la|parcō|remittō|āmittō|dōnō|dīmittō|perdōnō|condōnō}}
#: {{uxi|la|ut sibi '''ignosceret'''|in order that he would '''pardon''' them}} ([[w:Caesar|Caesar]], [[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]], VII, 12)
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|6|443-444|quote=haurit aquās tollēnsque manūs, ‘'''ignōscite''',’ dīxit<br/>‘sacra! vir intrābō nōn adeundā virō.’|trans=''He draws water, and lifting up his hands, says: “'''Forgive''' [me], sacred things! A man, I shall enter [a place which] ought not be approached by a man.”''<br/>(The {{w|Temple of Vesta}} afire, {{w|Lucius Caecilius Metellus (consul 251 BC)}} begs forgiveness from {{w|Vesta (mythology)}} prior to saving items from the flames.)}}

====Usage notes====
Takes the dative form of the person forgiven; in archaic Latin, can take the accusative.

====Conjugation====
Passive forms are not known except for the third-person singular.
{{la-conj|3|ignōscō|ignōv|ignōt}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|it|ignoscere}} (archaic)
* {{desc|es|iñocer}} (archaic)

===References===
<references/>

===Further reading===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
