Page 3131 incido: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From {{affix|la|in-|id1=in|t1=into, to|cadō|gloss2=to fall, fall down, drop}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA|incidō}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|3.nopass.supfutractvonly|incidō|incid|incās}}

# {{lb|la|literally}}
## {{q|in general}} {{+preo|la|in|acc}} {{+obj|la|dat}} to [[fall]] or [[drop]] into or upon something
## {{q|in particular}} {{+preo|la|in|acc}} {{+preo|la|inter}} {{+obj|la|dat}} to [[fall upon]], [[meet]], [[come upon]] ([[by chance]]), [[happen]] on, [[fall in with]] a [[person]] or [[thing]]
## {{lb|la|transferred sense}} to [[fall upon]], [[attack]], [[assault]]
##: {{uxi|la|'''incidere''' in hostes|to '''fall upon''' the enemy}}
# {{lb|la|figurative}} {{+preo|la|in|acc}} {{+obj|la|dat}}
## {{q|in general}} to [[fall into]] any [[condition]]
##: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|manibus}} (or {{l|la|in}} {{l|la|manus}}) '''incidere'''|to '''fall under''' the influence (or authority) of someone}}
## to [[fall upon]], [[arise]], [[occur]]; to [[happen]] to, [[befall]]
## {{q|in particular}}
### to [[fall upon]] [[accidentally]]; to [[crop up]], [[fall upon]], [[light upon]] in [[thought]] or [[conversation]]
### to [[come]] or [[occur]] to one's [[mind]]
###: {{uxi|la|casu in alicuius mentionem '''incidere'''|to '''come to''' one’s mind by chance}}
### to [[fall upon]], [[happen]] in a certain [[time]]
###* {{Q|la|[[w:Livy|Livius]]|[[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]]|I, 43|quote={{l|la|quod}} {{l|la|raro}} '''incidebat'''|t=which rarely happened}}

=====Usage notes=====
In [[Late Latin]], the prepositional use is sometimes replaced with a bare accusative.

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|3.nopass.supfutractvonly|incidō|incid|incās}}

