# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[cause]] to [[lean]], [[bend]], [[tilt]], [[incline]], [[turn]]; bend down, [[bow]] something; [[divert]]; [[swerve]]
#: {{syn|la|dēclīnō}}
## {{lb|la|transitive|figuratively|followed by {{l|la|ad}}}} to turn (someone's attention) towards
##* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab Urbe Condita|26|1|quote=Ea tum cura maxime intentos habebat Romanos, non ab ira tantum, quae in nullam unquam ciuitatem iustior fuit, quam quod urbs tam nobilis ac potens, sicut defectione sua traxerat aliquot populos, ita recepta '''inclinatura''' rursus animos uidebatur ad ueteris imperii respectum.|year=27-25 BCE|trans=This concern in particular troubled the mindful Romans at the time, not so much because of anger, which has never been more justified against any other city, rather because a city so noble and powerful, in the same way that it had attracted the support of a number of communities by its revolt, was thought would again '''turn''' attention back towards respect for the previous government once recaptured.}}
# {{lb|la|transitive|military}} to cause to [[fall back]] or [[give way]], [[drive]] back
# {{lb|la|transitive|of disease}} to [[abate]], [[diminish]]
# {{lb|la|transitive|figuratively}} to [[change]], [[alter]]; [[abase]], cause to [[decline]], bring down
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[sink]], [[droop]], turn, incline, decline, bend
#: {{syn|la|senēscō|prōlābor}}
# {{lb|la|intransitive|military}} to [[yield]], [[retreat]], give way
#: {{syn|la|dēcēdō|discēdō|dēficiō|concēdō|cēdō|excēdō|regredior|recipiō|referō|subtrahō|subdūcō|recēdō|āmoveō|vertō|facessō}}
#: {{ant|la|prōgredior|prōdeō|prōcēdō|prōficiō|aggredior|ēvehō|incēdō|accēdō|adeō}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Titus Livius|Titus Livius]]|[[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]]|I, 12|quote={{l|la|Romana}} '''inclinatur''' {{l|la|acies}} [...] {{l|la|ad}} {{l|la|veterem}} {{l|la|portam}} {{l|la|Palatii}}|t=The Roman line '''gives way''' and fled towards the old gate of the Palatine hill}}
# {{lb|la|intransitive|figuratively}} to be [[favorably]] [[disposed]] towards something, incline to

