Page 3290 inodio: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{af|la|in-|odium|t2=hate|id1=in|-ō|id3=denominative|pos3=suffix forming verbs from nouns|alt3=-ō, -āre}}, likely based on such expressions as {{l|la|in}} {{l|la|odiō}} {{l|la|esse}}<ref>{{R:fr:TLFi|ennuyer|ennuyer}}</ref> 'to be loathed'  (lit. 'to be in loathing'). Alternatively from {{af|la|in-|*odiō|t2=to hate|id1=in|alt2=*odiō, *odiāre}}.

Attested in Imperial times and in the late eighth century in the Reichenau Glossary, where it is spelled phonetically as ⟨anoget⟩, etymologically ''inodiat''. The word {{m|la|inodiātus}}, attested without context in the ''Commentarii notarum tironianarum,'' starts with the unrelated negative prefix {{m|la|in-|t=un-|id=not}} per Lewis and Short and Gaffiot. 

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Proto-Romance}} {{IPA|la|/ɪˈnɔdʲo/}}
* {{a|Proto-Western-Romance}} {{IPA|la|/eˈnɔjjo/}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|inodiō}}

# {{lb|la|non-standard}} to [[irritate]], [[bother]], [[cause]] to [[hate]]
#* {{Q|la|1st–3rd c. CE, Carthage|CIL VIII.13134|quote={{smc|...marito '''inodiari''' potvi...}}}}
#* {{Q|la|Late 8th c. CE| |Reichenau Glossary|quote=tedet '''anoget'''|t='t[a]edet' [means] ''''is bothersome''''}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+|inodiō}}

