Page 3313 inquam: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*sekʷ-|id=say}}
From {{inh|la|itc-ola|īnseque}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*h₁enskʷet}}, {{m|ine-pro|*h₁en-}}prefixed form of the thematic aorist of {{der|la|ine-pro|*sekʷ-||to say}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|inquam|eccl=1}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|irreg|inquam}}

# to {{l|en|say}}
#: {{syn|la|aio|for|dīcō|loquor|alloquor|effor|oro}}
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Captivi|3|4|572|thru=573|quote=Tun' te Philocratem esse ais? — Ego, '''inquam'''.|trans=So, you claim to be Philocrates, you say? — I certainly '''say''' that.}}
#* {{Q|la|Julius Caesar|Bello Gallico|7|20|quote="Haec ut intellegatis," '''inquit''', "a me sincere pronuntiari, audite Romanos milites"|t="In order that you may be assured," he '''said''', "that I sincerely announce these things, listen to the Roman soldiers!"}}

====Usage notes====
* Used to introduce a quotation, platitude, or logical argument.

====Conjugation====
This verb is largely conjugated as if it were ''*inquiō'' (3rd conjugation) in present tenses and ''*inquō'' (also 3rd conjugation) in perfect tenses except 1st person singular. However, there is an unusual first person singular present form ''inquam'' (cf. the athematic verb {{m|la|sum}}). This form was derived from ''*h₁enskʷom'', the 1st person singular indicative form of {{cog|ine-pro|*h₁enskʷet}} (an aorist verb).
{{la-conj|irreg|inquam}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|scn|nca}} {{qual|interjection}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
-------- end text --------
