===Etymology===
From {{affix|la|in-|id1=after|t1=after|stō|t2=to stand}}. The meaning developed from “stand behind” to “pursue, be imminent” to “press, urge”.<ref>{{R:ine:LIPP|vol=2|pages=243}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|īnstō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1.pass-3only.sup-futr-actv-only|īnstō|īnstit|īnstāt}}

# {{lb|la|ambitransitive}} to [[stand]] upon, [[set foot]] on
# to be [[nearby]], [[close to]], be [[imminent]]
#: {{syn|la|subsum|astō|immineo|stō|contingo|praesēns}}
#: {{ant|la|dissideō|distō|abstō}}
# {{lb|la|ambitransitive|with [[Appendix:Glossary#accusative case|accusative]] or [[Appendix:Glossary#dative case|dative]]}} to [[assail]], [[press]] upon, [[harass]]
#: {{syn|la|invādō|oppugnō|incurrō|impetō|incessō|aggredior|excurrō|concurrō|occurrō|petō|inruō|accēdō|intrō|incēdō|incidō|irrumpō|adorior|adeō|opprimō|accurrō|appetō|arripiō|assiliō|invehō|lacessō}}
#: {{ant|la|repugnō|resistō|adversor|obversor|obstō|sistō}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Titus Livius|Titus Livius]]|[[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]]|I, 37|quote='''instantes''' {{l|la|cedentibus}}|t=while '''pressing''' the retreating (soldiers)}}
# {{lb|la|intransitive|with dative}} to [[urge]], [[pursue]], [[insist]], to [[be]] [[intent]] [[upon]]
#: {{syn|la|exsequor|persequor|sequor|cōnsequor|premō|agō|apīscor}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to urge (forward), [[ply]]
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to be [[pressing]], [[draw]] [[nigh]], [[approach]], [[threaten]]
#: {{syn|la|immineō}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1.pass-3only.sup-futr-actv-only|īnstō|īnstit|īnstāt}}

