-------- end text --------
Page 3574 labor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|la|itc-ola|labōs}}, of {{unc|la|nocap=1}} origin. Perhaps from a {{der|la|ine-pro|-}} root {{m|ine-pro|*lebʰ-||to seize, take}}, whence {{cog|sa|लभते||take, gain}} and {{cog|lt|lõbis||wealth}}. More commonly connected with {{m|la|labō||I totter}}<ref>{{R:es:Roberts:2014}}</ref> (see Etymology 2 below), but this is rejected by de Vaan, who however provides no alternative.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=labor|page=320}}</ref> The semantic connection is weak in either case.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|la|labōs}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA}}

====Noun====
{{la-noun|labor<3>}}

# {{senseid|la|work}}[[work]]
# {{l|en|labor}}, [[toil]], [[exertion]]
#: {{syn|la|cōnātus|studium|opus|opera|cūra|intēnsiō|mōlēs|pulvis}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|4|641-642|quote=rege Numa, frūctū nōn respondente '''labōrī''',<br/>inrita dēceptī vōta colentīs erant|trans=''When Numa was king, the produce not responding '''to the labor''',<br/>prayers were ineffective, the farmer deceived''}}
# {{lb|la|mostly|_|poetic}} [[hardship]], [[trouble]], [[fatigue]], [[suffering]], [[drudgery]], [[distress]]
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Caesar|Caesar]]|[[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]]|VII, 28|quote='''Labore''' operis incitati|t=Incited '''by the fatigue''' of the work}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|6|384-385|quote=et mediae tempora noctis erant, iam ducibus somnum dederat '''labor'''|trans=''It was midnight, and by now their '''fatigue''' had given the leaders sleep.''}}
# [[illness]]
#: {{syn|la|aegritūdō|morbus|malum|pestis|incommodum|valētūdō|infirmitas}}
#: {{ant|la|salūs|valētūdō}}
# the pain accompanying childbirth, {{l|en|labor}}

