Page 3637 libet: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|la|itc-pro|*luβēō|*luβēt}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*lewbʰ-}}. Cognate with {{cog|en|love}}, {{cog|de|lieben}}, {{m|de|Liebe}}, {{cog|ru|любить}}, {{cog|sa|लुभ्यति}}.

The unrounding of [u] to [i] is a regular sound change between /l/ and a labial consonant; see also {{m|la|līber||free}}, {{m|la|liber||book}}, and {{m|la|clipeus}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|la|lubet}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA|eccl=yes}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|irreg|libet}}

# {{lb|la|with dative}} to be [[pleasing]]; to be [[agreeable]]
#* '''254-184 {{B.C.E.}}''', [[w:Plautus|Plautus]], ''[[w:Asinaria|Asinaria]]''
#*: ''Dīc quod '''libet'''.'' — "Say what you will." (''literally'': "Say what '''is pleasing'''.")

=====Usage notes=====
Designates pleasure in something desired, while {{m|la|placeō}} in something recognised as right.

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|irreg|libet}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|la|libēns}}
* {{l|la|libenter}}
* {{l|la|quīlibet}}
* {{l|la|quōlibet}}

