Page 3689 lugeo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Possibly from {{der|la|ine-pro|*lewǵ-||to break, injure}} with a semantic shift to “be in pain” in the stative. Cognate with {{cog|grc|λευγαλέος}}, {{m|grc|λυγρός}}, {{cog|sa|रुजति||to break open, shatter, injure, cause pain}}, {{cog|lv|lauzt||to break, fracture}}. Alternatively from {{m+|ine-pro|*(s)lewg-||to gulp, swallow, gasp}}; compare {{cog|sga|loingid||to eat}} and {{cog|grc|λύζω||to hiccup; to sob}}, {{m|grc|λυγγανώμενον||sobbing}}, {{m|grc|λυγμός||the hiccups}}.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=lūgeō|page=351}}</ref> In either case it may be influenced by {{onomatopoeic|la|title=onomatopoeia}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|lūgeō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2|lūgeō|lūx|lūct}}

# to [[mourn]], [[lament]], [[bewail]], [[deplore]]
#: {{syn|la|dēplōrō|queror|conqueror|ingemō|gemō|plangō|plōrō|fleō}}
#: {{ux|la|Beati, qui '''lugent''': quoniam ipsi consolabuntur.|t=Blessed are they who '''mourn''', for they shall be comforted. — ''Vulgate'', Mt 5, 5}}

====Conjugation====
* Please note that there is a disagreement over whether or not there is a macron on the third and fourth principal parts for the first syllable and for the subsequent verb forms from these ({{m|la||lūxī}} for {{m|la||luxī}} and {{m|la||lūctum}} for {{m|la||luctum}}).
{{la-conj|2|lūgeō|lūx|lūct}}

