Page 3705 machino: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{af|la|māchina|t1=device, mill|-ō|id2=denominative|pos2=verb-forming suffix}}. Parallel formation to the Classical Latin verb {{m|la|māchinor||plot, scheme}}, but never found in a deponent form or with that sense. First attested in the 6th century CE.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Proto-Romance}} {{IPA|la|/ˈmakɪno/}}
* {{a|Proto-Italo-Western-Romance}} {{IPA|la|/ˈmaceno/}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|māchinō}} {{tlb|la|LL.}}

# {{lb|la|non-standard}} to [[mill]], [[grind]]
#: {{syn|la|molō<qq:Classical>}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Ravenna (disambiguation)|Ravenna]]|Translation of [[w:Rufus of Ephesus|Rufus']] De Podagra|year=6th–7th c. CE|quote=...et orobu id est heruum '''muccinatum''' [''sic''] et tricoscinatum...|trans=...and ''orobos'', that is ''eruum'' [cultivated vetch], '''ground''' and sieved...}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Ravenna (disambiguation)|Ravenna]]|Translation of [[w:Oribasius|Oribasius']] Synopsis|year=6th c. CE|quote=Oportet autem antequam '''macenetur''' fricare leviter apud lenteum.|trans=But before '''it is ground up''' it should be gently rubbed with a cloth.}}
#* {{Q|la|''Itinerarium Antonini Placentini''|year=6th c. CE|quote=asellum qui illis '''macinabat'''|trans=...the donkey, which '''was turning the millstone''' for them...}}

