Page 3730 manduco: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|mandūcō}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{af|la|mandūcus|t1=glutton|-ō|id2=denominative|pos2=verb-forming suffix}}, from {{m|la|mandō|t=chew, eat, devour|id=chew}}. The noun {{m|la|mandūcus}} developed the specialized sense "masked figure with champing jaws".

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1+|mandūcō}}

# {{lb|la|Classical Latin|deponent|_|in|_|Old Latin lemma}} to [[chew]], [[gnaw]] on, [[masticate]]
#* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 100 {{BCE}}''', [[w:Lucius Afranius (poet)|Afranius]], ''Fratriae'' (fragment XVII) in ''Scaenicae Romanorum Poesis Fragmenta'' (volume II), [[w:Otto Ribbeck|Otto Ribbeck]] (editor), ''[[w:Bibliotheca Teubneriana|Bibliotheca Teubneriana]]'', [[w:Leipzig|Leipzig]] 1852, [https://archive.org/stream/bub_gb_zi8OAAAAYAAJ#page/n179/mode/2up page 159]:
#*: {{...}}facile '''mandūcārī''' quī potest.
#*:: {{...}}whoever can '''chew on''' it easily.
#* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 45 {{BCE}}''', [[w:Marcus Terentius Varro|Varro]], ''De lingua Latina'' [[s:la:De Lingua Latina (Varro, Schoppe)/Liber septimus|7.95]]:
#*: Dictum ''mandier'' ā ''mandendō'', unde '''''mandūcārī'''''{{...}}
#*:: Here ''mandier'' comes from {{l|la|mandō}}, whence also comes '''''mandūcārī'''''{{...}}
#* '''63 {{CE}} – 65 {{CE}}''', [[w:Seneca the Younger|Seneca the Younger]], ''[[w:Epistulae morales ad Lucilium|Epistulae morales ad Lucilium]]'' [[s:la:Epistulae morales ad Lucilium/Liber XIV - XV#XCV. SENECA LVCILIO SVO SALVTEM|95.27]]:
#*: In cēnā fit quod fierī dēbēbat in ventre: expectō iam ut '''mandūcāta''' pōnantur.
#*:: What should be done in my stomach is now done on the table: already I expect '''chewed things''' to be placed there.
# {{lb|la|Late Latin|colloquial|_|in|_|Classical Latin}} to [[eat]]
#* {{Q|la|Suetonius|De vita Caesarum|2|76|2|quote=Verba ipsīus ex epistulīs sunt: {{...}}Nē Iūdaeus quidem, mī Tiberī, tam dīligenter sabbatīs ieiunium servat quam ego hodiē servāvī, quī in balineō dēmum post hōram prīmam noctis duās buccās '''mandūcāvī''' prius quam unguī inciperem.|t=His [&zwnj;[[w:Augustus|Augustus’s]]] own words from his letters are: {{...}}Not even a Jew, my dear Tiberius, does the Saturday fasting as I did today, now that, at the baths, I finally '''ate''' a couple snacks at the first hour of the night, before starting with the cleaning oil.}}
#* {{Q|la|Petronius|Satyricon|56|4|quote=Nam mūtae bēstiae labōriōsissimae bovēs et ovēs: bovēs, quōrum beneficiō pānem '''mandūcāmus'''; ovēs, quod lānā illae nōs glōriōsōs faciunt.|t=Oxen and sheep are quiet beasts that work exceedingly well: we '''eat''' bread as a benefit from oxen, and sheep make us look glorious with their wool.}}
#* '''4th C. {{CE|nodots=yes}}''', [[w:Saint Jerome|Saint Jerome]], ''[[w:Vulgate|Vulgate]]'', [[w:Gospel of Mark|Mark]] 14:22:
#*: {{quote|la|Et '''mandūcantibus''' illīs, accēpit Iēsūs pānem, et benedīcēns frēgit, et dedit eīs, et ait, “Sūmite; hoc est corpus meum”.
|translation=And as they '''were eating''', Jesus took some bread, and broke it while blessing it, and gave it to them saying, “Take it; this is my body”.}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|1+|mandūcō}}

