Page 3784 meto: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*meteō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₂met-||to mow, reap}}, enlargement of {{m|ine-pro|*h₂meh₁-}}. The perfect {{m|la|messuī}} for the expected {{m|la||*messī}} is analogous to other perfects in ''-ui''.

Cognate with {{cog|cy|medi||to reap}}, {{cog|grc|ἀμάω|t=to reap corn}} and {{m|grc|ἄμητος||harvest}}, {{cog|lt|mèsti}} and {{m|lt|métyti||to throw}}, {{cog|ru|мести́||to sweep}} and {{m|ru|метáть||to throw; pile up hay}}, {{cog|en|mow}} and {{m|en|meadow}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|metō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|metō|messu|mess}}

# to [[reap]], [[harvest]]
#: {{syn|la|dēsecō}}
# to [[cut]], [[crop]] or [[snip]] off
# to [[cut]] [[through]], [[sever]]
# to [[mow]] down, [[cut]] down (in battle)
#* [[w:Tertullian|Tertullianus]], Apologeticus, 50.13
#*: Plūrēs efficimur, quotiēs '''metimur''' ā vōbīs; sēmen est sanguis chrīstiānōrum.
#*:: We multiply whenever '''we are cut down''' by you; the blood of Christians is seed.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|metō|messu|mess}}

====Derived terms====
{{col4|la
|dēmetō
|ēmetō
|messis
|messiō
|messor
}}

====Descendants====
* Italo-Romance:
** {{desc|it|mietere}}
** {{desc|nap|méte}}
** {{desc|scn|mètiri}}
* Padanian:
** {{desc|egl|mèder}}
** {{desc|lmo|meder}}
** {{desc|pms|meje}}
* Northern Gallo-Romance:
** {{desc|frp|miére}}
* Southern Gallo-Romance:
** {{desc|oc-auv|medre}}

