Page 3851 mulceo: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|la|mulcō||pcl}}
* {{alter|la|mulciō||pcl|vul}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*molk-eje-|t=to rub, stroke}}, {{m|itc-pro|*molk-ā-}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*m(o)lḱ-eie-|t=to touch repeatedly}}, see also {{cog|sa|मृक्षति|t=to stroke, smear, rub}}.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|mulceo|392-93}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|mulceō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2|mulceō|muls|muls/mulct}} {{tlb|la|poetic|in|_|Late Latin|_|prose}}

# to [[stroke]], [[graze]], [[touch]] [[lightly]] or [[gently]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|8|630|thru=634|quote=Fēcerat et viridī fētam Māvortis in antrō<br>prōcubuisse lupam, geminōs huic ūbera circum<br>lūdere pendentīs puerōs et lambere mātrem<br>impavidōs, illam teretī cervīce reflexā<br>'''mulcēre''' alternōs et corpora fingere linguā.|trans=He also made the she-wolf, daughter of Mars, in a green cave<br>to have laid down, around her teats twin<br>boys to play hanging and to lick the mother<br>fearless, her to, with shapely neck bent back,<br>'''stroke''' them by turn and shape their bodies with the tongue.}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Metamorphoses|1|107|thru=108|quote=Vēr erat aeternum, placidīque tepentibus aurīs<br>'''mulcēbant''' zephyrī nātōs sine sēmine flōrēs.|trans=The spring was everlasting, and, with warm breezes, the pleasant<br>zephyrs '''touched''' the flowers born without seeds.}}
## {{lb|la|medicine}} to [[rub]], [[apply]] an ointment
##*  '''2th c. {{CE}}''', [[w:Serenus Sammonicus|Quintus Serenus Sammonicus]], ''Liber Medicīnālis'' [http://cmg.bbaw.de/epubl/online/cml_02_03.php 280–281]:
##*: {{quote|la|At sī cervīcēs dūrātaque cōla rigēbunt,<br>(mīra loquor) geminus '''mulcēbitur''' unguine poples.
|translation=But if the nape and the hardened neck shall be stiff,<br>(wondrous things I'm saying) the twin knee shall be '''rubbed''' with ointment.}}
# {{label|la|very|rare}} to gently [[move]] something
#* {{Q|la|Ennius|Annales|257|quote='''mulserat''' hūc nāvem compulsam flūctibŭs pontus|trans=The sea '''had gently moved''' the ship stricken by waves hither.}}
# to [[soften]], [[relax]]
#* '''348 {{CE}} – ''[[circa|c.]]'' 410 {{CE}}''', [[w:Prudentius|Prudentius]], ''[[w:Psychomachia|Psȳchomachia]]'' 330–331:
#*: {{...}}et male dulcis odor domat ōra et pectora et arma<br>ferrātōsque torōs oblīsō rōbore '''mulcet'''.
#*:: {{...}}and the fatally sweet smell subdues the mouths and chests and weapons<br>and the iron-clad muscles with crushing strength '''softens'''.
# to [[make]] [[sweet]] or [[pleasant]]
#* '''5th c. {{CE}}''', [[w:Rutilius Namatianus|Rutilius Namatianus]], ''Dē reditū suō'' [[s:la:De reditu suo|113–114]]:
#*: {{quote|la|Vēre tuō numquam '''mulcērī''' dēsinit annus;<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;dēliciāsque tuās victa tuētur hiemps.<br>During your [Rome's] spring never does the year cease to '''be made pleasant''';<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;and the conquered winter respects your charms.}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[soothe]], [[soften]], [[appease]], [[placate]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Georgicon|4|507|thru=510|quote=Septem illum tōtōs perhibent ex ordine mēnsīs<br>rūpe sub āeriā dēsertī ad Strȳmonis undam<br>flēvisse, et gelidīs haec ēvolvisse sub antrīs<br>'''mulcentem''' tigrīs et agentem carmine quercūs:{{...}}|trans=Seven whole months they say of him [&zwnj;[[w:Orpheus|Orpheus]]&zwnj;],<br>under the tall crag, beside the wave of the deserted Strymon<br>to have wept, and to have said this under the icy caves,<br>'''softening''' tigers and moving oaks with song:{{...}}}}
#* {{Q|la|Seneca the Younger|Epistulae morales ad Lucilium|7|63|3|quote=Nōn differō in longius tempus, quō dēsīderium omne '''mulcētur''', quō etiam ācerrimī lūctūs residunt.|trans=I'm not putting off to any more distant time when every regret shall be '''soothed''', when even the most intense griefs shall quieten.}}
#* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 83 {{CE}} – 96 {{CE}}''', [[w:Silius Italicus|Silius Italicus]], ''[[w:Punica (poem)|Pūnica]]'' 17.46–47:
#*: Extemplō maior cūnctīs spēs pectora '''mulcet'''<br>fīnem armīs tandem fīnemque venīre periclīs.
#*:: A greater hope immediately '''soothes''' the breasts of all<br>that an end to weapons and an end to dangers may indeed come.
## {{lb|la|medicine}} to [[help]], [[alleviate]]
##* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|5|499|thru=504|quote=Ingemuit Chīrōn trāxitque ē corpore ferrum:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;adgemit Alcīdēs Haemoniusque puer.<br>Ipse tamen lēctās Pagasaeīs collibus herbās<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;temperat et variā volnera '''mulcet''' ope:<br>vīrus edāx superābat opem, penitusque recepta<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ossibus et tōtō corpore pestis erat.|trans=[[w:Chiron|Chiron]] groaned and drew the iron out of the body:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[[w:Heracles|Alcides]] and the [[w:Achilles|Haemonian boy]] groans back.<br>He himself herbs gathered from the Pagasaean hills<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;gathers and '''helps''' the wounds with diverse aid:<br>the greedy poison was overcoming the help, and received within<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;to the bones and the entire body was the pestilence.}}
##* {{Q|la|Pliny the Elder|Naturalis Historia|22|50|107|quote=Prīma propolis alvōrum, dē quā dīximus, acūleōs et omnia īnfīxa corporī extrahit, tūbera discutit, dūra concoquit, dolōrēs nervōrum '''mulcet''' ulceraque iam dēspērantia cicatrīcem clūdit.|trans=First, propolis of the hives, of which we've talked about, extracts bee stings and anything embedded in the body, reduces swellings, softens calluses, '''alleviates''' pains of the nerves and closes ulcers already hopeless with a scar.}}
# to [[delight]]
#* {{Q|la|Quintilian|Institutio Oratoria|9|2|4|quote=Quae cum adesse dēbent, tum disponenda atque varianda sunt, ut audītōrem, quod in fidibus fierī vidēmus, omnī sonō '''mulceant'''.|trans=Which, while they need to be present, also are to be arranged and varied, so as to '''delight''' the hearer with all the musicality, as we see with the lyre.}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|2|mulceō|muls|muls}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|soothe}} {{l|la|blandior}}, {{l|la|lēniō}}, {{l|la|plācō}}, {{l|la|sēdō}}
* {{sense|stroke}} {{l|la|palpō}}, {{l|la|percurrō}}

====Derived terms====
{{top2}}
* {{l|la|commulceō}}
* {{l|la|dēmulceō}}
* {{l|la|mulcēdō}}
* {{l|la|permulceō}}
* {{l|la|remulceō}}
{{bottom}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|it|molcere}}
* {{desc|es|mulcer}}

====See also====
* {{l|la|mulcō}}

===References===
