Page 3872 narro: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|naro}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|la|nārō}}, for {{inh|la|itc-pro|*gnārāō||to make known, tell}}, denominal from {{m|la|gnārus}}; or less likely for {{der|la|itc-pro|*gnārurāō}}. Ultimately from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*ǵneh₃-|t=to know}}. The /V:C/~/VCC/ vacillation is similar to, but probably a different phenomenon from, the so-called 'Littera Rule', as in {{m|la|Iūpiter}} > {{m|la|Iuppiter}}.<ref>{{cite-web|en|last=Weiss|first=Michael|work=Observations on the Littera Rule|year=2010|url=https://conf.ling.cornell.edu/weiss/Observations_on_the_littera_rule.pdf}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|narrō|eccl=0}}
* {{la-IPA|nārō|eccl=0|accent=Conservative}}
* {{la-IPA|cl=0}}
* Note: this word exhibits a common vacillation between long vowel and geminate consonant (perhaps associated with stress), still observed in Sardinian and Sicilian.

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+.poet-sync-perf|narrō}}

# to [[tell]], [[say]], [[relate]]
## {{lb|la|with|{{m|la|tibi}}|colloquial}} {{n-g|Used to emphasise that one is speaking in earnest, or to add expressiveness.}}
##* {{Q|la|Cicero|Epistulae ad Atticum|XV|21|1|quote='''Narrō''' tibī̆, Quīntus pater exsultat laetitiā!|t=[[I'm telling you]], Quintus the father is beside himself with joy!}}
# to [[describe]], [[report]], [[recount]]
# {{lb|la|with|{{m|la|dē}}}} to [[talk]] [[about]]
## {{lb|la|with|interrogatives|etc}} to be talking about, to [[mean]]
## {{lb|la|with|{{m|la|bene}}|discourse}} to be telling [[good news]]
##: {{uxi|la|bene '''narrās'''!|that's nice, that's good to hear!}}
## {{lb|la|passive}} to be the subject of [[talk]]
# {{lb|la|rhetoric}} to [[state]] the [[facts]] of a [[case]]

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+.poet-sync-perf|narrō}}

====Quotations====
* {{Q|la|Velius Longus|De Orthographia|80|8|quote=Sānē in eō quod est '''narrāre''' observātum est ut ūnum 'r' scrīberēmus, quoniam venit ā 'gnārō', cui est contrārium 'ignārus'.|t=Granted, in the word 'narrare' the rule is to write a single 'r', seeing as it's derived from 'gnarus' whose antonym is 'ignarus'.|refn=([https://latin.packhum.org/loc/1374/1/15/1708-1724#15  latin.packhum.org])}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|narrate, recount}} {{l|la|nūntiō}}
* {{sense|tell, speak}} {{l|la|dīcō}}, {{l|la|nūntiō}}

====Derived terms====
{{der2|la|ēnarrō|dēnarrō|narrābilis|narrātiō|narrātīvē|narrātīvus|narrātor|narrātum|narrātus|inanārrābilis}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|la|narrātiuncula}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|sc|nàrrere|narri|narai|t3=tell, say}}
