===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*neḱ-}}
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*neḱ-||perish, disappear}}; see also {{m|la|noxius||harmful}}, {{m|la|noceō||I hurt, harm}}, {{m|la|nex||murder, violent death}} (as opposed to {{m|la|mors}}), as well as {{cog|wlm|angheu||death}}, {{cog|br|ankou}}, {{cog|sga|éc}}, {{cog|grc|νέκυς||a dead body}} and {{m|grc|νεκρός||dead}}, {{cog|peo|𐎻𐎴𐎰𐎹𐎫𐎹|ts=vi-nathayatiy||he injures}}, {{cog|ae|𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬌𐬙𐬌||disappears}}, {{m|ae|𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬎|𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬎-|corpse}}, {{cog|sa|नश्यति||to disappear, perish}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|necō|eccl=1}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-neco.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+.poet-sync-perf|necō}}

# to [[kill]], [[murder]] (especially without physical wounding such as by poison or hunger)
#: {{syn|la|ēnecō|occīdō|interimō|cōnficiō|caedō|obtruncō|percutiō|interficiō|trucīdō|perimō|peragō|dēiciō|iugulō|sōpiō|tollō|absūmō|cōnsūmō}}
#: {{uxi|la|aliquem igni '''necare'''|to kill someone by fire (fire burning)}}
#: {{uxi|la|aliquem ferro '''necare'''|to kill someone by sword}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[thwart]], [[check]]
# {{lb|la|Late Latin|Medieval Latin}} {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[drown]]

