Page 3885 necto: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{unc|la}}. Likely related to {{m|la|nōdus||knot}}. The ending may be analogous to that found in {{m|la|plectō|t=plait, weave, braid}}<ref name="Persson">{{cite-book|author=Per Persson|year=1912|title=Beiträge zur indogermanischen Wortforschung|page=815|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/beitrgezurindo02persuoft/page/815/}}</ref> or {{m|la|pectō|t=comb wool}}.<ref name="Oxford Introduction">{{cite-book|title=The Oxford Introduction to Proto-Indo-European and the Proto-Indo-European World|year=2006|author=J. P. Mallory|author2=D. Q. Adams|page=234|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=iNUSDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA234&lpg=PA234}}</ref>

The following roots have been proposed:
* {{der|la|ine-pro|*gned-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*gnod-||to bind}}, making it cognate to {{cog|gem-pro|*knuttô|t=knot}} (whence Modern {{cog|en|knot}}), and possibly {{cog|grc|γνάθος||a jaw}}.
* {{der|la|ine-pro|*ned-|t=to turn, twist, knot}}, whence {{cog|en|net}}, {{cog|ae|𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬐𐬀|𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬐𐬀-|bundle}}, {{cog|sga|nascaid||to bind}}.<ref name="Oxford Introduction"/> This in turn is sometimes interpreted as being connected to {{der|la|ine-pro|*(s)neh₁-|t=spin, sew}}, the root of Latin {{m|la|neō|t=spin, weave}}.<ref name="Persson"/>
* {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₂neḱ-||to reach}}<ref name="De Vaan">{{R:itc:EDL|head=nectō|page=404}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|nectō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|nectō|nex|nex}}

# to [[connect]], [[interweave]], [[attach]], [[unite]]; [[relate]]
#: {{syn|la|cōnectō|iungō|vinciō}}
# to [[bind]], [[tie]], [[fasten]]
#: {{syn|la|cōnectō|colligō|adalligō|cōnfīgō|cōnserō|dēligō|ligō|illigō|alligō|dēfīgō|fīgō|vinculō|dēstinō}}
#: {{ant|la|explicō|absolvō|dissolvō|solvō}}
# to bind by [[obligation]], [[oblige]], make [[liable]]
# to [[contrive]], [[devise]], [[compose]], [[produce]]
#: {{syn|la|dēdūcō}}

====Conjugation====
* The third principal part may be {{m|la|nexī}} or {{m|la|nexuī}}.
{{la-conj|3|nectō|nex|nex}}

====Derived terms====
