Page 3918 nolo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*welh₁-}}
{{univerbation|la|ne-<t:not>|volō<t:want>}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|nōlō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|irreg|nōlō}}

# to be [[unwilling]], to not [[wish]], to not [[want]], to [[refuse]]
#: T. Maccius Plautus, Stichus, or The Parasite Rebuffed
#: {{ux|la|[[abire|Abire]] '''nolumus'''.|t='''We are unwilling''' to [[depart]].|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|la|'''Nollem'''.|t='''I would not'''/'''I could wish not'''.|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|la|'''Nollem''' [[factum]].|t=I am sorry for it.|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|la|[[quod|Quod]] '''nolim'''.|t=Which I must not do (loosely, which Heaven '''forbid''').|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|la|[[non|Non]] '''nolle'''.|t=To have no objection/To be willing.|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|la|Me '''nolente'''.|t=Without my consent/Against my will (literally, '''with''' me '''being unwilling''').|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|la|Alere '''nolunt''' hominem edacem.|t=They '''won't''' keep a greedy man.|inline=1}}

====Usage notes====
* This verb is especially frequent in the [[imperative]] (''[[noli#Latin|nōlī]]'', ''[[nolito#Latin|nōlīto]]'', ''[[nolite#Latin|nōlīte]]'') because its imperative form is used as the negation in a polite form of negative imperative: ''[[noli#Latin|nōlī]]'' / ''[[nolite#Latin|nōlīte]]'' (singular/plural, respectively) + [infinitive], literally "Be unwilling to [ [[bare infinitive]] ]," reflecting the substitution of ''nolle'' + [infinitive] for ''ne'' + [second-person present subjunctive], the latter phrase having the literal meaning of "May you not [ [[bare infinitive]] ]." Examples:
*: '''''Nōlīte''' '''spēluncās ''' '''intrāre''''' (plural)
*:: '''Don't (you people)''' enter the [[cave]]s! (plural)
*: '''''Nōlī''' [[irascor|irasci]].''
*:: '''Don't''' become [[angry]].
*: '''''Nōlī''' [[avorsaror|avorsari]].''
*:: '''Don't''' [[recoil]].
*: '''''Nōlī''' [[putare]].''
*:: '''Don't''' [[think]].
*: '''''Nōlītōte''' [[mirari]].'' (plural)
*:: '''Don't''' be [[surprised]]. (plural)
*: '''''Nōlītō''' [[putare]].''
*:: '''You shall not''' [[think]].
*: '''''Nōlīte''', [[hospes|hospites]], ad [[ego|me]] [[adire]].''
*:: '''Don't''' [[approach]] me, [[strangers]]!
*: '''''Nōlīte''', [[judex|judices]], [[existimare]].''
*:: '''Don't''' [[judge]], [[judge]]s!
*:'''Noli me tangere''' (dicit ei Iesus): nondum enim ascendi ad Patrem meum. Vade autem ad fratres meos et dic eis: ascendo ad Patrem meum et Patrem vestrum et Deum meum et Deum vestrum.
*:: Jesus saith to her: '''Do not''' touch Me: for I am not yet ascended to my Father. But go to My brethren and say to them: I ascend to My Father and to your Father, to My God and to your God. 

* Sometimes the verb ''[[volo]]'' (to [[wish]], to [[want]]) is used [[pleonastically]] with this verb:
* Sometimes followed by a negative, which does not undo the negation.
* The second person forms and third person singular of the present indicative are supplemented with "{{m|la|non}}" + "{{m|la|volo|velle}}". However, in Old Latin ''nevīs'' and ''nevolt'' for ''nōn vīs'' and ''nōn volt'' are attested.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|irreg|nōlō}}

====See also====
* [[w:Noli me tangere|Noli me tangere]] (Do not touch me)
* [[w:List_of_Latin_phrases (full)#N|Noli turbare circulos meos]] (Do not disturb my circles!)
* [[w:List_of_Latin_phrases (full)#N|Nolens, volens]] (Unwilling, willing)
* [[w:List_of_Latin_phrases (full)#N|Nolle prosequi]] (To be unwilling to prosecute)
* [[w:List_of_Latin_phrases (full)#N|Nolo contendere]] (I do not wish to contend)
* {{l|la|nolens volens}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
