Page 3994 obliviscor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{unc|la}}. Perhaps from {{affix|la|ob-}} plus the root of {{m|la|lēvis||smooth}} with ''-ēscor'' (passive inchoative suffix, from [[-esco|-ēscō]]), with ''oblīvīscor'' having an earlier meaning of "I start to erase".

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|oblīvīscor}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|oblīvīscor|oblīt}}

# to [[lose]] [[remembrance]] of, [[forget]] (+ [[Appendix:Glossary#genitive|genitive]] of person or genitive/[[Appendix:Glossary#accusative|accusative]] of person or thing)
#* {{Q|la|Jerome|Vulgate|Proverbs|31|7|quote=bibant ut '''oblīvīscantur''' egestātis suae et dolōris nōn recordentur amplius|trans=''Let them drink, and '''forget''' their want, and remember their sorrow no more.'' (Douay-Rheims trans., Challoner rev.; 1752 CE)}}
# to [[forget]]
#: {{ant|la|memorō|admoneō|referō|retineō|moneō|meminī|redūcō}}
# to [[disregard]], [[omit]], [[neglect]] (+genitive of person or genitive/accusative of person or thing)
#: {{syn|la|dēserō|relinquō|omittō|dēdō|concēdō|dēcēdō|reddō|remittō|dēstituō|dēficiō|cēdō|dissimulō|trādō|addīcō|praetereō|neglegō|pōnō|reddō|remittō|permittō|tribuō}}

====Usage notes====
*Expressing "I am forgotten" requires the subject and object to be reversed with this verb. For example: "She is forgotten by Paul," becomes "Paulus eam/eius oblivīscitur."

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|oblīvīscor|oblīt}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|oblītus}}
* {{l|la|oblīviō}}
* {{l|la|oblīvium}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|la|oblīviālis}}
* {{l|la|oblīviōsus}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|VL.|*oblītāre}} {{see desc}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
-------- end text --------
