{{la-conj|2|oporteō|oportu}}
-------- end text --------
Page 4124 oportet: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*wert-}}
For {{m|la||*opvortet}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*wortéyeti}}, causative stem (2nd conjugation) of the root of {{m|la|vertō||to turn}} (3rd conjugation). Some refer the ''op-'' to {{m|la|ob-}}, some to {{m|la|opus}}, with which compare the similar expressions {{m|la|opus}} {{m|la|est}} and {{m|la|operam}} {{m|la|dare}}.

See also {{m|la|ligō}}, {{m|la|vinciō}} for other examples of words meaning to bind or turn, having derivatives with the sense of obliging.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2.nopass|oportet|oportu}}

# {{lb|la|with accusative}} to be [[necessary]], [[proper]], [[becoming]]; to [[behoove]]
#* oportet nos patriam amare — ''it behoves us to love our country.''
#* non te oportebat illi argentum reddere — ''you ought not to have paid him the money.''
#* {{Q|la|Seneca the Younger|Epistulae morales ad Lucilium|48|2|quote=Alteri vivas '''oportet''', si vis tibi vivere.|trans=You '''must''' live for others if you wish to live for yourself.}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|2.nopass|oportet|oportu}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|la|dēbeō}}
* {{l|la|necesse}} {{l|la|est}}
* {{l|la|opus}} {{l|la|est}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|oporteō}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|io|oportar}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
* [[w:Julius Pokorny|Julius Pokorny]], ''[https://web.archive.org/web/20100523211431/http://dnghu.org/indoeuropean.html Indogermanisches Etymologisches Woerterbuch],'' radical ''*epi''

