Page 4146 orior: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*orjōr}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₃er-||to stir, rise}}. Cognate with {{cog|grc|ὄρνῡμι}}, {{cog|sa|ऋणोति|tr=ṛṇóti}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|orior|pres_actv_inf=orīrī|ort}}

# to [[rise]], [[get up]]
#: {{syn|la|coorior|exorior|oborior|surgō|ēmergō|assurgō}}
#: {{ant|la|concēdō|decēdō|cēdō|intereō|discēdō|excēdō|pereō}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|6|729-730|quote=Iam tua, Lāomedōn, '''oritur''' nurus ortaque noctem pellit|trans=''Now, Laomedon, your daughter-in-law '''is rising''', and having risen, she dispels the night''<br/>(That is to say, the goddess of dawn, the Greek {{w|Eos}} or Roman {{w|Aurora}}; her fabled consorts included {{w|Tithonus}}, son of {{w|Laomedon of Troy}}. See also “[[ortus]]”, the perfect active participle of the deponent verb “orior”.)}}
# to [[appear]], [[arise]], become [[visible]]
#: {{syn|la|appāreō|pāreō}}
# to be [[born]], come to [[exist]], [[originate]]

====Usage notes====
* Part of a small group of verbs, all with a short-vowel root, displaying both 3rd and 4th conjugation forms.
* Present active infinitive only {{m|la|orīrī}}, other 4th conjugation forms occur, particularly in manuscripts.
* Past participle exclusively {{m|la|ortus}}, future participle exclusively {{m|la|oritūrus}}, gerund almost exclusively {{m|la|oriundus}}.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|orior|ort|pres_actv_inf=orīrī|1s_impf_actv_subj=orīrer/orerer|2s_impf_actv_subj=orīrēris/orīrēre/orerēris/orerēre|3s_impf_actv_subj=orīrētur/orerētur|1p_impf_actv_subj=orīrēmur/orerēmur|2p_impf_actv_subj=orīrēminī/orerēminī|3p_impf_actv_subj=orīrentur/orerentur}}

