Page 4165 paeniteo: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|poeniteō|paeniteor||pcl}}
* {{alt|la|paenitīre||Late Latin}}

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*peh₁-}}
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*peh₁-||to hurt}}. See {{m|la|patior}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|paeniteō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2+.pass-impers.sup-futr-actv-only|paeniteō|paenitu|paenit}}

# to cause to [[repent]]
# to [[regret]], [[repent]]; to be [[sorry]]
# {{lb|la|impersonal}} {{lb|la|with accusative of person, genitive of thing or infinitive}} to [[regret]]
#* '''59 {{BCE}}''', [[w:Cicero|Cicero]], ''[[w:Epistulae ad Atticum|Epistulae ad Atticum]]'' [[s:la:Epistulae (Marcus Tullius Cicero)/Epistulae ad Atticum/II|2.23.2]]:
#*: Prīmum igitur illud tē scīre volō, Sampsiceramum, nostrum amīcum, vehementer suī stātūs '''paenitēre''' restituīque in eum locum cupere ex quō dēcidit.
#*:: First then I want you to know that our friend Sampsiceramus [Pompey] '''is''' bitterly '''unhappy''' about his position and longs to get back to where he stood before his fall.
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|6|51-52|quote=Sed neque '''paeniteat''', nec gēns mihi cārior ūlla est:<br/>hīc colar, hīc teneam cum Iove templa meō.|trans=And yet neither '''is there regret''', nor is any other nation dearer to me: here may I be worshipped, here may I occupy the temple with my Jupiter.<br>({{w|Juno (mythology)|Juno}}, after telling of the people and places she could have protected, now says she favors Rome.)}}
#: {{ux|la|Mē '''paenitet''' alicuius reī.|I regret something.}}
#: {{ux|la|Eum errōris suī multum '''paenituit'''.|He regretted his mistake very much.}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|2+.pass-impers.sup-futr-actv-only|paeniteō|paenitu|paenit}}

====Derived terms====
