Page 4166 paenulam alicui scindo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{m|la|paenulam||}}, the accusative singular of {{m|la|paenula||cloak}} + {{m|la|alicui}}, the dative singular of {{m|la|aliquis||someone}} + {{m|la|scindō||I separate by force}}. Literally “I tear apart someone's cloak”.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|paenulam alicuj scindō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|prefix=[[paenula|paenulam]] [[aliquis|alicui]]|passive_prefix=[[paenula]] [[aliquis|alicui]]|3|scindō|scid|sciss}}

# {{lb|la|idiomatic}} to press one strongly to stay
#* '''Letter 13''', [[w:Cicero|Cicero]], ''[[w:Epistulae ad Atticum|Letters to Atticus]]'' {{qualifier|Letters to Atticus}}, [http://www.thelatinlibrary.com/cicero/att13.shtml#33 paragraph 33]
#*: sed ego ita egi ut non '''scinderem paenulam'''.
#*:: And indeed, I have gone so that '''the cloak''' is not '''ripped apart'''. (ie. so that I'm not forced to stay)

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|prefix=[[paenula|paenulam]] [[aliquis|alicui]]|passive_prefix=[[paenula]] [[aliquis|alicui]]|3|scindō|scid|sciss}}
