#* '''[[circa|c.]] 7th C. {{CE|nodots=yes}}''', [[w:Isidore of Seville|Saint Isidore]] (traditional attribution), Vulcanius and [[w:Julius Caesar Scaliger|Julius Caesar Scaliger]] (compilators), ''Glossae Scaligeri'' in ''Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum'' (volume V), [[w:Georg Goetz|Georg Goetz]], [[w:Bibliotheca Teubneriana|Bibliotheca Teubneriana]], [[w:Leipzig|Leipzig]] 1888, page [https://archive.org/stream/corpusglossario02leipgoog#page/n647/mode/2up 608], line 25:
#*: '''pangitāre''' laudāre
#*:: '''''pangitāre''''': to praise

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|1|pangitō}}

===Etymology 2===

====Verb====
{{head|la|verb form|head=pangitō}}

# {{inflection of|la|pangō||2//3|s|futr|actv|impr}}

[[Category:Latin frequentative verbs]]
-------- end text --------
Page 4180 pango: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|la|pacō|pagō||arh}}

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*peh₂ǵ-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*pangō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*peh₂ǵ-}}. Cognates include {{cog|grc|πήγνυμι}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|eccl=yes|pangō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|pangō|pepig/pānx/pēg|pānct/pā̆ct}}

# to [[fasten]], [[fix]], [[set]], especially [[drive]], [[sink]], force in
#: {{syn|la|cōnserō|cōnfīgō|fīgō|illigō|colligō|ligō|adalligō|nectō|cōnectō|vinculō}}
#: {{ant|la|explicō|absolvō|dissolvō|solvō}}
#* {{RQ:Paul.Fest.|212|7
|passage='''Pangere''' fīgere, unde plantae '''pangī''' dīcuntur, quum in terram dēmittuntur; inde etiam versūs '''pangī''' vel fīgī in cērā dīcuntur.
|translation='''''Pangere''''' means to drive in, whence plants are said to be '''planted''', when set into the ground; therefore verses too are said to be '''written''' or set in wax.}}
# {{lb|la|by extension}} to set, [[plant]]
#* {{Q|la|Columella|De Re Rustica|11|2|42|quote=Hōc eōdem mēnse in pastinātō sēmināriō novissima positiō est oleāris tāleae, eamque oportet, cum '''pānxeris''', fimō et cinere mīxtīs oblinere, et superpōnere mūscum, nē sōle findātur.|trans=In the same month is the last season for olive cuttings in a trenched nursery-bed, and you should besmear it with manure and ash mixed together when you '''plant''' it, and to set moss over it, so that they may not be split by the sun.}}
## {{lb|la|transferred}} to {{l|en|beget}} {{gloss|children}}
# {{lb|la|mostly|poetic}} to [[compose]], make heard or give out
## {{lb|la|usually}} in [[verse]]
##* {{Q|la|Lucretius|De rerum natura|1|933|thru=934|quote={{...}}deinde quod obscūrā dē rē tam lūcida '''pangō'''<br>carmina, mūsaeō contingēns cūncta lepōre.|trans={{...}}then because I '''compose''' poems so lucid about such an obscure<br>subject, touching everything with the grace of the Muses.}}
##* {{Q|la|Horace|Ars Poetica|416|quote=Nunc satis est dīxisse: “ego mīra poēmata '''pangō'''.”|trans=Nowadays it's enough to say “I '''compose''' wonderful poems.”}}
##* '''37 {{CE}}''', [[w:Martial|Martial]], ''Epigrammata'' [[s:la:Epigrammaton libri XII/Liber III#XXXVIII|3.38.7–8]]
##*: Sī nihil hinc veniet, '''pangentur''' carmina nōbīs:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;audieris, dīcēs esse Marōnis opus.
##*:: If nothing will come out of that, poems '''shall be composed''' by us:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;should you hear, you'd say it's [[w:Vergil|Maro's]] work.
## {{lb|la|rare}} in [[prose]]
##* '''68 {{BC}} – 44 {{BC}}''', [[w:Cicero|Cicero]], ''[[w:Epistulae ad Atticum|Epistulae ad Atticum]]'' [[s:la:Epistulae (Marcus Tullius Cicero)/Epistulae ad Atticum/II|2.6.2]]:
##*: Itaque ἀνέκδοτα ā nōbīs, quae tibi ūnī legāmus, Theopompiō genere aut etiam asperiōre multō '''pangentur'''.
##*:: Therefore anecdotes, which I shall only read to you, will be '''composed''' by me, in [[w:Theopompus|Theopompian]] style or even more savage.
## in [[song]]
##* {{Q|la|Ennius|Annales|293|quote=Tibia Mūsārum '''pangit''' melos|trans=The pipe '''sings''' the melody of the Muses.}}
##* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 310 {{CE}} – ''[[circa|c.]]'' 395 {{CE}}''', [[w:Ausonius|Ausonius]], ''Commemorātiō professōrum Burdigalēnsium'' [[s:la:Commemoratio professorum Burdigalensium|10.42–45]]:
##*: '''Pange''' et Anastasiō<br>flēbile, Mūsa, melum<br>et memorā tenuem<br>nēnia, grammaticum.
##*:: '''Sing''' to Anastasius too<br>a flowing melody, Muse,<br>and remember the delicate<br>''grammaticus'', O dirge.
# {{lb|la|Late Latin|Medieval Latin|poetic}} to celebrate, tell of, record, compose accounts of
#* '''''[[ante|a.]]'' 405 {{CE}}''', [[w:Prudentius|Prudentius]], ''Liber Cathēmerinōn'' [[s:la:Liber Cathemerinon/IX. Hymnus Omnis Horae|9.7]]:
#*: Facta nōs et iam probāta '''pangimus''' mīrācula.
#*:: We '''tell of''' miracles done and already proven.
#* '''1225 {{CE}} – 1274 {{CE}}''', [[w:Thomas Aquinas|Thomas Aquinas]], ''[[w:Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium|Pange lingua]]'' [[s:la:Pange lingua|1–6]]:
#*: {{quote|la|'''Pange''', lingua, glōriōsī<br>corporis mystērium,<br>sanguinisque prētiōsī,<br>quem in mundī pretium<br>frūctus ventris generōsī<br>rēx effūdit gentium.
|translation='''Tell''', tongue, of the mystery<br>of the glorious Body,<br>and of the precious blood,<br>which, for the price of the world,<br>the Fruit of the noble Womb,<br>the King of Nations spilled.}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively|almost exclusively|_|perfect|or|participle}} to fix, [[determine]]
## to [[agree]] upon, [[settle]]
##: {{syn|la|concordō|condīcō|conveniō|cōnsentiō|assentiō|concurrō|cōnstō|congruō}}
##: {{ant|la|discordō|dissentiō|variō|dissideō|abhorreō}}
##* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 190 {{BCE}} – 185 {{BCE}}''', [[w:Plautus|Plautus]], ''[[w:Bacchides (play)|Bacchides]]'' 4.8.38:
##*: Dūcentīs Philippīs rem '''pepigī'''.
##*:: I've '''settled''' the matter for two hundred Philippics.
##* {{Q|la|Ovid|Metamorphoses|4|115|thru=116|quote={{...}}Vēlāmina Thisbēs<br>tollit et ad '''pactae''' sēcum fert arboris umbram.|trans=The cloak of [[w:Thisbe|Thisbe]]<br>he picks up and takes it with him to the shadow of the '''agreed upon''' tree.}}
##* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 69 {{CE}} – ''[[post|p.]]'' 122 {{CE}}''', [[w:Suetonius|Suetonius]], ''Dē rhētoribus'' [[s:la:De viris illustribus (Suetonius)/De grammaticis et rhetoribus#XXV|1.9]]:
##*: Aestīvō tempore adulēscentēs urbānī cum Ōstiam vēnissent, lītus ingressī, piscātōrēs trahentēs rēte adiērunt et '''pepigērunt''', bolum quantī ēmerent.
##*:: When youths from the city came to Ostia in the summer, having went on the beach, they approached the net-drawing fishermen and '''agreed''' on what they should pay for the haul.
## {{lb|la|finance}} to settle a [[price]] or [[monetary]] value
##* {{Q|la|Cicero|De Officiis|3|107|quote=Quod enim ita iūrātum est, ut mēns conciperet fierī oportere, id servandum est; quod aliter, id sī nōn fēcerit, nūllum est periūrium. Ut, sī praedōnibus '''pactum''' prō capite prētium non attulerīs, nūlla fraus sit, nē sī iūrātus quidem id nōn fēcerīs.|trans=That is to be kept, which is so sworn, that the mind conceives that it must be done; else, if one didn't do it, it's no perjury. Like, if you didn't bring the pirates the price '''settled''' for your head, there were no fraud, not even if you didn’t do it having vowed to prior.}}
##* {{Q|la|Suetonius|De vita Caesarum|Vespasian|23|2|quote=Quendam ē cārīs ministrīs dispēnsātiōnem cuidam quasi frātrī petentem cum distulisset, ipsum candidātum ad sē vocāvit; exāctāque pecūniā, quantam is cum suffrāgātōre suō '''pepigerat''', sine morā ōrdināvit; interpellantī mox ministrō: “Alium tibi,” ait, “quaere frātrem; hic, quem tuum putās, meus est.”|trans=When he [&zwnj;[[w:Vespasian|Vespasian]]] put off one of his dear servants requesting a stewardship for someone as for a brother, he called the candidate himself to him; and, having asked for as much money as that man '''had agreed''' with his advocate, he appointed him without delay; to the servant, soon asking about the matter, he said: “Search for another brother; this one, whom you believe your own, is mine.”}}
## to make an [[agreement]], [[pledge]]
##: {{syn|la|pacīscor|ī̆cō|percutiō|feriō}}
##* {{Q|la|Cicero|De Officiis|3|92|quote=Sī quis medicāmentum cuipiam dederit ad aquam intercutem '''pepigerit'''que, sī eō medicāmentō sānus factus esset, nē illō medicāmentō umquam posteā ūterētur, sī eō medicāmentō sānus factus sit et annīs aliquot post inciderit in eundem morbum nec ab eō, quīcum '''pepigerat''', impetret, ut iterum eō liceat utī, quid faciendum sit.|trans=What were to be done, if someone gave somebody a medicine for dropsy and '''made an agreement'''  that, if he were to be made healthy by that medicine, he would never use that medicine afterwards, then, if he were made healthy by that medicine and some years afterwards fell sick to the same disease and didn't manage to get permission from the one he '''made a pledge with''' to use it again.}}
##* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab urbe condita libri|1|30|7|quote=Pūblicō auxiliō nūllō adiūtī sunt, valuitque apud Vēientēs—nam dē cēterīs minus mīrum est—'''pacta''' cum Rōmulō indūtiārum fidēs.|trans=They were helped by no public aid, and the faith of the truce, '''agreed upon''' with Romulus, held among the Veiians—for it's less of a wonder concerning the others.}}
##* '''''[[circa|c.]]'' 330 {{CE}}''', [[w:Juvencus|Juvencus]], ''Evangeliorum libri'' [https://archive.org/details/cvettiaquilinii00juvegoog/page/n123/mode/2up 4.513–516]:
##*: Pars strīctīs gladiīs pars fīdēns pondere clāvae<br>sīgna sequēbātur Iūdae prōmissa furentis.<br>Ōscula nam '''pepigit''' sēsē contingere Chrīstī,<br>quō facile ignōtum caperet miserābile vulgus.
##*:: One part with drawn swords, another confident in the weight of the cudgel<br>was following the promised signs of thievish Judas.<br>For he '''agreed''' to give Christ a kiss,<br>so that the pitiable masses could easily capture the unknown one.
##* '''4th C. {{CE}}''', [[w:Saint Jerome|Saint Jerome]], ''[[w:Vulgate|Vulgate]]'', [[w:Genesis|Genesis]] 15:18:
##*: {{quote|la|In illō diē '''pepigit''' Dominus foedus cum Ābram, dīcēns: “Sēminī tuō dabō terram hanc ā fluviō Aegyptī ūsque ad fluvium magnum Euphrāten {{...}}”
|translation=On that day the Lord '''made''' a covenant with Abraham, saying: “To your seed shall I give this land, from the river of Egypt all the way to the great river Euphrates {{...}}”}}
### {{lb|la|of marital matters}} to [[promise]], [[pledge]], [[arrange]], [[undertake]] to perform
###: {{syn|la|dēspondeō|voveō|spondeō|stipulor|ostentō|profiteor|polliceor|prōmittō}}
###* {{Q|la|Catullus|Carmina|62|26|thru=29|quote=Hespere, quī caelō lūcet iūcundior ignis?<br>qui dēspōnsa tuā firmēs connūbia flammā,<br>quae '''pepigēre''' virī, '''pepigērunt''' ante parentēs,<br>nec iūnxēre prius quam sē tuus extulit ardor.|trans=[[w:Hesperus|Hesperus]], what happier fire shines in the sky?<br>for you strengthen with your flame the contracted marriages,<br>which husbands have '''arranged''', which parents have '''arranged''' beforehand,<br>and united not before your fire arose.}}
###* {{Q|la|Tacitus|Annales|12|5|1|quote=Gāiō Pompēiō Quīntō Vērāniō cōnsulibus '''pactum''' inter Claudium et Agrippīnam mātrimōnium iam fāmā, iam amōre inlicitō fīrmābātur.|trans=With Gnaeus Pompeius and [[w:Quintus Veranius|Quintus Veranius]] as consuls, the espousal '''agreed''' between [[w:Claudius|Claudius]] and [[w:Agrippina the Younger|Agrippina]] was already being strengthened by rumour, by illicit love.}}
### {{lb|la|almost exclusively of women}} to [[betroth]], promise in [[wedlock]]
###* {{Q|la|Ovid|Heroides|8|95|thru=96|quote=Nōn cultūs tibi cūra meī, nec '''pacta''' marītō<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;intrāvī thalamōs mātre parante novōs.|trans=My rearing was not your concern, nor have I, '''betrothed''' to a husband,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;entered new bedchambers, with mother preparing them.}}
###* {{Q|la|Valerius Flaccus|Argonautica|584|thru=586|quote=Quāque iterum tacitō sparsit vaga lūmina vultū<br>aut frātris quaerēns aut '''pactī''' coniugis arma,<br>saevus ibī miserae sōlusque occurrit Iāsōn.|trans=And wherever she cast wandering eyes with silent face<br>either searching for her brother's or her '''betrothed''' husband's weapons,<br>there fierce [[w:Jason|Jason]], alone, met her.}}

====Usage notes====
The third principal part {{m|la|pānxī}} is very rare; outside a few textually uncertain attestations, the above quotation from Columella is the only place it is found. The perfect {{m|la|pēgī}} might belong to the archaic form {{m|la|pagō}}/{{m|la|pacō}} instead.

