Page 4181 papo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA|papō}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1.nopass|papō|papāv}}

# {{alternative form of|la|pappō|nodot=1}}
#* Aulus Persius Flaccus. In: ''The Satires of A. Persius Flaccus with a Translation and Commentary by John Conington. To which is prefixed A Lecture on the Life and Writings of Persius Delivered at Oxford by the same author, January 1855. Edited by H. Nettleship'', 2nd edition, Oxford 1874, p. 52
#*: [...] '''papare''' minutum<br /> poscis [...]
#*:: Note: There are also editions spelling it {{m|la|pappō|pappare}}.

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|1.nopass|papō|papāv}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|la|pāpārium}}

===Etymology 2===
{{af|la|pāpa|-ō|id2=denominative}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1+|pāpō}}

# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin|intransitive}} to be [[pope]], to become pope
#* {{quote-book|la|year=c. 1367|title=Eulogium Historiarum|text=Conjuravit etiam ille suo vivente, Marcellum presbyterum qui post ipsum '''papavit''' ut praeceptum Diocletiani de immolatione non adimpleret.|section=2.37|sectionurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=BOAsAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA186|t=And while he lived he conspired that Marcellus the presbyter who '''became pope''' after him not carry out Diocletian's order concerning the sacrifice.}}
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin|transitive}} to make someone pope

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|1+|pāpō}}

