Page 4201 pateo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*peth₂-}}
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*peth₂-}}.

Cognate with {{m|la|pandō}}, {{cog|osc|patensíns||they opened}}, {{cog|grc|πετάννυμι||to spread out, to spread wide}} (< {{m|ine-pro||*peth₂-néu-}}) and {{m|grc|πίτνημι||to spread out}} (< {{m|ine-pro||*pt-ne-h₂-}}), {{cog|ae|𐬞𐬀𐬚𐬀𐬥𐬀|tr=paθana|t=broad}}, {{cog|ang|fæþm}} (whence {{cog|en|fathom}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|pateō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2.nopass|pateō|patu}}

# to be [[open]], [[accessible]], [[attainable]]
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|6|371-372|quote=‘atque utinam pugnae '''pateat''' locus! arma capessant<br/>et, sī nōn poterunt exsuperāre, cadant’|trans=''“Yet if only a battlefield '''would open'''! May they fight; and, if they are unable to prevail, let them fall.”''<br/>(The poetic voice of Mars, asking Jupiter to defend Rome against the invading {{w|Gauls}}, is saying in effect, “Give the Romans a chance to fight.”)}}
#: {{syn|la|patesco}}
# to be [[exposed]], [[vulnerable]]
# to [[increase]] or [[extend]] (said of frontiers or land)
#* Julius Caesar, ''De Bello Gallico'', book 1, chapter 2:
#*: Fīnēs...quī in longitūdinem mīlia passuum CCXL, in latitudinem CLXXX '''patēbant'''.
# To be [[clear]], [[evident]]

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|2.nopass|pateō|patu}}

====Derived terms====
{{col-top|2}}
* {{l|la|patefaciō}}
* {{l|la|patera}}
* {{l|la|patibulum}}
* {{l|la|pator}}
* {{l|la|Patulcius}}
* {{l|la|bipatēns}}
{{bottom}}

