Page 4265 perdo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*peridō}}. Equivalent to {{af|la|per-|*dere|t2=put/give}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|perdō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3.nopass|perdō|perdid}} {{i|Classical}}<br />
{{la-verb|3|perdō|perdid|perdit}} {{i|post-Classical}}

# to [[destroy]], [[ruin]], [[wreck]]
#: {{syn|la|dēstruō|ruīnō|occīdō|dēvāstō|ēvāstō|vāstō|tollō|dīruō|aboleō|exscindō|accīdō|populor|interimō|impellō|sepeliō|absūmō|perimō|trucīdō}}
#: {{ant|la|ēmendō|reficiō|reparō|corrigō|medeor}}
# to [[waste]], [[squander]]
#: {{syn|la|abūtor|conterō|cōnsūmō|dissipō|effundō}}
#* [[w:Suetonius|Suetonius]] writing of [[w:Titus|Titus]]
#*: Atque etiam recordatus quondam super cenam, quod nihil cuiquam toto die praestitisset, memorabilem illam meritoque laudatam vocem edidit: "Amici, diem '''perdidi'''".
#*:: One evening at dinner, realizing that he had done nobody any favour throughout the entire day, he spoke these memorable words: "Friends, '''I have wasted''' a day".
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Tristia|1|2|13|quote=verba miser frūstrā nōn prōficientia '''perdō'''.|trans=''Wretched [as I am], vainly '''I waste''' words, making no progress.''}}
# to [[have]] no more, to be [[deprived]] of
#: {{syn|la|dēsum|egeō|indigeō|careō|dēlinquō|deficiō|cessō}}
#: {{ant|la|flōreō|abundō|affluō|niteō}}
# to [[lose]] utterly, [[suffer]] loss
#: {{syn|la|āmittō}}

====Usage notes====
* Speakers of Classical Latin typically avoided the passive forms of this verb, using {{m|la|pereō}} instead.

====Conjugation====
* Classical Latin conjugation, without passive forms (note the alternative present subjunctive, especially common in Plautus):
{{la-conj|3.nopass|perdō|perdid|1s_pres_actv_subj=perdam/perduim|2s_pres_actv_subj=perdās/perduīs|3s_pres_actv_subj=perdat/perduit|3p_pres_actv_subj=perdant/perduint}}

* Complete conjugation, in later Latin:
{{la-conj|3|perdō|perdid|perdit}}

====Coordinate terms====
* {{l|la|pereō}}

