===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*pinsō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*peys-||to crush}}.

Cognate includes Ancient Greek {{m|grc|πτισάνη||barley}}, {{m|grc|πτίσσω||to winnow, peel}}; Proto-Slavic {{m|sla-pro|*pьšenìca||wheat}}; Sanskrit {{m|sa|पिनष्टि||to grind}}. Compare {{m|la|pīla}}, {{m|la|pīlum}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|la|pindō||Late Latin, glosses}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{la-IPA|pīnsō}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|3|pīnsō|pīnsu/pīns|pīns/pīnsit/pī̆st}}

# to [[beat]], [[pound]]
#* '''AD 4th C.''', [[w:Diomedes Grammaticus|Diomedes Grammaticus]] (author), [[w:Heinrich Keil|Heinrich Keil]] (editor), ''Artis Grammaticae Liber I'' (1857), page [https://archive.org/details/grammaticilatini01keil/page/373#kmode/2up 373]:
#*: Sed apud veterēs reperīmus etiam n litterā additā '''pīnsō''', quod est tundō, ut Ennius decimō Annālium '''''pīnsunt''' terram genibus''.
#*:: But in the older authors we also find ''pīnsō'' with an added ''n'', which is 'to beat', like Ennius in the tenth book of the Annals: ''they '''pound''' the dirt with their knees''.
# to [[lash]], [[scourge]]
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Mercator|2|3|80|quote=ea molet, coquet, cōnficiet pēnsum, '''pīnsētur''' flagrō|trans=She will grind, cook, spin wool, she'll be '''lashed''' by a whip{{...}}}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|3|pīnsō|pīnsu/pīns|pīns/pīnsit/pī̆st}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|la|pī̆stor}}
* {{l|la|pī̆stō}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|la|pīla}}
* {{l|la|pīlum}}
* {{l|la|pīsō}}

===Etymology 2===
Post-classical conjugation transfer of Etymology 1.

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1+|pīnsō}}

# {{lb|la|post-classical}} {{alternative_form_of|la|pīnsō}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{la-conj|1+|pīnsō}}

