Page 4452 pleo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*pleh₁-}}
Exists only as a [[bound morpheme]] in prefixed verbs, where it continues {{inh|la|itc-pro|*plēō}}, a yé-present innovatively formed to the aorist stem of {{der|la|ine-pro|*pleh₁-||to fill}}. Compare {{cog|ru|-по́лнить}}, a cognate with the same meaning that likewise does not occur uncompounded.

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2|pleō|plēv|plēt}}

# {{n-g|Combining form used to form prefixed verbs with the approximate meaning "to [[fill]]".}}
#* {{Q|la|Pompeius|year=ca. 500|In artem Donati|p. 240 |l|34|quote={{...}}est ''<etiam>'' conpositum quod nōn potest fierī simplex, ut ''con'''pleō''''': nēmō potest dīcere '''pleō'''.|t=it's likewise a compound when it cannot be used on its own, such as ''com'''plete''''': one can't say '''plete'''.}}
#* {{seeCites|la}}

====Conjugation====
Only one form, {{m|la|plendī}} (CIL 2.6278.38), is attested in classical Latin without a prefix outside of grammarians. (Festus p.230M) also cites {{m|la|plentur}} without attribution.
{{la-conj|2|-pleō|-plēv|-plēt|ger_gen=plendī}}

