Page 4475 populor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{m|la|populus|t=human, community, people}}. According to De Vaan, the meaning developed from an earlier sense "to have an army pass through"<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=populus|pages=480}}</ref>.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|populor}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|populor}}

# to [[lay waste]], [[ravage]] or [[devastate]], [[destroy]] or [[ruin]]
#: {{syn|la|ruīnō|dēvāstō|ēvāstō|vāstō|aboleō|occīdō|perdō|dēstruō|exscindō|impellō|accīdō|tollō|dīruō|sepeliō|absūmō|interimō|perimō|trucīdō}}
#: {{ant|la|ēmendō|reficiō|reparō|corrigō|medeor}}
#* {{Q|la|Julius Caesar|Bello Gallico|1|11|quote=Helvetii iam per angustias et fines Sequanorum suas copias traduxerant et in Haeduorum fines pervenerant eorumque agros '''populabantur'''.|trans=The Helvetii had by this time led their forces over through the narrow defile and the territories of the Sequani, and had arrived at the territories of the Aedui, and '''were ravaging''' their lands.}}
# to [[plunder]] or [[pillage]]
#: {{syn|la|dīripiō|dēpraedor|praedor|expugnō|trahō|agō}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|1+|populor}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|populābundus}}
* {{l|la|Populōnia}}

===References===
<references/>
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
* {{R:M&A}}
-------- end text --------
Page 4476 porceo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*h₂erk-}}
From {{af|la|po-|arceō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|pō̆rceō}}
* Note: there are no other examples of ''ŏ-ă'' hiatus from which to generalize the length of the resulting vowel; however, by the general rule a short vowel is simply deleted.

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2|pō̆rceō}}

# {{lb|la|archaic}} to [[keep]] or [[ward]] off or back, [[hinder]], [[restrain]]
#* {{Q|la|Sextus Pompeius Festus|De verborum significatione|218|quote=porcae appellantur rārī sulcī, quī dūcuntur aquae dērīvandae grātiā, dictī quod '''pō̆rcent''', id est prohibent aquam frūmentīs nocēre.| (''This folk-etymology might yield evidence for short pǒrcēre'')}}
#* {{Q|la|Lucilius|Saturae|apud Nonium Marcellum 160|9|quote=nōn tē porrō prōcēdere '''pō̆rcent'''.}}
#* {{Q|la|Ennius|Telamo|apud Nonium Marcellum 160|6|quote=deum mē sentit facere pietās, cīvium '''pō̆rcet''' pudor.}}
#* {{Q|la|Varro|Menippeae|apud Nonium Marcellum 160|8|quote=hunc Cerēs, cibī ministra, frūgibus suīs '''pō̆rcet'''.}}
#* {{Q|la|Marcus Pacuvius|Atalanta|year=2nd century BCE|apud Nonium Marcellum 160|2|quote=mī gnāte, ut verear ēloquī, '''pō̆rcet''' pudor.}}
#* {{Q|la|Accius|Epigoni|apud Nonium Marcellum 160|5|quote=quibus oculīs quisquam nostrum poterit illōrum optuī<br>vultūs, quōs jam ab armīs annī '''pō̆rcent'''?}}

====Usage notes====
The perfect stem {{m|la||porxī}} appears only once in a gloss by the grammarian Charisius.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|2|pō̆rceō}}

====Related terms====
