Page 4483 posso: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Partial regularization of the Classical Latin {{m|la|possum}}. The form {{m|VL.||possō}} is found beginning in the early second century CE in the letters of {{w|Claudius Terentianus}}.<ref>Laurent, Richard. 1999. ''Past participles from Latin to Romance''. Berkeley: University of California Press. §2.1.2.</ref> The 3SG imperfect {{m|VL.||potēbat}} is attested at the end of the second century CE,<ref>{{R:DÉRom|'pOt-e-}}</ref> while the infinitive {{m|VL.||potēre}} and the 1SG imperfect {{m|VL.||potēbam}} are attested several times in the sixth century CE., and {{m|VL.||poteō}} (replacing {{m|VL.||possō}} and reflecting a full regularization of the verb) is first attested in 745 CE.<ref>Grandgent, Charles Hall. 1907. ''An introduction to Vulgar Latin''. Boston: D.C. Heath & Co. §403.</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Proto-Romance}} {{IPA|la|/ˈpɔsso/}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2|poteō|potu|*potūt|1s_pres_actv_indc=possō}} {{lb|la|Vulgar Latin}}

# to [[can]], to be [[able]] to

