Page 4484 possum: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*h₁es-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*possom}}, from {{m|itc-pro|*potis||master in control of}} + {{m|itc-pro|*som||I am}}. Cognates include {{cog|osc|púttiad||they can}} and {{cog|spx|puti||I could}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-possum.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|irreg|possum}}

# to be [[be able to|able]], to [[can]], to [[may]]
#: {{syn|la|polleō|queō|valeō|praevaleō|vigeō}}
#: {{ant|la|nequeō}}
#: {{uxi|la|Tunc, modo edere non '''potuit'''|t=At that time, he just '''wasn't able''' to eat}}
#: {{uxi|la|'''Possum''' Latine loqui|t=I '''am able''' to speak in Latin}}
#: {{uxi|la|'''Potes'''ne mihi succurrere, quaeso?|t='''Can''' you help me please?}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|39-41|quote=“[...] Pallāsne exūrere classem<br/>Argīvum atque ipsōs '''potuit''' submergere pontō,<br/>ūnīus ob noxam et furiās Aiācis Oīlēī?”|trans=''“Was not Pallas '''able''' to burn the Argive fleet and to drown [the men] themselves in the sea – [all] because of the crime and fury of [just] one man, Ajax, [son] of Oileus?”''<br/>(Pallas or {{w|Athena}} meted divine retribution after the fall of Troy; there the [[Argive]]/Greek invaders had desecrated Athena’s temple, in which {{w|Ajax the Lesser}} assaulted {{w|Cassandra}}.)}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Tristia|1|57-58|quote=tū tamen ī prō mē, tū, cui licet, aspice Rōmam,<br/>dī facerent, '''possem''' nunc meus esse līber!|trans=''Nevertheless, you go instead of me – you, to whom it is allowed, behold [the city  of] Rome – Gods! [If only you] would grant [it], [that] now '''I would be able''' to be my book!''<br/>(The exiled poet addresses his book as if it were a living emissary that he will send to Rome in his place. The optative subjunctive “facerent” expresses a wish, followed by the jussive subunctive “possem” stating what the writer believes should be done.)}}

====Conjugation====
This verb is irregular, but resembles {{m|la|sum}} prefixed with ''pot-''. Several contractions and simplifications occur, however: -ts- → -ss-, -tf- → -t-, -tess- → -ss- (note that {{m|la|potēns}} does not originate from non-existent ''*fēns'').

In Vulgar Latin, the first person singular is changed to {{m|la|possō}} (causing the verb to be treated like a third conjugation verb (base poss-) in the present subjunctive (''*possam'', ''*possās'')), though the rest of the verb became the second conjugation verb ''potēre'' ((''tu'') ''*potēs'', (''is''/''ea''/''id'') ''*potet'', (''nōs'') ''*potēmus'', (''vōs'') ''*potētis'', and (''eī''/''eae''/''ea'') ''*possunt''), based on forms like ''potuī'' and ''potēns'', just like ''velle'' (infinitive of {{m|la|volō}}) was reformed to ''*volēre'' after forms like ''voluī'' and ''volēns''.

However, Osco-Umbrian forms confirm that a Proto-Italic verb {{cog|itc-pro|*poteō|t=to be master}} (stem ''*pot-ē-'') must also be reconstructed that, although lost in Latin otherwise, appears to have been the ultimate historical source of ''potuī'' and ''potēns''.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|potis, pote|484f.}}</ref>
{{la-conj|irreg|possum}}
* Some passive forms are attested in pre-classical authors (e.g. {{m|la|potestur}}, {{m|la|poteratur}}, {{m|la|possitur}}, {{m|la|possetur}}).

