Page 4676 profero: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*bʰer-|*telh₂-|id2=bear}}
From {{affix|la|prō-|gloss1=in front of|ferō|gloss2=carry, bear}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|prōferō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|irreg|prōferō}}

# to [[bring out]], [[bring forth]]
#: {{syn|la|praefero|induco|produco|affero|prōpōnō|perfero|edo}}
#: {{ant|la|auferō|tollō|adimō|rapiō|ēripiō|dīripiō|arceō|abdūcō}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|signa}} '''proferre'''|t=to push forward the army}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|aliquid}} {{l|la|in}} {{l|la|medium}} '''proferre'''|t=to expose something publicly}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|aliquid}} {{l|la|in}} {{l|la|diem}} {{l|la|posterum}} '''proferre'''|t=to postpone something until the following day}}
#* {{Q|la|Suetonius|De vita Caesarum|2|18|quote=Per idem tempus conditōrium et corpus Magnī Alexandrī, cum '''prōlātum''' ē penetrālī subiēcisset oculīs, corōnā aureā impositā ac flōribus aspersīs venerātus est cōnsultusque, num et Ptolemaeum īnspicere vellet, rēgem sē voluisse ait vidēre, nōn mortuōs.|trans=Around the same time he paid honours to the sarcophagus and body of Alexander the Great, when he examined it '''brought out''' of the mausoleum, by putting on it a golden crown and spreading flowers, and was asked whether he also wished to see the Ptolemaean mausoleum, he said that he wanted to see a king, not corpses.}}
#* {{Q|la|Juvenal|Satires|1|126|quote=''''Prōfer''', Galla, caput.' 'Nōlī vexāre, quiēscit.'|trans=''''Bring out''' your head, Galla.' 'Don't disturb her, she's resting.'}}
# to [[make known]], [[produce]] in [[public]], [[publish]], [[reveal]]
#: {{syn|la|praebeō|ostendō|ostentō|prōdō|indicō|prōpōnō|expōnō|prōtrahō|profiteor|vulgō|gerō|praestō|coarguō|fateor|acclārō}}
# to [[appear]], [[show]] oneself
#: {{syn|la|prōcēdō|appāreō|obversor|cresco|exorior|orior|coorior}}
#: {{ant|la|concēdō|decēdō|cēdō|intereō}}
# to [[offer]], [[proffer]], [[present]]
#: {{syn|la|offerō|expōnō|praebeō|obiciō|porrigō|afferō|prōpōnō|sufferō|polliceor|dōnō|condōnō|largior|moveō}}
# to [[stretch]] out, [[extend]]
#: {{syn|la|extendō|distendō|prōlongō|pandō|porrigō|prōtrahō|explicō|trahō}}
#* {{Q|la|Gellius|Noctes Atticae|13|14|3|quote=Habēbat autem iūs '''prōferendī''' pōmēriī quī populum Rōmānum agrō dē hostibus captō auxerat.|trans=He who had enlarged the Roman peoples with captured land had the right of '''extending''' the [[w:Pomerium|Pomerium]].}}
# {{lb|la|military}} to [[march]] on, [[advance]]
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[produce]], induce [[growth]]
# to [[utter]], [[pronounce]]
# to [[put off]], [[defer]], [[adjourn]]
# {{lb|la|painting}} to [[bring out]], [[represent]] [[distinctly]]
#* {{Q|la|Pliny the Elder|Naturalis Historia|35|56|quote={{...}}Cīmōnem Cleōnaeum. Hic catagrapha invēnit, hoc est oblīquās imāginēs, et variē fōrmāre voltūs, respicientēs suspicientēsve vel dēspicientēs; articulīs membra distīnxit, vēnās '''prōtulit''', praeterque in vestibus rūgās et sinūs invēnit.|trans={{...}}Cimon of Cleonae. He invented profile paintings, that is, side-views, and fashioning faces in different ways: looking back, up or down; he distinguished members from joints, '''brought out''' the veins, and beside that devised creases and folds in clothes.}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively|with [[se]]}} to [[raise]] or [[elevate]] oneself from a lower station
# to [[make known]], [[publicize]]
# to [[discover]], [[invent]]
#: {{syn|la|inveniō}}
# to [[quote]], [[cite]], [[mention]]
#* {{Q|la|Terence|Phormio|2|3|48|quote=DEMIPHO. Dī tibi maleficiant! PHORMIO. Prīmus essēs memoriter<br>prōgeniem vestram ūsque ab avō atque atavō '''prōferēns'''.|trans=DEMIPHO. May the Gods confound you! PHORMIO. You'd have been the first, from memory,<br>to '''cite''' your line of kindred, even as far back as from grandfather and great-grandfather.}}
# to [[impel]]
# to [[prolong]]

