Page 4802 quaeso: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|la|quæso||ecclesiastical}}
* {{alter|la|quēsō||medieval}}

===Etymology===
Related to {{m|la|quaerō}}. Perhaps for ''*quaessō'', [[desiderative]] formation with {{der|la|ine-pro|*-(h₁)seti}} (as in {{m|la|vīsō}}).<ref>{{R:Sihler 1995|section=457.B}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|quaesō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|quaesō|quaesīv/quaesi|quaesīt}}

# to [[beg]] or [[ask]] (for)
#: {{syn|la|exposcō|flagitō|efflagitō|exigō|rogō|ērogō|exorō|precor|petō|expetō|prehensō}}
# to [[seek]]
#: {{syn|la|inquīrō|conquīrō|spectō|scrūtor|circumspiciō|quaerō|requīrō}}
# {{lb|la|in first-person}} [[please]] [[File: Vatican ATM in Latin.jpg|thumb|Pre-historic ATM politely asking for credit card.]]

====Usage notes====
The verb was used normally by pre-Classical authors. Classically, however, it was used mainly in the first person singular present, '''quaesō'''. Other attested forms listed in the Oxford Latin Dictionary are:
* {{m|la|quaesit}}, third person singular present active indicative
* {{m|la|quaesumus}}, first person plural present active indicative {{q|common in Ecclesiastical Latin}}
* {{m|la|quaesitur}}, third person singular present passive
* {{m|la|quaesere}}, present active infinitive
* {{m|la|quaesentibus}}, dative or ablative plural of *quaesens, present active participle
* {{m|la|quaesendum}}, inflected form of *quaesendus, future passive participle (gerundive)

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|quaesō|quaesīv/quaesi|quaesīt}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Elementary Lewis}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
