Page 4853 recipio: -------- begin text --------
===Alternative forms===
* {{sense|imperative in medical contexts}} {{l|la|r.}}, {{l|la|℞}}, {{l|la|Rx}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|la|re-|capiō|t2=take, seize}}, with characteristic vowel reduction of unstressed /a/ in Old Latin (< *récapiō).

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|recipiō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|recipiō|recēp|recept}}

# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[take back]], (i.e., regain possession of something)
# {{lb|la|transitive|of a city}} to [[recapture]]
#* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab Urbe Condita|26|1|quote=Ea tum cura maxime intentos habebat Romanos, non ab ira tantum, quae in nullam unquam ciuitatem iustior fuit, quam quod urbs tam nobilis ac potens, sicut defectione sua traxerat aliquot populos, ita '''recepta''' inclinatura rursus animos uidebatur ad ueteris imperii respectum.|year=27-25 BCE|trans=This concern in particular troubled the mindful Romans at the time, not so much because of anger, which has never been more justified against any other city, rather because a city so noble and powerful, in the same way that it had attracted the support of a number of communities by its revolt, was thought would again turn attention back towards respect for the previous government once '''recaptured'''.}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[receive]], [[accept]], [[admit]]
#: {{uxi|la|'''accipere''' {{l|la|beneficium}} {{l|la|ab}} {{l|la|aliquo}}|t=to receive favor from someone}}
#* {{Q|la|Marcus Tullius Cicero|Epistulae ad Atticum|year=1st c. BC|quote=Numestium ex litteris tuis studiose scriptis libenter in amicitiam '''recepi'''.|t=I have gladly '''received''' Numestius into friendship on account of your eagerly written letters.}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Caesar|Caesar]]|[[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]]|VII, 20|quote=quem [...] ne qua civitas suis finibus '''recipiat'''|t=that no state should '''admit''' him within its territories}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Caesar|Caesar]]|[[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]]|VII, 26|quote=Timor misericordiam non '''recipit'''|t=The fear does not '''admit''' to the mercy}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to take upon [[oneself]], [[undertake]], [[accept]] (esp. when done as a [[duty]] or under an [[obligation]]. Cf. {{m|la|suscipiō}})
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[retreat]], [[withdraw]]
#: {{uxi|la|Eorum impetum Galli sustinere non potuerunt atque in fugam coniecti multis amissis se ad agmen '''receperunt'''|The Gauls could not sustain their attack but were put to flight, and having lost a great number of men, '''retreated''' to their main body}} ([[w:Caesar|Caesar]], [[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]], VII, 13)
#: {{uxi|la|Gladiis destrictis portas occupaverunt suosque omnes incolumes '''receperunt'''|having drawn the swords, they occupied the gates and '''withdrew''' all their men safe}} ([[w:Caesar|Caesar]], [[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]], VII, 12)
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[endure]], [[tolerate]], [[allow]]
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[rescue]], [[secure]], [[save]]

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3.sigm|recipiō|recēp|recept|receps}}

