Page 5133 rugio: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Per De Vaan, related to {{cog|grc|ἐρεύγομαι||roar}}, {{m|grc|ὀρῠμαγδός||noise}}, {{m|grc|ὠρῡγή||noise, roaring}}, and possibly related to {{m|la|rū̆dō|t=(of lions) to roar; (of donkeys) to bray}}. Despite the phonetic similarity to {{m|la|ērūgō|t=belch}}, De Vaan thinks cognacy is unlikely, viewing the semantics as a poor match (although Greek {{m|grc|ἐρεύγομαι}} also has the meaning "belch", which De Vaan considers a homophone).<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=-rūgiō, -īre|page=528-529}}</ref> Perhaps from {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₁rewg-|t=to roar}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|rū̆giō}}
* The ''u'' in the first syllable is marked short by Lewis and Short as well as Gaffiot, but long by De Vaan (2008)  and Wartburg (1928–2002)<ref>{{R:FEW|10|546|rūgīre}}</ref>. Buchi and Schweickard say that although the Romance outcomes require the reconstruction of Proto-Romance */u/, the quantity of the vowel in written Latin is uncertain.<ref name=DÉRom>{{R:DÉRom|'rug-i-|v|18 May 2023|text=Si la reconstruction comparative exige clairement */ˈu/, la quantité du &lt;u> en latin écrit est mal assurée (''cf.'' Ernout/Meillet<sub>4</sub>).}}</ref> 

===Verb===
{{la-verb|4.nopass|rū̆giō|rū̆gīv/rū̆gi}}

# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[roar]], [[bellow]]; [[rumble]]
#* {{Q|la|Jerome|Vulgate|Ieremias|2|15|quote=super eum '''rugierunt''' leones et dederunt vocem suam posuerunt terram eius in solitudinem civitates eius exustae sunt et non est qui habitet in eis|trans=The young lions have '''roared''' on him, and yelled; and they have made his land waste: his cities are burned up, without inhabitant.}}
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[bray]]

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|4.nopass|rū̆giō|rū̆gīv/rū̆gi}}

====Synonyms====
