Page 5174 sarpo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*serp-|*sr̥p-}}, with cognates including Old Church Slavonic {{m|cu|срьпъ||sickle}}, Latvian {{m|lv|sirpis}}. Traditionally connected with {{cog|grc|ἁρπάγη||hook}}, whence {{cog|la|harpagō}} and {{cog|en|harpoon}}, in turn uncertainly related to {{m|grc|ἅρπη||bird of prey; sickle}}, itself uncertainly derived from {{m|ine-pro|*serp-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|sarpō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|sarpō|sarps|sarpt}}

# to [[prune]] (especially the vine)

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|sarpō|sarps|sarpt}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|es|serpollo}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
-------- end text --------
Page 5175 sarrio: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|sariō}}

===Etymology===
De Vaan (2008) expressed uncertainty on whether the geminate consonant form {{m|la|sarriō}} or the long vowel form {{m|la|sārio}} was the more original form. (Note, however, that Gaffiot lists the form with one r as having short ă.) He assigns this word to {{der|la|ine-pro|*sers-}}, connecting it with {{m|la|serra||saw}}, while contemplating on whether it could be derived from a root {{m|ine-pro|*ser-||to cut off}}.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|page=539|head=sārio, -īre}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|sarriō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|4|sarriō|sarru/sarrīv|sarrīt}}

# to [[hoe]]
# to [[weed]] (crops)

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|4|sarriō|sarru/sarrīv|sarrīt}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|la|sarculum}}
* {{l|la|sarrītor}}/ {{l|la|sartor}}

===References===
* {{R:L&S}}
* {{R:Gaffiot}}
