Page 5195 scio: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*skey-}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*skijō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*skey-|t=to distinguish, dissect}}. Related to {{m|la|secō||to cut off}}, {{m|la|signum||a sign}}, {{cog|grc|σχίζω||to split}} and {{cog|en|shit}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|sciō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|4++|sciō}}

# to [[be able to]], to [[know]] {{gl|how to do}}, [[understand]], to have [[practical]] [[knowledge]]
#: {{syn|la|agnōscō|cognōscō|inveniō|sentiō|cōnsciō|sapiō|nōscō|scīscō|intellegō|percipiō|discernō|tongeō|cernō|audiō}}
#: {{ant|la|ignōrō|nesciō}}
#: {{uxi|la|'''Scīs'''{{l|la|-ne|ne}} {{l|la|ubi}} {{l|la|habitēmus}}?|t=Do you '''know''' where we live?}}
# {{lb|la|euphemism}} to [[know]] [[carnally]]

====Conjugation====
* Used with adverb, accusative, or ablative
* The third and fourth principal parts are shared with {{m|la|scīscō}}.
* The regular present imperatives, {{m|la|scī}} and {{m|la|scīte}}, are almost never encountered, with the regular second person future imperative forms {{m|la|scītō}} and {{m|la|scītōte}} being used instead.
* Irregular forms are commonly encountered in early Latin, especially in the imperfect and future tenses.
** syncopated perfect forms: {{m|la|scīsse}} (= {{m|la|scīvisse}}), {{m|la|scīstī}} (= {{m|la|scīvistī}}), {{m|la|scīrint}} (= {{m|la|scīverint}})
** archaic imperfect forms: {{m|la|scībam}}, {{m|la|scībās}}, {{m|la|scībat}}, {{m|la|*scībāmus}}, {{m|la|scībātis}}, {{m|la|scībant}} (= {{m|la|sciēbam}} etc.)
** archaic future forms: {{m|la|scībō}}, {{m|la|scībis}}, {{m|la|scībit}}, {{m|la|scībimus}}, {{m|la|*scībitis}}, {{m|la|scībunt}} (= {{m|la|sciam}} etc.), ''passive'' {{m|la|scībitur}}
** Contraction {{m|la|scīn}} (or {{m|la|scīn'}}) for ''scīsne'' ({{m|la|scīs}} + {{m|la|-ne}})
{{la-conj|4++|sciō}}

====Derived terms====
