Page 5247 sequor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*sekʷ-|id=follow}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*sekʷōr}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*sekʷ-||to follow}}. Cognates include {{cog|sa|सचते|tr=sácate}}, {{cog|ae|𐬵𐬀𐬗𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬉}} and {{cog|grc|ἕπομαι}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|sequor|secūt}}

# {{lb|la|with accusative}} to [[follow]], to [[come]] or [[go]] after, to [[pursue]]
#: {{syn|la|persequor|cōnsequor|īnsequor|īnsector|continuō|excipiō|exsequor|agō|premō|īnstō}}
#*{{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|3|188|quote=Cēdāmus Phoebō et monitī meliōra '''sequāmur'''.|trans=Let us yield to Phoebus, and having been warned '''follow''' better things.}}
#*{{Q|la|Ovid|Metamorphoses|7|20|quote=Video meliora proboque, deteriora '''sequor'''.|trans=I see, and I desire the better: I '''follow''' the worse.}}
#*{{Q|la|Cato the Elder|quote=Rem tene, verba '''sequentur'''.|trans=Grasp the subject, the words '''will follow'''.}}
# to [[accede]] to, to [[conform]] to
# to [[attend]], [[accompany]]
#: {{syn|la|comitō|exsequor|cōnsequor}}
# to [[succeed]] (i.e., follow in position)
#: {{syn|la|subeō|succēdō|excipiō}}
# {{lb|la|logic|third person}} to [[follow]], [[ensue]]

====Usage notes====
The verb {{m|la|duco|ducere}} is used for "to be followed," effectively giving ducere two passives, this verb being one of them.

In later Latin, an active voice was invented for "to follow," leading to the passive forms being relegated to "to be followed."

