Page 5256 servo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*serwāō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*ser-||to watch over, protect}}. Possible cognates in Ancient Greek {{m|grc|Ἥρα}}, {{m|grc|ἥρως}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|servō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+.poet-sync-perf|servō}}

# to [[maintain]], [[keep]]
#: {{syn|la|teneo|obtineō|retineo|capio|contineō|comprehendo|obsideo|sustineo}}
#* {{Q|la|Terence|Hecyra|3|3|42|quote=Pollicitus sum et '''servāre''' in eō certumst quod dīxī fidem.|trans=I’ve made a promise and it’s certain that I shall '''keep''' what I said.}}
#* {{Q|la|Horace|Odes|2|3|1|thru=2|quote=Aequam mementō rēbus in arduīs<br>'''servāre''' mentem{{...}}|trans=Remember to '''maintain''' a level mind in<br>difficult situations{{...}}}}
# to [[protect]], [[save]], [[keep]], [[guard]], [[safeguard]], [[watch over]]
#: {{syn|la|salvō|tūtor|vindicō|cū̆stōdiō|sospitō|teneō|adimō|prōtegō|tegō|adsum|sustineō|dēfendō|tueor|prohibeō|arceō|mūniō|ēripiō}}
#: {{ant|la|immineō|īnstō|obiectō}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|546-548|quote=“Quem sī Fāta virum '''servant''', sī vēscitur aurā<br/>aetheriā, neque adhūc crūdēlibus occubat umbrīs,<br/>nōn metus [...].”|trans=''“If the Fates '''protect''' this man, if he breathes in the upper air, and neither still has he fallen among the cruel shades, [have] no fear [...].”''<br/>(Ilioneus, speaking of Aeneas, assures Queen Dido regarding the Trojan presence in Carthage.)}}
# to [[give heed]] to, [[pay attention]] to; [[watch]], [[observe]] any thing
#: {{syn|la|observō|conspiciō|cōnspicor|cū̆stōdiō|animadvertō|caveō|intueor}}
# {{lb|la|with {{m|la|ex}} in|_|Classical Latin|;|with {{m|la|ab}} in|_|Late Latin}} to [[save]], to [[deliver]], [[rescue]]
#: {{syn|la|līberō|eximō|absolvō|vindicō|excipiō}}
#: {{ant|la|refrēnō|coerceō|saepiō|officiō|obstō|comprimō|impediō|arceō|supprimō}}
#* {{Q|la|Cicero|De re publica|1|3|5|quote=Hinc enim illa et apud Graecōs exempla, Miltiadem, victōrem domitōremque Persārum, nōndum sānātīs volneribus iīs, quae corpore adversō in clārissima victōriā accēpisset, vītam ex hostium tēlīs '''servātam''' in cīvium vinclīs prōfūdisse, et Themistoclem patriā, quam līberāvisset, pulsum atque prōterritum non in Graeciae portūs per sē '''servātōs''', sed in barbariae sinūs cōnfūgisse, quam adflīxerat.|trans=Hence these examples among the Greeks as well: [[w:Miltiades|Miltiades]], victor and conqueror of the Persians, to have spilt his life, '''preserved''' from enemies’ weapons, in the chains of his citizens, with the wounds received on the front of his body in the course of the most glorious victory not yet healed; and [[w:Themistocles|Themistocles]], banished and driven away from the country he had freed, to have fled not to the harbours of Greece, '''saved''' by himself, but to the gulfs of a foreign country, which he had oppressed.}}
#* {{Q|la|Pliny the Elder|Naturalis Historia|7|29|103|quote=Super omnia Capitōlium summamque rem in eō sōlus ā Gallīs '''servāverat''', sī nōn regnō suō '''servasset'''.|trans=Above all [&zwnj;{{w|Lucius Siccius Dentatus}}] had singlehandedly '''saved''' the Capitol and the state treasure therein from the Gauls—had he only not '''saved''' it for his own reign.}}
# to [[preserve]], [[store]], [[keep]], [[reserve]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|207|quote=Dūrāte, et vōsmet rēbus '''servāte''' secundīs.|trans=Endure, and '''preserve''' yourselves for favourable things.}}
# {{lb|la|figurative}} to [[permit]], [[allow]]

