Page 5342 sto: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|stō}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-sto.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}
From {{der|la|itc-pro|*staēō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*sth₂éh₁yeti}}, stative verb from {{m|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sa|तिष्ठति|tr=tíṣṭhati}} (root {{m|sa|स्था}}), {{cog|fa|ایستا|tr=istâ||standing; stopping}}, {{cog|non|standa}}, {{cog|grc|ἵστημι}}, {{m|grc|στάσις}}, {{cog|bg|стоя}}, {{cog|ang|standan}} (whence {{cog|en|stand}}).

By its appearance through Latin sound laws, this stative verb, against all others of this class in the 2nd conjugation, belongs to the 1st conjugation. The perfect and supine stems are shared with {{m|la|sistō}}, the corresponding [[athematic]] verb from the same Indo-European root.

====Verb====
{{la-verb|1.pass-impers|stō|stet|stat}}

# to [[stand]]
#: {{syn|la|astō}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|2|56|quote=“Troiaque, nunc '''stārēs''', Priamīque arx alta, manērēs.”|trans=''“And Troy, '''you would be standing''' now, and high citadel of Priam, you would remain!” – Aeneas''}}
# to [[stay]], [[remain]]
#: {{syn|la|cōnstō|sistō|cōnsistō|remaneō|maneō|haereō}}
# to [[cost]], to be [[set]] at, [[stand]] at (e.g., a price)
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|4|885-886|quote=‘'''stat''' mihi nōn parvō virtūs mea: volnera testor<br/>armaque, quae sparsī sanguine saepe meō.’|trans=''“My bravery '''costs''' me no small [price]: I call to witness my scars<br/>and weapons, which I have often splattered with my own blood.”''<br/>({{w|Mezentius}} replies to a request to fight for {{w|Turnus}}.)}}
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin}} to [[be]]
#: {{syn|la|adsum|subsum|astō|exstō}}
#: {{ant|la|desum}}
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin}} to [[be]] [located at]
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin}} to [[live]]

=====Conjugation=====
Passive forms exist only in the third-person singular.
{{la-conj|1.pass-impers|stō|stet|stat}}
* {{m|la|stetērai|Stetērai}} is an early form of {{m|la||stetēre}} (= {{m|la|stetērunt}}).

