Page 5351 studeo: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|la|studio}}

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*(s)tewd-}}
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*(s)tewd-}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*(s)tew-||to push, hit}}.  Related to English {{m|en|stub}}; compare also {{cog|sco|stap||to strike, to forcibly insert}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|studeō}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-studeo.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2.nopass|studeō|studu}}

# {{lb|la|with dative}} to [[dedicate]] oneself (to), [[direct]] one's [[effort]]s or [[attention]] (to), [[strive]] after, [[be]] [[devoted]] to
#: {{syn|la|lūctor|dēdō|certō|ēlabōrō|cōnītor|cōnor|ēnītor|appetō|affectō|tendō|temptō|quaerō|contendō|adnītor|īnsequor|labōrō|pugnō|molior|perīclitor|nītor|spectō|serviō}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|agriculturae}} '''studeo'''|t=I '''dedicate myself''' to agriculture}}
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|sacrificiis}} '''studeo'''|t=I '''dedicate myself''' to sacrifices}}
#* {{Q|la|Catullus|Carmina|92|quote=Nīl nimium '''studeō''', Caesar, tibi velle placēre,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nec scīre utrum sīs albus an āter homō.|trans=I do not '''strive''' too much, [[w:Julius Caesar|Caesar]], to want to please you,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nor to know whether you are a white or black human.}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Caesar|Caesar]]|[[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]]|VII, 28|quote=Nec fuit quisquam, qui praedae '''studeret'''|t=There was not anyone who was '''striving for''' the plunder}}
# to [[aspire]] to, [[tend]] to, [[desire]], [[look for]]
#: {{syn|la|requīrō|affectō|cupiō|quaerō|aveō|concupiō|petō|expetō|indigeō|circumspiciō|sitiō|spectō|voveō|appetō|intendō}}
#: {{ant|la|āversor|abhorreō}}
# {{lb|la|with dative}} to be [[attached]] or [[favorable]] to, [[favor]], [[support]], [[side with]]
#: {{uxi|la|{{l|la|alicui}} '''studere'''|t=to '''side with''' someone}}
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Caesar|Caesar]]|[[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]]|VII, 20|quote=Cui rei propter animi mollitiem '''studere''' omnes videret|t=to which he perceived that all were '''inclined''' owing to their want of energy}}
# {{lb|la|especially|Late Latin|Medieval Latin}} to [[study]], to apply oneself to learning
#* {{Q|la|Pliny the Younger|Epistulae|quote='''Studēs''' an piscāris?|trans='''Are you studying''' or fishing?}}
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin}} to [[care]], to [[think]]
#: {{syn|la|cōnsulō|cūrō|cōnsultō|colō|respiciō}}
#* {{quote-text|la|year=1678|author=w:du Cange|title=Glossarium mediæ et infimæ latinitatis|volume=3|column=977|brackets=on|passage=S<small>TUDERE</small>, ''Studiare :'' [[curo|Curare]], Gall. ''[[penser#French|Penser]]''. Gregorius Turon. lib. 6. Hist. c. 32.|t=Glossary of middle and low Latin}}

====Usage notes====
When used with a dative, ''studeō'' means to have a taste or inclination for a person or thing, to keep close to it.

The verb ''studeō'' used with an accusative means to search earnestly for a thing, to desire and covet it.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|2.nopass|studeō|studu}}

