Page 5359 suadeo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*sweh₂d-}}
From a {{der|la|ine-pro|-}} causative verb in {{m|ine-pro|*-éyeti}} from the root {{m|ine-pro|*sweh₂d-|t=sweet}},<ref>{{R:ine:IEW|head=su̯ād-|page=1039f.}}</ref><ref name=LIV>{{R:ine:LIV|head=*su̯eh₂d-|page=606f.}}</ref><ref name=deVaan>{{R:itc:EDL|suāvis|page=594}}</ref> hence {{lit|nocap=1|to make sweet, pleasing}}. The stem of the original verb may have been either the zero-grade {{m|ine-pro||*suh₂d-éyeti}},<ref name=LIV/> as in {{cog|sa|सूदयति|tr=sūdáyati|t=to make tasty}}, or the ''o''-grade {{m|ine-pro||*swoh₂d-éyeti}},<ref name=deVaan/> but in the end it was secondarily replaced with the full grade by influence of the related adjective {{m|la|suāvis|t=sweet}}.<ref name=LIV/><ref name=deVaan/>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|svādeō|eccl=0}}
* {{la-IPA|suādeō}}
* Note: while the word generally scans with a glide in classical poetry, the rare pronunciation of the {{m|la||u}} as a vowel (in hiatus) is attested as early as [[w:Lucretius|Lucretius]] (see quotations). Compare the similar phenomenon in {{m|la|suāvis}}, which Romance languages reflect with a hiatus.

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2|suādeō|suās|suās}}

# to [[recommend]], [[advise]]
#: {{syn|la|commendō|admoneō|moneō|dēlīberō|conciliō|cēnseō|praedicō|suggerō}}
#* {{Q|la|Lucretius|De Rerum Natura|4|1157-8|quote=Atque aliōs aliī irrīdent Veneremque '''sŭādent'''<br>ut plācent, quoniam foedō adflictentur amōre.|t=They [lovers] make fun of each other, '''advising''' to placate Venus as they're afflicted with foul love.}}
# to [[urge]], [[exhort]], [[induce]], [[impel]], [[suade]], [[persuade]]
#: {{syn|la|persuādeō|perdūcō|convincō|exhortor|indūcō|moveō}}
#: {{ant|la|dissuādeō|tardō|obiūrgō}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Georgicon|4|264|quote=hic iam galbaneōs '''suādēbō''' incendere odōrēs|trans=Then I would '''urge''' you to burn fragrant resin of galbanum}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|5|703-704|quote=hīs amor, ut repetant, illīs, ut reddere nōlint,<br/>'''suādet'''; et ex causā pugnat uterque parī.|trans=''Love '''persuades''' – these [men], to reclaim [their betrothed women], and those [men], [who] do not wish to return [the women]; and so from the same motive each one of them fights.''<br/>({{w|Phoebe (daughter of Leucippus)}} and {{w|Hilaeira}}, already betrothed to {{w|Lynceus of Messene}} and {{w|Idas of Messene}}, had been abducted by {{w|Castor and Pollux}}.)}}
# to [[advocate]], [[promote]], [[support]]

====Conjugation====
* This verb has only limited passive conjugation; only third-person passive forms are attested in surviving sources.
{{la-conj|2|suādeō|suās|suās}}

