{{la-verb|3|surgō|surrēx|surrēct}}

# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to [[rise]], to [[arise]], to [[rise]] from bed, to [[get up]], to [[stand up]]
#: {{syn|la|ēmergō|assurgō|orior|coorior|oborior}}
#* '''4th c.''', Jerome, ''Canticum Canticōrum'' 2:10
#*: {{quote|la|'''''Surge''', properā, amīca mea, formōsa mea, et venī.''
|translation='''Arise''', hurry up, my beloved, my beautiful, and come.}}
#* ‘’Exsurge, Domine, et iudica causam Tuam; Memor esto improperiorum tuorum, Eorum quae ab insipiente sunt tota Die’’
# {{lb|la|archaic|transitive}} to [[lift up]], to [[straighten]]
#: {{syn|la|levō|allevō|ēlevō|ērigō|excellō|scandō|ēvehō|efferō|sublīmō|tollō|ēdō}}
#: {{ant|la|abiciō|dēiciō}}
# (of things) to [[rise]] in [[growth]], to [[spring]] [[up]], [[grow up]]; to [[rise]] in [[building]], be [[built]]
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|4|830|quote=‘auspicibus vōbīs hoc mihi '''surgat''' opus.’|trans=''“May this work of mine '''rise''' under your auspices.”''<br/>({{w|Romulus}} has marked out the walls of Rome and prays for divine favor.)}}

