Page 5625 taceo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*takēō}}, possibly from {{der|la|ine-pro|*tak-}} or {{m|ine-pro|*tHk-}}. Akin to {{cog|got|𐌸𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽}}, {{cog|non|þegja}} ({{cog|da|tie}} and {{cog|is|þegja}}), {{cog|goh|dagen}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|taceō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2+|taceō}}

# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to be [[silent]], say nothing, [[shut up]], [[hold one's tongue]]
#: {{syn|la|sileō|conticēscō}}
#: {{ux|la|Aut '''tacē''' aut face.|t=''Either '''shut up''' or do [it].''}}
#* {{Q|la|Plautus|Curculio|5|3|5|quote=''Nōn '''tacēs'''?'' - ''Nōn '''taceō'''!''<ref>[http://www.thelatinlibrary.com/plautus/curculio.shtml Latin]</ref>|trans='''Hold your tongue''', will you? - I will not!<ref>[http://artemis.austincollege.edu/acad/cml/rcape/comedy/5-3.html English]</ref>}}
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to be [[still]] or at rest
#: {{syn|la|conquiēscō|conticēscō|sileō|cessō}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to [[leave]] [[unsaid]], [[keep quiet]], [[pass over]] or [[omit]] in silence, make no [[mention]] of
#: {{syn|la|sileō|conticēscō}}
#* {{Q|la|Julius Caesar|Bello Gallico|1|17|quote=Tum demum Liscus oratione Caesaris adductus quod antea '''tacuerat''' proponit.|trans=Then at length Liscus, moved by Caesar’s speech, discloses what he '''had''' hitherto '''kept secret'''.}}
#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Ibis|9|thru=10|quote=Quisquis is est—nam nōmen adhūc utcumque '''tacēbō'''—<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;cōgit inassuētās sūmere tēla manūs.|trans=Whoever he is, for the name I am still going to '''omit''',<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;he forces my unaccustomed hands to take weapons.}}

