Page 5696 traho: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|la|tragō}} {{qualifier|Early Medieval Latin}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*traɣō}}, seemingly from {{der|la|ine-pro|*tregʰ-||to drag, pull?}}, a variation of {{m|ine-pro|*dʰregʰ-||to pull, draw, drag}}.

The closest IE cognates are {{cog|sga|tethraig|t=ran away, receded}}, {{cog|wlm|treul|t=trouble, weakness}} < {{cog|cel-pro|*trāglo-}}. Possibly connected with {{cog|cel-pro|*tregess|t=foot}}, {{cog|got|𐌸𐍂𐌰𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽|t=to run}}, {{cog|sla-pro|*tragъ}}, but semantically problematic. De Vaan deems a connection with {{cog|gem-pro|*draganą|t=to drag, draw}} as formally impossible, but this could be another reflex of a substrate loanword.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|trahō|627}}</ref>

Weiss prefers a connection with {{cog|gem-pro|*draganą|t=to drag, draw}}, under the assumptions that Grassmann's law operated in Latin before liquids so that the resulting initial deaspirated cluster ''*dr-'' would then regularly appear as ''tr-'' in Latin as it does word-medially.<ref>{{cite-book|chapter=Limited Latin Grassmann's Law: Do We Need It?|title=Vina Diem Celebrent: Studies in Linguistics and Philology in Honor of Brent Vine|location=Ann Arbor|publisher=Beech Stave Press|year=2018|last=Weiss|first=Michael|editors=Dieter Gunkel, Stephanie W. Jamison, Angelo O. Mercado and Kazuhiko Yoshida|pages=438-447}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|trahō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|trahō|trāx|tract}}

# to [[drag]], [[pull]]
#: {{syn|la|efferō|portō|ferō|gerō|vehō|addūcō}}
# to [[trail]]
# to [[extract]], [[withdraw]]
# to [[plunder]], [[squander]]
#: {{syn|la|dēpraedor|praedor|dīripiō|populor|expugnō|agō}}
# to [[draw out]], [[extend]], [[lengthen]], [[prolong]]
#: {{syn|la|extendō|distendō|pandō|prōlongō|porrigō|prōferō|explicō|prōtrahō}}
# {{lb|la|of time}} to [[protract]], [[drag out]], [[linger]]
#: {{syn|la|retardō|moror|cūnctor|tardō|prōtrahō|dubitō|extrahō|differō}}
#: {{ant|la|ruō|accurrō|currō|festīnō|prōvolō|properō|corripiō|affluō|mātūrō}}
# to [[weigh]], [[ponder]], [[consider]]
#: {{syn|la|cōnsīderō|dubitō|ponderō|pendō|perpendō|dēlīberō|circumspiciō|cōnsultō|putō|reputō|videō}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[attract]], [[draw]] (someone; their attention)
# {{lb|la|by extension}} to attract the [[support]] of, [[sway]], [[win over]]
#: {{syn|la|persuādeō|convincō|perpellō|flectō|admoneō}}
#* {{Q|la|Livy|Ab Urbe Condita|26|1|quote=Ea tum cura maxime intentos habebat Romanos, non ab ira tantum, quae in nullam unquam ciuitatem iustior fuit, quam quod urbs tam nobilis ac potens, sicut defectione sua '''traxerat''' aliquot populos, ita recepta inclinatura rursus animos uidebatur ad ueteris imperii respectum.|year=27-25 BCE|trans=This concern in particular troubled the mindful Romans at the time, not so much because of anger, which has never been more justified against any other city, rather because a city so noble and powerful, in the same way that it had '''attracted the support of''' a number of communities by its revolt, was thought would again turn attention back towards respect for the previous government once recaptured.}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|trahō|trāx|tract}}

