#* {{Q|la|Ovid|Fasti|2|858|quote=Mārsque citōs iūnctīs curribus '''urget''' equōs|trans=''and Mars, with chariots harnessed, '''drives''' swift horses''<br/>(Translations of Ovid's ''Fasti'', by H.T. Riley, James G. Frazer, and Anne and Peter Wiseman, all give Mars one harnessed or yoked chariot in the singular; however, ‘‘iunctis curribus’’ is plural. The plural seems appropriate if the poet’s meaning is understood to be that of Mars menacing with an army of charioteers. Ovid’s verse is an imaginative [[segue]] as he closes his book on February and introduces the month of March, named in honor of the war god.)}}
