Page 5826 utor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{der|la|itc-pro|*oitōr}} (with {{m|la|ūsus}} for {{m|la||*issus}} after {{m|la|ūtor}}), possibly from {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₃eyt-||to take along, fetch}}. Compare the future tense {{m|grc|οἴσω}} of {{noncog|grc|φέρω||carry}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|ūtor}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|ūtor|ūs}}

# to [[use]], [[make]] use of, [[employ]]
#: {{syn|la|sūmō|adhibeō|occupō|ūsūrpō}}
#* '''late 1st c. BC''', Livy, ''Ab Urbe Condita'' 22.51 (reportedly said by Hasdrubal, Hannibal's brother, when Hannibal failed to invade Rome after his victory at Cannae)
#*: {{quote|la|Vincere scīs, Hannibal, victōriā '''ūtī ''' nescīs.
|translation=''You know how to obtain victory, Hannibal, but you do not know how '''to use''' it.''}}
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|64|quote=Ad quem tum Iūnō supplex hīs vōcibus '''ūsa est''':|trans=''To this one then Juno, suppliant, '''used''' these words:''<br/>(Note the correlation of the indicative perfect tense verb “usa est” with the ablative plural “his vocibus”: Juno used, or that is to say, spoke “with these words”.)}}
# to [[enjoy]], [[take advantage of]]
#: {{syn|la|vēscor|adhibeō|sūmō|assūmō|ūsūrpō}}
# to [[experience]], [[undergo]], [[encounter]]
#: {{syn|la|experior}}
# to [[wear]], [[consume]]
#: {{syn|la|conterō|terō|dēterō|atterō|tenuō|abūtor|exhauriō|ēnecō|adedō|peragō|accīdō|effundō}}

====Usage notes====
# The verb {{m-self|la|ūtor}} and others like it, {{m|la|fruor}}, {{m|la|fungor}}, {{m|la|potior}}, {{m|la|vescor}}, and their compounds, regularly [[wikipedia:Case government|govern]] the ablative case
#:{{ux|la|Mīles gladiō '''ūtitur'''.|The soldier '''uses''' a sword.}}
#:{{ux|la|Quo expeditiore re frumentaria '''uteretur'''|in order that he might '''make use of''' the looser supplies of provisions ([[w:Caesar|Caesar]], [[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]], VII, 11)}}
# Later regularized as {{m|la|ūsō}}

====Conjugation====
