Page 5849 vaticinor: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{m|la|vātēs|t=seer, soothsayer, prophet}} and {{m|la|canō|t=to sing; to recite; to foretell, predict, prophesy}}. The change from {{m|la||-a-}} to {{m|la||-i-}} follows the common pattern of Latin vowel reduction in non-initial syllables. As {{m|la||canō}} is a third-conjugation verb, the derivation of first-conjugation {{m|la||vāticinor}} may involve more steps than simple compounding of the base noun and verb; many first-conjugation verbs are denominative (derived from nouns or adjectives), and it has been hypothesized that an intermediate step in the derivation of {{m|la||vāticinor}} was a compound noun {{m|la||*vāti-cinium}}.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=canō|page=88}}</ref>

It is also hypothesized that this verb was the original basis from which the ending {{af|la|-cinor}} was extended by analogy to be used as a suffix to form other verbs, such as {{m|la|ratiōcinor}} and {{m|la|sermōcinor}}.<ref>ibid.</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|vāticinor}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|1+|vāticinor}}

# to [[prophesy]], [[foretell]]
#: {{syn|la|praesāgiō|praemoneō|portendō|moneō|praedīcō|canō}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[sing]], [[celebrate]] (as a poet)
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[rave]], [[rant]], [[spout]] [[foolishness]]

